(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["cuda"] = {"s":"cuda","t":[["why-gpus","Why GPUs: latency, throughput and power","1.1"],["speedup-amdahl","Speedup, the PCIe trip and Amdahl's law","1.2"],["first-workloads","First GPU workloads: heat simulation and convolution","1.3"],["first-cuda-program","Your first CUDA program","2.1"],["thread-indexing","Threads, blocks and grids: indexing in 1-D, 2-D and 3-D","2.2"],["warps-sms","SMs, warps and hiding latency","2.3"],["occupancy","Occupancy: how many warps fit on an SM","2.4"],["memory-hierarchy","The GPU memory hierarchy","3.1"],["coalescing","Coalescing: reading global memory well","3.2"],["shared-memory","Shared memory and bank conflicts","3.3"],["floating-point","Floating point numbers on the GPU","4.1"],["roofline","FLOPs, bandwidth and the roofline","4.2"],["reduction","Parallel reduction","5.1"],["tiled-matmul","Tiled matrix multiplication","5.2"],["stencils","Stencils, halos and heat simulation","5.3"],["convolution","Convolution and Sobel edge detection","5.4"],["scan-histogram","Scan, compaction and histograms","5.5"],["divergence-scheduling","Warp divergence and warp scheduling","6.1"],["streams","Streams, asynchrony and overlap","6.2"],["races-locks","Race conditions and locks","7.1"],["barriers-atomics","Barriers, atomics and GPU locks","7.2"],["number-formats","Low precision numbers and quantisation","8.1"],["tensor-cores","Tensor cores, GEMM and WMMA","8.2"],["fast-matmul","How fast matmul kernels are built","8.3"],["debug-profile","Debugging and profiling CUDA code","9.1"],["gpu-python","GPU programming from Python","9.2"],["quiz1-solved","Quiz 1 solved step by step","10.1"],["challenge-problems","Challenge problems","10.2"],["exam-revision","Exam revision","10.3"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","Why GPUs: latency, throughput and power","CPU against GPU design, latency and throughput, FPS and batch time, clock and power, peak FLOPs, and the hardware spectrum. · GPU · CPU · latency · throughput · FPS · peak FLOPs · power · TPU · NPU · FPGA · ASIC Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 1: Why GPUs›Why GPUs Step 1 · Topic 1 of 3 · School See why a GPU spends its silicon on thousands of small arithmetic units, measure speed as latency and throughput, and work out peak FLOPs and power from first principles. About 40 min5 lessons2 animations8 formulas12 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Two ways to build a processor 2Latency, throughput and frames per second 3Clock speed, power and peak work 4CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA and ASIC 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[0,"designs","Two ways to build a processor","A computer chip can spend its area on two kinds of things. It can spend it on clever control and big caches, so that each instruction finishes as soon as possible. Or it can spend it on many small arithmetic units, so that a huge number of simple instructions finish together. The first choice gives a CPU. The second gives a GPU. Part of the chip CPU GPU Control logic (decides what runs next) Large and clever: branch prediction, out of order execution Small and simple, shared by a group of threads Cache (fast memory on the chip) Large per core Small per core, plus a shared L2 ALUs (the units that add and multiply) A few, powerful Thousands, small and energy efficient Clock High, about 2 to 5 GHz Moderate, about 0.8 to 2 GHz Built for Low latency: one task, fast High throughput: many tasks per second Real world scenario: the school bus and the race car A race car takes one child to school in 5 minutes. A bus takes 20 minutes but carries 50 children. For one child the car wins. For a whole school the bus wins by a mile. A CPU is the race car. A GPU is a fleet of buses. Neither is \"faster\" in general. It depends on whether you move one child or a thousand. Real world scenario: the gardener (from the lecture) One 25 year old gardener picks up any single leaf faster than a 10 year old. But leaves are scattered everywhere, and 25 children can each grab a different leaf at the same moment. The children clear the garden first. Each child is slower, the team is faster. That is a GPU. CPU against GPU on a pile of jobsPress Play. The CPU finishes one job at a time. The GPU finishes a whole batch per round. Step 20 of 20 Jobs in the pile1000 CPU time per job (ms)10 GPU time per round (ms)15 Jobs the GPU does in one round1000 Latency of one job: CPU 10 ms, GPU 15 ms, so the CPU wins one job. Whole pile: CPU 1,000 × 10 = 10,000 ms. GPU ⌈1,000 ÷ 1,000⌉ = 1 round × 15 = 15 ms. Winner: GPU. Throughput: CPU 100 jobs per second, GPU 66,666.667 jobs per second (666.667 times the CPU). Try this The lecture case: 1000 images, CPU 10 ms each, GPU 15 ms per round of 1000. The CPU wins one image, the GPU wins the pile by about 667 times. Only one job. Now the CPU is faster, because latency is all that counts. Workbook P1.1.4: CPU 5 ms, GPU 40 ms for up to 256. Try 8 jobs (a tie) and 9 jobs (GPU wins). Workbook P1.1.3: 1024 jobs, rounds of 256 take 40 ms. Watch four GPU rounds against 1024 CPU jobs. Common mistake GPU does not mean \"always faster\". A GPU wins only when there is enough independent work to keep thousands of small units busy. For a single small task the CPU usually wins. Teachers in the class asked if a GPU is SIMD and a CPU is SISD. Broadly yes. SISD means a single instruction works on a single piece of data. SIMD means one instruction works on many pieces of data at once. NVIDIA calls its version SIMT, single instruction multiple threads: 32 threads (a warp) run the same instruction together, but each thread has its own registers and can take its own branch. Chapter 2.3 shows this in detail. Lesson 2 of 5"],[0,"latency","Latency, throughput and frames per second","Latency and throughputExam favourite Latency = time to finish ONE job Throughput = jobs finished ÷ time taken A CPU is built for low latency. A GPU is built for high throughput. Frames per second FPS = 1000 ÷ (ms per frame) ms per frame = 1000 ÷ FPS There are 1000 milliseconds in one second. Time for a batchExam favourite T = ⌈N ÷ C⌉ × (time per round) N jobs, C jobs done together in one round. ⌈ ⌉ means round up, because a part full round still costs a whole round. Pipeline latency = sum of stage times throughput = 1 ÷ (slowest stage) Once the line is full, one result comes out every time the slowest stage finishes. Worked example from the workbook: who clears 1000 images first? CPU: 10 ms per image, one at a time. GPU: 15 ms per round, 1000 images per round. One image: CPU 10 ms, GPU 15 ms. The CPU has lower latency. Pile of 1000: CPU needs 1000 × 10 = 10,000 ms = 10 s. GPU needs ⌈1000 ÷ 1000⌉ = 1 round = 15 ms. Throughput: CPU 1000 ÷ 10 = 100 images per second. GPU 1000 ÷ 0.015 ≈ 66,667 images per second. The GPU has about 667 times the throughput, even though each image takes longer Real world scenario: a toll plaza on a highway One toll booth with a very fast operator still makes a long queue at rush hour. Twenty booths with slower operators clear the queue far sooner. Each car still waits about as long at its own booth, so latency per car does not drop, but cars per minute go up twenty times. The inference study in the lecture. Ten small models were timed on an Intel Xeon CPU, an NVIDIA T4 GPU and a custom circuit on an FPGA board. Model M1 (three convolution layers) ran at 13.39 FPS on the CPU, 15.07 on the GPU and 1464.05 on custom hardware. The GPU gain was only about 1.13 times because M1 is tiny and the T4 had too little parallel work. Custom hardware has no instruction fetch or decode at all, which is why it won by about 97 times. The tailor analogy from class: ready made clothes (CPU, GPU) fit everyone, a tailored suit (custom hardware) fits one person perfectly. Lesson 3 of 5"],[0,"clock","Clock speed, power and peak work","Cycle time tcycle = 1 ÷ f 1 GHz = 10⁹ ticks per second, so one tick lasts 1 ns. At 800 MHz a tick lasts 1.25 ns. Dynamic powerExam favourite Pdyn ∝ α · C · V² · f α is how often the circuit switches, C its capacitance, V the voltage (squared), f the clock. A faster clock needs a higher voltage, so power grows much faster than speed. Peak work per secondExam favourite peak = (FLOPs per tick per core) × f × cores One FMA, a × b + c, counts as 2 FLOPs. Why GPUs add cores instead of chasing clock speed (workbook P1.2.3 and P1.2.4) Running 20 percent faster needs about 10 percent more voltage. Power factor = 1.1² × 1.2 = 1.21 × 1.2 = 1.452. Power rises 45 percent for only 20 percent more speed. Now go the other way: halve both voltage and clock. One slow core uses (½)² × ½ = ⅛ of the power. So 8 slow cores fit in the power budget of 1 fast core. 8 cores × ½ speed = 4 times the work for the same power, if the work can be split Going deeper: the power wall Until about 2005 every new chip shrank its transistors and lowered the voltage at the same time (Dennard scaling), so clocks rose for free. Below about 1 volt the transistors leak current, the voltage could not keep falling, and power per chip hit the limit a fan can cool (about 100 to 300 W). Clock speeds stopped near 3 to 5 GHz. Since then extra transistors have been spent on more cores. GPUs push this to the extreme. Remember Peak FLOP/s is a ceiling, not a promise. Real kernels usually reach a fraction of it, because they wait for memory. Chapter 4.2 (the roofline) shows how to predict that fraction. Lesson 4 of 5"],[0,"platforms","CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA and ASIC","Platform Strength Weakness Examples from the lecture CPU Most flexible, cheapest, runs any program Limited parallelism Arm Cortex-M, Raspberry Pi GPU Massive parallelism, good programmability Power hungry NVIDIA Jetson, T4, A100, Arm Mali FPGA Custom circuits, low latency, good work per watt, can be reprogrammed Small on chip memory, needs hardware design skill Xilinx Zynq, Intel Arria 10 ASIC (TPU, NPU) Lowest power, highest performance for one task Very costly to design, long time to market, fixed Google Edge TPU, Ascend 310 Moving from CPU to GPU to FPGA to ASIC trades flexibility for efficiency. TPU is Google's name for its own neural accelerator, and other makers call theirs an NPU. A smartwatch that checks your heartbeat the same way all day is a perfect job for a tiny NPU. A GPU is kept because its work keeps changing: a heat simulation today, image processing tomorrow, a language model next week. Host and device Host = CPU (starts the program, owns the operating system) Device = GPU (runs the parallel parts you send it) Every GPU system is driven by a CPU. CUDA is the library and language extension that lets the host hand work to the device. In the exam A GPU never runs alone. The CPU boots the machine, runs the serial parts and launches GPU work. This is why the time to copy data between them matters so much (chapter 1.2). Lesson 5 of 5"],[0,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A CPU is one very fast worker. A GPU is thousands of slower workers. If the job can be split into many small pieces that do not depend on each other, the many workers finish first. Latency is time per job, throughput is jobs per second. Peak = FLOPs per tick × clock × cores. Power ∝ V²f, so GPUs use many cores at a moderate clock. Use ⌈N ÷ C⌉ rounds for batch time. GPUs hide the long latency of each operation by keeping thousands of threads in flight and switching between them every cycle at no cost. The design only pays off when the program exposes enough parallelism, which Little's law turns into a number in chapter 2.3. Modern accelerators mix the ideas: GPUs gained tensor cores (fixed function matrix units), and research chips explore near memory and in memory computing, systolic arrays (the TPU) and dataflow designs. Picking hardware is now a question of matching arithmetic intensity, precision and memory bandwidth to the workload. Work it out: the T4 peak (workbook P1.2.5) Cores = 40 SMs × 64 = 2560. FLOPs per tick = 2560 × 2 = 5120 (one FMA each). Per second = 5120 × 1.59 × 10⁹ = 8.1408 × 10¹². Peak ≈ 8.14 TFLOP/s, and the T4 data sheet says 8.1 Going deeper: why the GPU took over deep learning Training a neural network is mostly matrix multiplication, millions of identical multiply adds. That is exactly the kind of work a GPU was built for in graphics. In 2012 AlexNet was trained on two consumer GPUs, and since then GPU makers have added hardware for the exact number formats AI uses (chapter 8.1)."],[1,"","Speedup, the PCIe trip and Amdahl's law","Speedup with copies counted, Amdahl's law and its ceiling, working backwards, Gustafson's law, and break even sizes. · speedup · Amdahl's law · Gustafson's law · PCIe · efficiency · strong scaling · weak scaling Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 1: Why GPUs›Speedup and Amdahl Step 1 · Topic 2 of 3 · School Measure speedup honestly with the copies included, then use Amdahl's law to see how much a GPU can ever help, and Gustafson's law for problems that grow. About 45 min4 lessons2 animations9 formulas12 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Speedup and the trip over PCIe 2Amdahl's law 3Gustafson's law and scaling (beyond the lecture) 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[1,"speedup","Speedup and the trip over PCIe","SpeedupExam favourite S = old time ÷ new time S = 10 means the new version is ten times faster. With FPS, use new FPS ÷ old FPS. Total GPU timeExam favourite TGPU = Tcopy in + Tkernel + Tcopy out The GPU is worth it only if TGPU < TCPU. Copy time Tcopy = bytes ÷ bandwidth PCIe 3.0 x16 gives about 12 to 16 GB/s in practice. 12 GB/s = 12 × 10⁹ bytes per second. The GPU has its own memory. Data lives in the CPU's memory first, so it must travel over the PCIe bus to the GPU, and results must travel back. That trip can cost more than the computing. Real world scenario: a calculator friend across town Your friend adds numbers 100 times faster than you, but lives a 30 minute bus ride away. For one sum you do it yourself. For ten thousand sums you take the bus. The bus ride is the PCIe copy. The trip over PCIe: copy in, compute, copy outEach step adds one part of the GPU time. Compare the total with the CPU. Step 3 of 3 Data to copy in (MB)8 Data to copy out (MB)4 PCIe bandwidth (GB/s)12 Kernel time (ms)0.50 CPU time (ms)2.00 Time steps (kernel launches)1 Copy in and out every step Copy in 0.667 ms + kernel 0.5 ms + copy out 0.333 ms = 1.5 ms on the GPU against 2 ms on the CPU. Kernel only speedup 4. End to end speedup 1.333 . Copy time = bytes ÷ bandwidth. Try this Workbook P1.3.3 style: the kernel is 4 times faster than the CPU, yet the copies make the GPU slower overall. Workbook P1.3.5: a 4 MB grid for 100 steps, kernel 0.05 ms, CPU 9 ms per step. Copy once: about 158 times faster. Same, but copy every step. The speedup falls to about 12 times. Keep data on the GPU. Grid CPU (ms) GPU (ms) Shared GPU (ms) GPU speedup 256 × 256 51.55 0.48 0.53 107.76 512 × 512 236.54 1.48 1.87 160.28 1024 × 1024 906.35 5.34 6.13 169.79 2048 × 2048 3593.75 17.75 16.32 202.44 This is the battery heat simulation from lecture 1. The speedup grows with the grid, because a bigger grid gives the GPU more independent cells to hide its fixed costs. Recomputing from the printed times gives slightly different numbers, for example 906.35 ÷ 5.34 ≈ 169.7, because the slide used more decimals. Python # Is the GPU worth it once copies over PCIe are counted?def copy_ms(bytes_, gb_per_s):return bytes_ / (gb_per_s * 1e9) * 1000grid = 1024 * 1024 * 4# one float grid in bytessteps, gpu_step, cpu_step, pcie = 100, 0.05, 9.0, 12once = copy_ms(grid, pcie) + steps * gpu_step + copy_ms(grid, pcie)every = steps * (copy_ms(grid, pcie) + gpu_step + copy_ms(grid, pcie))cpu = steps * cpu_stepprint(f\"copy once : {once:7.2f} ms, speedup {cpu / once:6.1f}x\")print(f\"copy every: {every:7.2f} ms, speedup {cpu / every:6.1f}x\") Output copy once : 5.70 ms, speedup 157.9x copy every: 74.91 ms, speedup 12.0x Common mistake Keep data on the GPU across many kernel launches. Copy in once, run all time steps, copy out once. Copying every step can turn a 158 times speedup into 12 times (workbook P1.3.5). Lesson 2 of 4"],[1,"amdahl","Amdahl's law","Split a program into a part that can run in parallel (fraction P of the time on one processor) and a part that cannot (1 − P). With N processors the parallel part shrinks to P ÷ N, the serial part stays. Amdahl's lawExam favourite S(N) = 1 ÷ [ (1 − P) + P ÷ N ] P is the parallel fraction measured on one processor, N the number of processors. The ceilingExam favourite Smax = 1 ÷ (1 − P) Let N grow without limit. 95 percent parallel can never beat 20 times. Working backwards P = (1 − 1/S) ÷ (1 − 1/N) From a measured speedup S on N processors. Efficiency E = S ÷ N How much of each processor is really used. Speeding up one part s times S = 1 ÷ [ (1 − p) + p ÷ s ] The same law for any part p that gets s times faster, for example a kernel. Derivation in four lines Time on one processor: T₁ = (1 − P)T₁ + PT₁. On N processors only the parallel part is divided: TN = (1 − P)T₁ + PT₁ ÷ N. Speedup S = T₁ ÷ TN. The T₁ cancels. S(N) = 1 ÷ [(1 − P) + P ÷ N], and as N → ∞, S → 1 ÷ (1 − P) Lecture 5 quiz: 1 percent serial, 61 cores 1 − P = 0.01 and P ÷ N = 0.99 ÷ 61 ≈ 0.016230. Denominator 0.01 + 0.016230 = 0.026230. S = 1 ÷ 0.026230 ≈ 38.125. Efficiency 38.125 ÷ 61 = 62.5 percent. Ceiling 1 ÷ 0.01 = 100 Real world scenario: cooking for a wedding Fifty cooks can chop vegetables together, but the biryani must still simmer for 40 minutes in one pot. Adding cooks shortens the chopping, never the simmering. That 40 minutes is the serial part. Common mistake P is a fraction of the time on one processor, not a fraction of the lines of code. A tiny loop that runs for 90 percent of the time gives P = 0.9. Lesson 3 of 4"],[1,"gustafson","Gustafson's law and scaling (beyond the lecture)","Amdahl keeps the problem size fixed. In practice people buy a bigger GPU to solve a bigger problem in the same time: a finer heat grid, a larger batch, a longer video. Then the parallel work grows with N while the serial part stays about the same. Gustafson's law (scaled speedup)Exam favourite Sscaled = N − (1 − P)(N − 1) Here P is the parallel share of the time measured on the N processor run. The speedup grows almost in a straight line with N. Strong scaling (Amdahl) Weak scaling (Gustafson) What is fixed Total problem size Work per processor Question asked How much faster does the same job finish? How much bigger a job fits in the same time? Limit 1 ÷ (1 − P) Grows with N GPU example One 1024 × 1024 grid on more SMs Grid grows from 1024² to 4096² with a bigger GPU Example: P = 0.9 on 64 processors Amdahl: S = 1 ÷ (0.1 + 0.9 ÷ 64) = 1 ÷ 0.1140625 ≈ 8.77. Gustafson: S = 64 − 0.1 × 63 = 64 − 6.3. = 57.7. Both are right. They answer different questions In the exam In an exam, Amdahl is the default unless the question says the problem grows with the number of processors. Lesson 4 of 4"],[1,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Extra helpers only speed up the part of a job that can be shared. The part that must be done by one person sets the limit. S = 1 ÷ ((1 − P) + P ÷ N), ceiling 1 ÷ (1 − P). Count copy in plus kernel plus copy out. Use P = (1 − 1/S) ÷ (1 − 1/N) to measure how parallel a program is. Amdahl applies to every part of a pipeline: a 10 times faster kernel inside an 8.66 ms frame where the kernel is 1.65 ms gives only about 1.21 times overall (workbook P1.4.7). Profile first, then speed up the biggest part. Hill and Marty extended Amdahl to multicore chip design (few big cores against many small ones), and asymmetric chips like CPU plus GPU systems follow from it. Overlapping copies with compute (chapter 6.2) and unified memory with prefetching attack the serial copy term directly. Work it out: the frame that will not speed up (workbook P1.4.7) Frame 8.66 ms, kernel 1.65 ms, so p = 1.65 ÷ 8.66 ≈ 0.1905. Kernel gets s = 10 times faster: S = 1 ÷ (0.8095 + 0.01905). S ≈ 1.21. The copies are the real bottleneck Going deeper: break even size for a GPU If the CPU takes 2 ns per element and the GPU takes a fixed 50 µs plus 0.2 ns per element, the GPU wins when 2n > 50,000 + 0.2n, so n > 27,778 elements. At 10 million elements the speedup is 20 ms ÷ 2.05 ms ≈ 9.76 (workbook P1.3.6). Every GPU decision has a break even size like this."],[2,"","First GPU workloads: heat simulation and convolution","The heat simulation update rule and its stability, why it suits a GPU, and normal against depthwise separable convolution cost. · heat simulation · finite difference · stencil · stability · convolution · depthwise separable · MobileNet Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 1: Why GPUs›First workloads Step 1 · Topic 3 of 3 · School Turn the heat equation into an update rule that thousands of threads can run at once, and count the work of normal and depthwise separable convolutions. About 40 min3 lessons2 animations7 formulas11 worked problems11 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Workload 1: a battery heat simulation 2Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost 3From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 3"],[2,"heat","Workload 1: a battery heat simulation","Engineers who design cooling for electric scooter and car batteries need to know how heat spreads through a battery pack. Lecture 1 showed a student project that simulated this on a CPU and a GPU. The heat equation ∂T/∂t = α (∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y²) + Q T is temperature, α the thermal diffusivity (how fast heat spreads), Q the heat the battery makes itself. 2-D update rule (5 points)Exam favourite Tnew = T + α (up + down + left + right − 4T) + βQ New temperature = old temperature + heat from the four neighbours + heat made inside. 1-D version Tnew = T + α (left + right − 2T) + βQ The same idea along a rod. How much work cells = height × width updates = cells × time steps A 1024 × 1024 grid has 1,048,576 cells per step. The bracket is the finite difference version of the second derivative (the Laplacian). It says how much hotter or colder the four neighbours are than the cell itself. If all neighbours equal the cell, nothing changes. Heat spreading on a grid, one time step at a timeEach cell takes the average pull of its four neighbours. Click a cell to heat it, then step. Step 30 of 30 Grid size12 α (how fast heat spreads)0.20 βQ (heat added to the hot spot each step)0.0 Time steps to animate30 Tnew = T + α(up + down + left + right − 4T) + βQ. Every cell uses only old values, so all 144 cells can be updated at the same time by 144 threads. The code keeps two grids (old and new) so no thread reads a value another thread already changed. Try this α = 0.2: the hot spot spreads smoothly and cools, like a hot battery cell warming its neighbours. Add a heat source βQ = 10 at the centre: the battery keeps heating and a warm zone grows. α = 0.3 is above the safe limit 0.25 for 2 D. The centre weight 1 − 4α turns negative and the values start to flip sign (unstable). Worked example from the workbook A cell at 25 degrees has neighbours up 20, down 30, left 25, right 35. α = 0.1 and βQ = 0.5. Neighbour sum = 20 + 30 + 25 + 35 = 110. Four times the cell = 100. Difference 110 − 100 = 10, times α = 1. Tnew = 25 + 1 + 0.5 = 26.5 degrees Why this is perfect GPU work. Each cell needs only its own old value and four neighbours. No cell depends on another cell's new value in the same step. So one thread per cell can update the whole grid at once. heat_step.cupreview of chapter 2 // One time step of the 2-D heat simulation. One thread updates one cell.__global__void heatStep(constfloat* Told, float* Tnew, int n,float alpha, float betaQ) {int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (row < 1 || col < 1 || row >= n - 1 || col >= n - 1) return;int i = row * n + col;float lap = Told[i - n] + Told[i + n] + Told[i - 1] + Told[i + 1] - 4.0f * Told[i]; Tnew[i] = Told[i] + alpha * lap + betaQ;} What each marked line does and why Line 2__global__ void heatStep(const float* Told, float* Tnew, int n,Two separate grids: every thread reads old values and writes new ones, so no thread reads a value that another thread has already changed in this step. Line 4int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;Each thread works out which column it owns. Chapter 2.2 explains this formula. Line 6if (row < 1 || col < 1 || row >= n - 1 || col >= n - 1) return;Border cells are held fixed. Threads outside the grid or on the border do nothing. Line 8float lap = Told[i - n] + Told[i + n] + Told[i - 1] + Told[i + 1]The 2-D grid is stored row by row in 1-D memory, so the cell above is n places back and the one below n places ahead. Line 9- 4.0f * Told[i];The four neighbours minus four times the cell: the discrete Laplacian. Line 10Tnew[i] = Told[i] + alpha * lap + betaQ;The update rule from the lecture. Python # 1-D heat rod with fixed ends: T_new[i] = T[i] + a*(T[i-1] + T[i+1] - 2*T[i])T = [0, 0, 100, 0, 0]a = 0.25for step inrange(2): new = T[:] # a second buffer: read old, write newfor i inrange(1, len(T) - 1): # the ends stay fixed new[i] = T[i] + a * (T[i - 1] + T[i + 1] - 2 * T[i])T = newprint(\"after step\", step + 1, T)"],[2,"heat","Workload 1: a battery heat simulation","Output after step 1 [0, 25.0, 50.0, 25.0, 0] after step 2 [0, 25.0, 37.5, 25.0, 0] Common mistake A GPU heat code must keep two buffers. If threads wrote into the same grid they read from, some would read a neighbour's new value and some its old value, and the answer would depend on timing. Going deeper: stability: why α must be small Rewrite the 2-D rule as Tnew = (1 − 4α)T + α(sum of neighbours). Every weight must be zero or positive or the scheme amplifies errors. So 1 − 4α ≥ 0, α ≤ 0.25 in 2-D (and α ≤ 0.5 in 1-D, α ≤ 1/6 in 3-D). With α = 0.3 the centre weight is −0.2 and the simulation blows up. This is the explicit scheme stability limit, and in physical units α Δt ÷ h² must stay below the same bound. Lesson 2 of 3"],[2,"vision","Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost","Lecture 1's second example was an image recognition pipeline: a camera frame goes through convolution layers, pooling and dense layers to a label. Almost all of the time is spent in convolutions, which are millions of multiply adds that do not depend on each other. That again is GPU work. Normal convolutionExam favourite mults = H × W × Cin × Cout × K × K Every output pixel of every output channel looks at a K × K patch of every input channel. Depthwise separable depthwise = H W CinK² pointwise = H W CinCout First one K × K filter per input channel, then a 1 × 1 convolution that mixes channels. MobileNet uses this. Saving ratioExam favourite separable ÷ normal = (K² + Cout) ÷ (K² × Cout) For many output channels this tends to 1 ÷ K², so at most 9 times fewer for 3 × 3 filters. Worked example: K = 3, 64 to 128 channels, 32 × 32 output Normal: 32 × 32 × 64 × 128 × 9 = 75,497,472. Depthwise: 32 × 32 × 64 × 9 = 589,824. Pointwise: 32 × 32 × 64 × 128 = 8,388,608. Separable total = 8,978,432. Ratio = (9 + 128) ÷ (9 × 128) = 137 ÷ 1152 ≈ 0.119, about 8.4 times fewer Real world scenario: why MobileNet runs on your phone Your phone camera can recognise faces and food in real time because networks like MobileNet use depthwise separable convolutions. In the lecture study, MobileNetV2 had more layers than a plain CNN but ran faster, because each layer needs far fewer multiplications. Remember Fewer multiplications does not always mean proportionally faster on a GPU. Depthwise layers do little work per byte loaded, so they are often limited by memory, not maths (chapter 4.2). Lesson 3 of 3"],[2,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths To find tomorrow's temperature of a spot, look at its neighbours today. Every spot can do this at the same moment, so thousands of helpers can work together. Explicit finite difference: Tnew = T + α(neighbours − 4T) + βQ, stable for α ≤ 0.25 in 2-D. Convolution cost H W Cin Cout K², separable saving (K² + Cout) ÷ (K² Cout). Both workloads are stencils: each output reads a small fixed neighbourhood. Neighbouring threads read overlapping inputs, so loading a tile with a halo into shared memory cuts global reads (chapters 5.3 and 5.4). That is why the \"Shared GPU\" column of the battery table exists. Production codes use implicit or multigrid solvers for stiff heat problems, temporal blocking to reuse data across time steps, and for CNNs the convolution is turned into a matrix multiply (im2col) or computed with Winograd or FFT methods on tensor cores. Work it out: a rod with fixed ends (workbook P1.5.4) Start [0, 0, 100, 0, 0], α = 0.25. Step 1: middle 100 + 0.25(0 + 0 − 200) = 50. Its neighbours 0 + 0.25(0 + 100 − 0) = 25. Rod [0, 25, 50, 25, 0]. Step 2: middle 50 + 0.25(25 + 25 − 100) = 37.5. Neighbours 25 + 0.25(0 + 50 − 50) = 25. [0, 25, 37.5, 25, 0]"],[3,"","Your first CUDA program","Host and device, the CUDA recipe, __global__ void kernels, vector add line by line, nvcc, asynchronous launches, error checks and unified memory. · CUDA · kernel · __global__ · cudaMalloc · cudaMemcpy · vector add · nvcc · unified memory Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 2: The CUDA programming model›First CUDA program Step 2 · Topic 1 of 4 · College Write, compile and run a complete CUDA program: allocate device memory, copy data, launch a kernel, copy back and check every call for errors. About 50 min4 lessons1 animation10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Host, device and the recipe every program follows 2The complete vector addition program 3Hello world, asynchronous launches and unified memory 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[3,"recipe","Host, device and the recipe every program follows","A CUDA program is an ordinary C++ program that runs on the CPU (the host) and now and then hands a function, called a kernel, to the GPU (the device). Host and device have separate memories, so data must be copied across. Allocate on hostmalloc or new Allocate on devicecudaMalloc Copy incudaMemcpy host to device Launchkernel<<<blocks, threads>>> Copy outcudaMemcpy device to host FreecudaFree and free The six steps of every CUDA programPress Step to watch data move between host (CPU) memory and device (GPU) memory. Step 6 of 6 Elements N8 Threads per block4 Forget the copy back (a common bug) Done. Host array c holds a[i] + b[i]. Every CUDA program follows these six steps, or uses unified memory to hide steps 2 and 5. Try this Vector add of 8 numbers with blocks of 4: two blocks, every thread adds one pair. N = 10 with blocks of 4: three blocks are launched and two threads must do nothing (the if (i < n) guard). Forget cudaMemcpy back: the host array c stays zero even though the GPU computed the answer. Qualifier Runs on Called from Notes __global__ device host (and device with dynamic parallelism) A kernel. Must return void. Launched with <<< >>> __device__ device device A helper function used inside kernels. May return any type __host__ host host The default for normal functions __host__ __device__ both both Compiled twice, handy for small maths helpers In the exam Quiz 1 question 6: the correct kernel signature is __global__ void. A kernel cannot return a value, because thousands of threads run it and the launch returns to the host at once without waiting. Results go back through memory. __shared__ is a memory space, not a function qualifier. Python # The CUDA recipe, simulated in plain Python: host lists, \"device\" lists, and a kernel run by every threadn, threads = 10, 4a = [i for i inrange(n)]b = [2 * i for i inrange(n)]d_a, d_b = a[:], b[:] # cudaMemcpy host to deviced_c = [0] * n # cudaMallocblocks = (n + threads - 1) // threads # round up: 3 blocksfor blockIdx inrange(blocks): # on a GPU all of these run at the same timefor threadIdx inrange(threads): i = blockIdx * threads + threadIdxif i < n: # the guard: threads 10 and 11 do nothing d_c[i] = d_a[i] + d_b[i]c = d_c[:] # cudaMemcpy device to hostprint(\"blocks\", blocks, \"threads\", blocks * threads)print(c) Output blocks 3 threads 12 [0, 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18, 21, 24, 27] Real world scenario: posting parcels to a factory You (the host) pack raw material into boxes, ship them to a factory (cudaMemcpy), send a work order (the launch), and wait for the finished goods to be shipped back. The factory has thousands of workers, but nothing happens until the material arrives, and you cannot use the goods until they come back. Lesson 2 of 4"],[3,"vadd","The complete vector addition program","vector_add.cu #include <cstdio>#include <cstdlib>#include <cuda_runtime.h>#define CHECK(call) do { cudaError_t e = (call); if (e != cudaSuccess) { \\ printf(\"CUDA error %s at line %d\\n\", cudaGetErrorString(e), __LINE__); exit(1); } } while (0)__global__void add(constfloat* a, constfloat* b, float* c, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) c[i] = a[i] + b[i];}int main() {int n = 1000000;size_t bytes = n * sizeof(float);float *a = (float*)malloc(bytes), *b = (float*)malloc(bytes), *c = (float*)malloc(bytes);for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) { a[i] = i; b[i] = 2.0f * i; }float *d_a, *d_b, *d_c; CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_a, bytes)); CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_b, bytes)); CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_c, bytes)); CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_a, a, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice)); CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_b, b, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));int threads = 256;int blocks = (n + threads - 1) / threads; add<<<blocks, threads>>>(d_a, d_b, d_c, n); CHECK(cudaGetLastError()); CHECK(cudaMemcpy(c, d_c, bytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost)); printf(\"c[10] = %.1f, c[%d] = %.1f\\n\", c[10], n - 1, c[n - 1]); CHECK(cudaFree(d_a)); CHECK(cudaFree(d_b)); CHECK(cudaFree(d_c)); free(a); free(b); free(c);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 3#include <cuda_runtime.h>The CUDA runtime header: cudaMalloc, cudaMemcpy, cudaFree and the error types. nvcc includes it automatically, but writing it makes the file clearer. Line 5#define CHECK(call) do { cudaError_t e = (call); if (e != cudaSuccess) { \\A small macro that checks every CUDA call. Almost every CUDA function returns a cudaError_t. Without checks a failed call goes unnoticed and later results are silently wrong. Line 8__global__ void add(const float* a, const float* b, float* c, int n) {__global__ marks a kernel: it runs on the GPU, is launched from the host, and must return void. const and pointers to device memory are normal C++. Line 9int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;The global thread index. Each thread computes a different i, so each handles a different element. Line 10if (i < n) c[i] = a[i] + b[i];The guard. We launch a whole number of blocks, so some threads may have i ≥ n. They must do nothing, or they write past the end of the array. Line 15size_t bytes = n * sizeof(float);size_t for byte counts, because large arrays overflow a 32 bit int. Line 16float *a = (float*)malloc(bytes), *b = (float*)malloc(bytes), *c = (float*)malloc(bytes);Host memory, allocated with ordinary malloc. Line 20CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d_a, bytes));cudaMalloc reserves memory in the GPU's global memory and writes the device address into d_a. The host must never dereference d_a. Line 23CHECK(cudaMemcpy(d_a, a, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice));Copy the input from host to device over PCIe. The last argument names the direction. Line 26int threads = 256;Threads per block: a multiple of 32 (a warp). 128 to 512 is a good default. Line 27int blocks = (n + threads - 1) / threads;Blocks needed = ⌈n ÷ threads⌉ using whole number arithmetic. For a million elements that is 3907 blocks. Line 28add<<<blocks, threads>>>(d_a, d_b, d_c, n);The launch. <<<blocks, threads>>> is the execution configuration. It returns to the host immediately, the GPU runs in the background. Line 29CHECK(cudaGetLastError());A launch returns no error code, so ask for it. This catches a bad configuration such as 2000 threads per block. Line 30CHECK(cudaMemcpy(c, d_c, bytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost));Copy the result back. This cudaMemcpy waits until the kernel has finished, so it also synchronises. Line 33CHECK(cudaFree(d_a)); CHECK(cudaFree(d_b)); CHECK(cudaFree(d_c));Give the device memory back. Leaking it in a loop eventually makes cudaMalloc fail. What it prints c[10] = 30.0, c[999999] = 2999997.0 Compile and run with nvcc nvcc -O3 -arch=sm_75 vector_add.cu -o vector_add./vector_add# on Google Colab, put %%writefile vector_add.cu at the top of a cell, then run !nvcc ... in the next cell What you will see c[10] = 30.0, c[999999] = 2999997.0 -arch=sm_75 compiles for"],[3,"vadd","The complete vector addition program","compute capability 7.5 (a T4). Use sm_80 for an A100, sm_86 for an RTX 30 card, sm_89 for an RTX 40 card. nvcc splits the file: host code goes to the normal C++ compiler, device code becomes PTX (a virtual assembly) and then SASS (the real machine code of that GPU). ipynb Notebook: your first CUDA kernelsHello world from the GPU, vector add in CUDA C with error checks, then the same kernel in Python with Numba, timed against NumPy.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 3 of 4"],[3,"hello","Hello world, asynchronous launches and unified memory","hello.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void hello() { printf(\"Hello from block %d, thread %d\\n\", blockIdx.x, threadIdx.x);}int main() { hello<<<2, 3>>>();cudaDeviceSynchronize();return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 4printf(\"Hello from block %d, thread %d\\n\", blockIdx.x, threadIdx.x);printf works inside a kernel. Output is buffered on the GPU and printed when the host synchronises. Line 8hello<<<2, 3>>>();Two blocks of three threads: six lines of output. Line 9cudaDeviceSynchronize();Kernel launches are asynchronous. Without this wait, main could return and the program end before the GPU prints anything. What it prints Hello from block 0, thread 0 Hello from block 0, thread 1 Hello from block 0, thread 2 Hello from block 1, thread 0 Hello from block 1, thread 1 Hello from block 1, thread 2 Common mistake The order of the six lines is not guaranteed. Blocks may run in any order, so block 1 can print first. Never write code that depends on the order blocks run in. managed.cubeyond the lecture: unified memory #include <cstdio>__global__void scale(float* x, float s, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) x[i] *= s;}int main() {int n = 1 << 20;float* x;cudaMallocManaged(&x, n * sizeof(float));for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) x[i] = 1.0f; scale<<<(n + 255) / 256, 256>>>(x, 3.0f, n);cudaDeviceSynchronize(); printf(\"x[0] = %.1f\\n\", x[0]);cudaFree(x);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 11cudaMallocManaged(&x, n * sizeof(float));cudaMallocManaged gives one pointer that works on both host and device. The driver moves pages to whichever side touches them (unified memory). Line 12for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) x[i] = 1.0f;The host fills the array directly, no cudaMemcpy. Line 14cudaDeviceSynchronize();Now the wait is essential: the host may not read x until the kernel has finished. What it prints x[0] = 3.0 Unified memory makes the first program shorter, but the copies still happen behind the scenes as page migrations. For speed, explicit copies or cudaMemPrefetchAsync are often better, because you control when the data moves. Going deeper: what the launch really does The host puts a launch command into a queue (a stream) and returns. The GPU front end takes blocks from the grid and hands each block to an SM that has room. A block stays on its SM until all its threads finish, and the SM runs its threads warp by warp. Blocks are never guaranteed to run in a fixed order or at the same time, which is why blocks may not wait for each other inside a kernel. Lesson 4 of 4"],[3,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths The CPU is the boss and the GPU is a big team in another building. The boss sends materials, gives the order, waits, and collects the result. Six steps: cudaMalloc, cudaMemcpy H2D, launch <<<⌈n/B⌉, B>>>, guard if (i < n), cudaMemcpy D2H, cudaFree. Kernels are __global__ void. Check every call. Launches are asynchronous and ordered within a stream. cudaMemcpy on the default stream synchronises. Errors in a kernel appear at the next synchronising call, so debug builds call cudaDeviceSynchronize after each launch. nvcc compiles device code to PTX and SASS for the -arch you name. CUDA Graphs record a whole sequence of launches and copies once and replay it with one call, cutting launch overhead. Dynamic parallelism lets kernels launch kernels. Libraries such as cuBLAS, cuDNN and Thrust, and Python front ends like Numba, CuPy and Triton, generate the same kernels for you. Work it out: how many threads start? (lecture 3 slide 4) Kernel1<<<100, 256>>>: 100 × 256 = 25,600 threads. Kernel2<<<50, 1024>>>: 50 × 1024 = 51,200 threads (the slide is right, 5120 said in class was a slip). Total threads = blocks × threads per block"],[4,"","Threads, blocks and grids: indexing in 1-D, 2-D and 3-D","The global index, blocks and idle threads, dim3 and 2-D image indexing, going backwards, grid stride loops and matrix vector per row. · threadIdx · blockIdx · blockDim · gridDim · dim3 · global index · grid stride loop · row major Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 2: The CUDA programming model›Thread indexing Step 2 · Topic 2 of 4 · College Give every thread its own piece of data: the global index, rounding up, 2-D images, 3-D grids, going backwards from a thread number, and grid stride loops. About 50 min5 lessons2 animations8 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The global index in one dimension 2dim3, images and two dimensional grids 3Three dimensions, going backwards, and the slide correction 4Grid stride loops and one thread per row 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[4,"one-d","The global index in one dimension","Every thread runs the same kernel code. The only thing that differs is its position, given by four built in variables. Each thread uses them to pick its own piece of data. Variable Meaning Range threadIdx.x/y/z my position inside my block 0 to blockDim − 1 blockIdx.x/y/z my block's position inside the grid 0 to gridDim − 1 blockDim.x/y/z size of every block set by the launch, at most 1024 threads in total gridDim.x/y/z number of blocks x up to 2³¹ − 1, y and z up to 65,535 Global indexExam favourite i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x Skip all the full blocks before mine, then step to my seat in my block. Blocks neededExam favourite blocks = ⌈N ÷ B⌉ = (N + B − 1) ÷ B Whole number division drops the remainder, so adding B − 1 first rounds up. Going backwards block = ⌊i ÷ B⌋, thread = i − block × B Which thread handles element i. Idle threads idle = blocks × B − N They fail the guard and do nothing. Which element does each thread handle?i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x. Pick a block and a thread, or let it step through every block. Step 3 of 3 Elements N20 blockDim.x (threads per block)8 Grid stride loop with a fixed number of blocks Blocks in the grid (stride mode)2 Look at blockIdx.x1 Look at threadIdx.x3 Blocks needed = ⌈N ÷ B⌉ = (20 + 8 − 1) ÷ 8 = 3. Idle threads = 3 × 8 − 20 = 4. Going back: element e lives in block ⌊e ÷ B⌋, thread e − block × B. Try this N = 20, blocks of 8: ⌈20 ÷ 8⌉ = 3 blocks, 24 threads, 4 idle. Workbook P2.1.5: two blocks of 4. Which thread handles element 5? Block 1, thread 1. Grid stride loop: only 2 blocks of 8 (16 threads) cover 40 elements. Thread 3 of block 0 handles 3, 19 and 35. Real world scenario: cinema seats A cinema has rows of 20 seats. Your ticket says row 3, seat 7. Your seat number counting from the very first seat is 3 × 20 + 7 = 67. Rows are blocks, seats are threads, and 20 is blockDim. Workbook worked example: 10,000 numbers, blocks of 256 Blocks = (10,000 + 255) ÷ 256 = 10,255 ÷ 256 = 40 (whole number division). Threads launched = 40 × 256 = 10,240, so 240 are idle. Thread 10 of block 3 handles i = 3 × 256 + 10 = 778 Lesson 2 of 5"],[4,"two-d","dim3, images and two dimensional grids","Blocks and grids can be 1-D, 2-D or 3-D. The shape is only a way of numbering. It makes images (2-D) and volumes (3-D) easy to map. A missing dimension counts as 1, so dim3 threads(256) means 256 × 1 × 1. Pixels in an imageExam favourite col = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x row = blockIdx.y × blockDim.y + threadIdx.y memory position = row × width + col Images are stored row by row (row major). x is the column, y is the row. Grid for a W × H image grid = (⌈W ÷ Bx⌉, ⌈H ÷ By⌉) Idle threads = all threads − W × H. Position to one number (x fastest)Exam favourite thread = tx + tyBx + tzBxBy block = bx + byGx + bzGxGy global = block × (threads per block) + thread This is how the hardware lines threads up before cutting them into warps. invert.cu __global__void invert(unsignedchar* img, int width, int height) {int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (col < width && row < height) {int idx = row * width + col; img[idx] = 255 - img[idx]; }}void launchInvert(unsignedchar* d_img, int width, int height) {dim3 block(16, 16);dim3 grid((width + block.x - 1) / block.x, (height + block.y - 1) / block.y); invert<<<grid, block>>>(d_img, width, height);} What each marked line does and why Line 2int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;x picks the column. Neighbouring threads in a warp have neighbouring x, so they touch neighbouring bytes in memory (good for speed, chapter 3.2). Line 3int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;y picks the row. Line 4if (col < width && row < height) {Two guards, one per dimension. The right and bottom edge blocks can hang over the image. Line 5int idx = row * width + col;Row major: skip row full rows, then step col places. Line 11dim3 block(16, 16);16 × 16 = 256 threads per block, 8 warps. Line 12dim3 grid((width + block.x - 1) / block.x, (height + block.y - 1) / block.y);Round up in each dimension separately. Workbook worked example: a 1920 × 1080 picture with 16 × 16 blocks Across: ⌈1920 ÷ 16⌉ = 120 exactly. Down: ⌈1080 ÷ 16⌉ = 67.5, rounded up to 68. Blocks = 120 × 68 = 8160. Threads = 8160 × 256 = 2,088,960. Idle = 2,088,960 − 2,073,600 = 15,360 (the half block row at the bottom) Remember Using 32 × 8 blocks for 1920 × 1080 wastes nothing (1080 ÷ 8 = 135) and each warp covers one full 32 pixel row. Picking a block shape that divides the image is free speed. Lesson 3 of 5"],[4,"three-d","Three dimensions, going backwards, and the slide correction","Lecture 3 slides 15 to 16: grid 5 × 4 blocks, each block 5 × 5 threads. Where is global thread 343? Threads per block = 25. Block number = ⌊343 ÷ 25⌋ = 13, leftover 343 − 325 = 18. Block 13 in a grid 5 wide: x = 13 mod 5 = 3, y = ⌊13 ÷ 5⌋ = 2. So blockIdx = (3, 2, 0). Thread 18 in a block 5 wide: x = 18 mod 5 = 3, y = ⌊18 ÷ 5⌋ = 3. So threadIdx = (3, 3, 0). The slide showed threadIdx (2, 3, 0), which is actually thread 342 One number to position block = ⌊g ÷ threads per block⌋, leftover = g − block × threads per block x = leftover mod Bx, y = ⌊leftover ÷ Bx⌋ The same mod and divide works for the block inside the grid. In the exam Exams love going backwards. Always divide by the number of threads per block first, then split the leftover with mod and divide, x first. Going deeper: why the x dimension is special Warps are cut from the linear thread number t = x + y Bx + z BxBy. So a warp holds 32 consecutive x values (or several short rows if Bx < 32). Make x the dimension that walks along contiguous memory, and keep Bx a multiple of 32 when you can. Lesson 4 of 5"],[4,"patterns","Grid stride loops and one thread per row","Two more patterns finish the picture. A grid stride loop lets a fixed size grid cover any N: each thread handles i, then i plus the total number of threads, and so on. It also reuses each thread's set up work and lets you launch just enough blocks to fill the GPU. saxpy_stride.cu __global__void saxpy(int n, float a, constfloat* x, float* y) {for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x) { y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i]; }} What each marked line does and why Line 2for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n;Start at my global index. Line 3i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x) {Jump by the total number of threads in the grid. With 2 blocks of 8, thread 3 handles 3, 19, 35 and so on. Line 4y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i];SAXPY: y = a x + y, a classic BLAS routine. One thread per output row. Quiz 1 question 7 launches one thread per row of a matrix vector product: thread i computes the dot product of row i of A with x. matvec.cu __global__void matVecMul(constfloat* A, constfloat* x, float* y, int N) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < N) {float sum = 0.0f;for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) { sum += A[i * N + j] * x[j]; } y[i] = sum; }} What each marked line does and why Line 2int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;With one block of 4 threads, thread 2 has i = 0 × 4 + 2 = 2. Line 6sum += A[i * N + j] * x[j];Row i starts at i × N in the flat row major array. Line 8y[i] = sum;One store per thread. Common mistake In matVecMul neighbouring threads read A[i × N + j] for the same j, which are N floats apart. That is a strided, uncoalesced read (chapter 3.2). A transposed layout or one warp per row is faster for large N. ipynb Notebook: 2D grids, one thread per pixelGrey scale and a 3 × 3 blur on an image with a 2D grid of 2D blocks.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[4,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Every worker gets a badge with a team number and a seat number. From these two numbers each worker knows which piece of the job is theirs. i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x, blocks = (N + B − 1) / B, guard i < N. In 2-D, col from x, row from y, index row × width + col. The thread to data mapping decides memory efficiency: let threadIdx.x walk contiguous addresses. Grid stride loops decouple the grid from N and are the default style in libraries. Persistent kernels launch exactly one wave of blocks that pull work from a queue. Hopper adds thread block clusters, a level between block and grid whose blocks can share memory. Index arithmetic in 64 bits matters once tensors exceed 2³¹ elements."],[5,"","SMs, warps and hiding latency","SM anatomy, warps and lanes, wasted lanes, Little's law and latency hiding, and registers per thread. · SM · warp · lane · SIMT · Little's law · latency hiding · registers · Fermi Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 2: The CUDA programming model›Warps and SMs Step 2 · Topic 3 of 4 · College Look inside an SM, see how blocks are cut into warps of 32, and use Little's law to work out how many warps a GPU needs to hide its latency. About 45 min5 lessons2 animations9 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Streaming multiprocessors: what really runs your threads 2Warps, lanes and SIMT 3Hiding latency: Little's law 4Registers: the hidden limit 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[5,"sm","Streaming multiprocessors: what really runs your threads","A GPU is a set of identical streaming multiprocessors (SMs). Each SM has its own cores, registers, schedulers and on chip memory. A kernel's blocks are handed out to SMs, and an SM may hold several blocks at once. Software (what you write) Hardware (what runs it) Rule Thread A CUDA core (one lane) Has its own registers and program counter Warp of 32 threads A warp scheduler Issues one instruction for all 32 lanes Block (up to 1024 threads) One SM Stays on that SM until it finishes, shares its shared memory Grid (all blocks of a launch) The whole GPU Blocks spread over all SMs in any order Common mistake In class it was said that grids sit inside cores. It is the other way round: a thread runs on a core, a block on one SM, and a grid across all SMs. Fermi SM part (lecture 1) Count Job CUDA cores 32 Integer and floating point units, one lane each Warp schedulers and dispatch units 2 Two warps can issue at the same time Register file 32,768 × 32 bit Private variables of every resident thread Load/store units 16 Addresses for 16 threads per clock Special function units 4 sin, cos, reciprocal, square root Shared memory / L1 64 KB Split 48/16 or 16/48 between shared memory and L1 Fermi had 16 SMs × 32 cores = 512 cores. An A100 has 108 SMs with 64 FP32 cores each, an H100 has 132. Lesson 2 of 5"],[5,"warps","Warps, lanes and SIMT","The SM does not schedule single threads. It schedules warps: 32 threads with consecutive linear thread numbers. All lanes of a warp execute the same instruction at the same time on their own data. NVIDIA calls this SIMT, single instruction, multiple threads. Warps per blockExam favourite warps = ⌈B ÷ 32⌉ A part full warp still takes 32 lanes. Warp and lane of thread tExam favourite warp = ⌊t ÷ 32⌋, lane = t − 32 × warp For 2-D blocks first compute t = x + y × Bx. Wasted lanes wasted = 32 × warps − B efficiency = B ÷ (32 × warps) Use block sizes that are multiples of 32. Real world scenario: a school trip in 32 seat buses The school hires buses with exactly 32 seats. 100 children need 4 buses, and the last bus leaves with only 4 children and 28 empty seats. The fuel cost is the same for a full bus. Choosing 96 or 128 children per trip wastes nothing. who_am_i.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void whoAmI() {int t = threadIdx.x + threadIdx.y * blockDim.x;int warp = t / warpSize;int lane = t % warpSize;if (lane == 0) printf(\"block %d: warp %d starts at thread (%d, %d)\\n\",blockIdx.x, warp, threadIdx.x, threadIdx.y);}int main() {dim3 block(16, 16); whoAmI<<<1, block>>>();cudaDeviceSynchronize();return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 4int t = threadIdx.x + threadIdx.y * blockDim.x;Linear thread number inside the block, x fastest. This is the order the hardware uses to cut warps. Line 5int warp = t / warpSize;warpSize is a built in constant, 32 on every NVIDIA GPU so far. Line 7if (lane == 0)Only lane 0 of each warp prints, so we see one line per warp. Line 13dim3 block(16, 16);A 16 × 16 block has 256 threads, so 8 warps of two rows each. What it prints block 0: warp 0 starts at thread (0, 0) block 0: warp 1 starts at thread (0, 2) ... (8 lines, warp w starts at row 2w) Going deeper: independent thread scheduling (Volta and later) Since Volta each thread has its own program counter, so threads of a diverged warp can interleave and even wait for each other. The warp still issues one instruction at a time for its active lanes, so divergence still costs time (chapter 6.1), and code that relies on all 32 lanes being in step must say so with __syncwarp(). Lesson 3 of 5"],[5,"little","Hiding latency: Little's law","A GPU core waits about 20 ticks for an arithmetic result and several hundred ticks for global memory. It does not stall. Each tick the warp scheduler picks another warp that is ready and issues its instruction. The cost of switching is zero, because every warp keeps its registers on the SM all the time. Little's lawExam favourite jobs in flight = latency (ticks) × throughput (jobs per tick) To keep a pipe full you need as many jobs inside it as its length times its width. In warps warps in flight = (latency × operations per tick) ÷ 32 One warp instruction is 32 operations. Memory version bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency To use all the bandwidth, this many bytes must be on the way at every moment. Workbook worked example: Fermi against Kepler Fermi: latency 20 ticks, 32 operations per tick. In flight 20 × 32 = 640 operations = 20 warps. Kepler: latency 20 ticks, 192 operations per tick. In flight 20 × 192 = 3840 operations = 120 warps. Kepler needs 6 times more parallel work to stay busy Real world scenario: a pizza oven A pizza bakes for 8 minutes and the chef can slide in one pizza per minute. To keep the oven always working, there must be 8 pizzas inside at any moment. With only 4 orders the chef stands idle half the time. Warps are the pizzas. Remember Latency hiding needs either many warps (thread level parallelism) or several independent instructions inside each warp (instruction level parallelism). Two independent loads per warp halve the warps needed. Lesson 4 of 5"],[5,"registers","Registers: the hidden limit","Registers per thread registers per thread = registers on the SM ÷ threads on the SM Lecture example: 32K registers for 1024 threads gives 32 each. Binary K 32K = 32 × 1024 = 32,768 64K = 65,536 For registers K means 1024. Bits to name a register smallest b with 2b ≥ number of registers An instruction names its operands, so 32 registers need 5 bits, 255 need 8. Every resident thread keeps all its variables in the SM's register file, which is fixed when the chip is made (65,536 on recent GPUs). A kernel that needs more registers per thread leaves room for fewer threads, so there are fewer warps to hide latency. If a kernel needs more registers than allowed, the compiler spills them to local memory, which lives in slow DRAM. How many threads fit by registers? (workbook P2.5.4) The kernel needs 40 registers per thread and the SM has 65,536. 65,536 ÷ 40 = 1638.4 threads. Threads come in whole warps: 1638 ÷ 32 = 51.2, so 51 warps. 51 warps = 1632 threads (the exact allocation rules are in chapter 2.4) Lesson 5 of 5"],[5,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths The GPU moves threads around in groups of 32, like buses. While one bus waits at a red light, the driver of another bus goes. With enough buses the road is always busy. warps = ⌈B/32⌉, warp = ⌊t/32⌋, lane = t mod 32. Little: in flight = latency × throughput, divide by 32 for warps. Registers per thread = register file ÷ threads. Each SM has 4 sub partitions on recent GPUs, each with its own scheduler issuing one warp instruction per tick. Occupancy (chapter 2.4) measures how many warps are resident, and the profiler's \"stall reasons\" say why ready warps are missing. Volkov showed that high ILP can beat high occupancy: fewer warps with more independent work per thread reach peak bandwidth. Modern GEMM kernels run at low occupancy with large register tiles and asynchronous copies."],[6,"","Occupancy: how many warps fit on an SM","Occupancy from four limits, register rounding, shared memory reserve, launch bounds and the occupancy API. · occupancy · registers · shared memory · launch bounds · occupancy calculator Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 2: The CUDA programming model›Occupancy Step 2 · Topic 4 of 4 · College Work out how many blocks fit on one SM from four limits, threads, blocks, registers and shared memory, and learn when chasing 100 percent is not worth it. About 45 min5 lessons1 animation6 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What occupancy means 2Limit 1 and 2: threads and blocks 3Limit 3: registers, with rounding 4Limit 4: shared memory 5Is higher occupancy always faster? Lesson 1 of 5"],[6,"what","What occupancy means","Chapter 2.3 showed that an SM hides waiting by switching between warps. Occupancy says how many warps an SM really holds compared with the most it could hold. OccupancyExam favourite occupancy = active warps per SM ÷ maximum warps per SM A100: maximum 64 warps (2048 threads). 48 active warps is 75 percent. Blocks that fitExam favourite blocks = min(warp limit, block limit, register limit, shared memory limit) Every resource must fit at the same time, so the tightest one decides. GPU (compute capability) Max threads Max warps Max blocks Registers Shared memory per SM Fermi (2.0) 1536 48 8 32,768 48 KB T4 (7.5) 1024 32 16 65,536 64 KB A100 (8.0) 2048 64 32 65,536 up to 164 KB RTX 30xx (8.6) 1536 48 16 65,536 up to 100 KB H100 (9.0) 2048 64 32 65,536 up to 228 KB Real world scenario: a cinema with four rules A cinema lets in a group only if there are enough seats, the group count is under the limit, the snack counter has enough cups and the car park has space. The hall may have empty seats but still turn groups away because the car park is full. The SM works the same way. Lesson 2 of 5"],[6,"threads","Limit 1 and 2: threads and blocks","An SM holds at most a fixed number of warps and a fixed number of blocks. Small blocks run into the block limit long before the warps are used up. Workbook worked example: 1024 threads, 32 warps, 8 blocks per SM, 16 × 16 blocks A block has 256 threads = 8 warps. Warp limit: 32 ÷ 8 = 4 blocks. Block limit: 8 blocks. The smaller is 4 blocks, so 4 × 8 = 32 warps. occupancy = 32 ÷ 32 = 100 percent Small blocks on the same SM (P2.3.3) A 64 thread block is 2 warps. Warp limit 32 ÷ 2 = 16 blocks, block limit 8. 8 blocks × 2 warps = 16 warps. 16 ÷ 32 = 50 percent: the block limit bites Smallest block for full occupancy Bmin = max threads per SM ÷ max blocks per SM A100: 2048 ÷ 32 = 64 threads. Anything smaller cannot reach 100 percent. Lesson 3 of 5"],[6,"regs","Limit 3: registers, with rounding","Registers are not handed out one by one. On recent GPUs each warp receives registers in chunks of 256, so the per warp count is rounded up. Registers per warpExam favourite per warp = ⌈(r × 32) ÷ 256⌉ × 256 r = registers per thread. 37 registers give 1184, rounded up to 1280, the same as 40. Register limit in blocksExam favourite blocks = ⌊⌊65,536 ÷ per warp⌋ ÷ warps per block⌋ First whole warps, then whole blocks. Workbook worked example 7.5: A100, 256 threads, 40 registers 40 × 32 = 1280 registers per warp (already a multiple of 256). 65,536 ÷ 1280 = 51.2, so 51 warps. 51 ÷ 8 warps per block = 6.4, so 6 blocks = 48 warps. 48 ÷ 64 = 75 percent bounds.cu // Ask the compiler to keep registers low enough for 4 blocks of 256 threads per SM__global__void __launch_bounds__(256, 4)heavy(float *out, constfloat *in, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i >= n) return;float a = in[i], s = 0.0f;for (int k = 1; k <= 8; ++k) s += a / k; out[i] = s;} What each marked line does and why Line 2__global__ void __launch_bounds__(256, 4)__launch_bounds__(max threads per block, min blocks per SM) tells the compiler how you will launch the kernel. Line 3heavy(float *out, const float *in, int n) {With 256 threads and 4 blocks the SM needs 1024 threads, so the compiler caps each thread at 65,536 ÷ 1024 = 64 registers. Line 7for (int k = 1; k <= 8; ++k) s += a / k;A small loop. If a kernel needed more registers than the cap, the compiler would spill to local memory. Remember The flag -maxrregcount=N caps registers for every kernel in a file. __launch_bounds__ is better because it is per kernel and says why. Lesson 4 of 5"],[6,"smem","Limit 4: shared memory","Each block that uses shared memory takes its bytes from the SM's pool. On compute capability 8.x the system also reserves 1 KB per block. Shared memory limitExam favourite blocks = ⌊shared memory per SM ÷ (bytes per block + reserved)⌋ CC 8.6: 102,400 bytes per SM, 1024 reserved per block. Workbook 9.7: CC 8.6, 256 threads, 32 registers, 32 KB of shared memory Warp limit 48 ÷ 8 = 6. Block limit 16. Registers 32 × 32 = 1024 per warp, 64 warps, 8 blocks. Shared memory: 102,400 ÷ (32,768 + 1024) = 3.03, so 3 blocks. The smallest limit is 3 blocks = 24 warps. 24 ÷ 48 = 50 percent occupancy_api.cu #include <cstdio>#define CHECK(x) do { cudaError_t e = (x); if (e != cudaSuccess) { \\ printf(\"CUDA error %s at line %d\\n\", cudaGetErrorString(e), __LINE__); return1; } } while (0)__global__void scale(float *y, constfloat *x, float a, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) y[i] = a * x[i];}int main() {cudaDeviceProp p; CHECK(cudaGetDeviceProperties(&p, 0));int blocks = 0; CHECK(cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor(&blocks, scale, 256, 0));int warps = blocks * 256 / 32;int maxWarps = p.maxThreadsPerMultiProcessor / 32; printf(\"%d blocks of 256 per SM, occupancy %.1f%%\\n\", blocks, 100.0 * warps / maxWarps);int minGrid = 0, best = 0; CHECK(cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize(&minGrid, &best, scale, 0, 0)); printf(\"suggested block size %d, at least %d blocks\\n\", best, minGrid);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 13int blocks = 0;The result lands in blocks. In the call below, the last argument is the dynamic shared memory per block in bytes. Line 14CHECK(cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor(&blocks, scale, 256, 0));cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor does the same four limit sum for a real kernel on the real GPU. Line 15int warps = blocks * 256 / 32;Blocks × threads ÷ 32 gives active warps. Line 20CHECK(cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize(&minGrid, &best, scale, 0, 0));cudaOccupancyMaxPotentialBlockSize suggests the block size that gives the highest occupancy, and the smallest grid that fills the GPU. What it prints (on an A100) 8 blocks of 256 per SM, occupancy 100.0% suggested block size 1024, at least 216 blocks Remember The printed numbers above are what this code gives on an A100 by the four limit rule. They were worked out, not measured on a real GPU. Lesson 5 of 5"],[6,"levels","Is higher occupancy always faster?","One idea, four depths The SM is a hall with limited seats, groups, cups and parking. Occupancy is the share of seats in use. More people waiting means less idle time, up to a point. Blocks = min of four limits. Registers round up to 256 per warp. Shared memory adds 1 KB reserved per block on 8.x. Occupancy = blocks × warps per block ÷ max warps. Once enough warps hide the latency (Little's law), more occupancy gives nothing. A kernel at 50 percent with 4 independent loads per thread can beat one at 100 percent with 1. Measure with Nsight Compute \"achieved occupancy\", which can be lower than the theory because of tail effects and uneven blocks. High performance GEMM and attention kernels deliberately run at 12 to 25 percent occupancy: each thread holds a large tile of results in registers, and asynchronous copies (cp.async, TMA) keep memory busy. Occupancy is a means, not the goal."],[7,"","The GPU memory hierarchy","Memory spaces, scope and lifetime, constant memory, caches, pinned and unified memory, copy time. · memory hierarchy · registers · shared memory · global memory · constant memory · pinned memory · unified memory Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 3: GPU memory›Memory hierarchy Step 3 · Topic 1 of 3 · College Learn the five places a value can live on a GPU, who can see each one and for how long, and how data gets from the CPU to the GPU. About 45 min5 lessons1 animation2 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Five places a value can live 2Quiz 1 question 8: visible to all SMs, for the whole application 3Constant memory and caches 4Host memory: pageable, pinned and unified 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[7,"spaces","Five places a value can live","A GPU has several memories because no memory can be big, fast and visible to everyone all at once. Fast memory sits on the chip and is small. Big memory is far away and slow. CUDA gives you a menu and lets you choose. Memory Who sees it Lives for Speed How you get it Registers one thread the thread about 1 tick any normal local variable Local one thread the thread DRAM speed (cached) big private arrays, spilled registers Shared all threads of one block the block about 20 to 30 ticks __shared__ Global all threads, all kernels, the host until cudaFree about 400 to 800 ticks cudaMalloc Constant all threads, read only the application fast when the warp reads one address __constant__ and cudaMemcpyToSymbol Where does a variable live?Pick a declaration and step: where it is stored, who can see it, how long it lives, and how fast it is. Step 4 of 4 Declaration __shared__ float tile[256]; Stored in: shared memory (on the SM) Seen by: all threads of one block Lives for: the block Speed: about 20 to 30 ticks Each block gets its own copy. Threads of other blocks cannot see it. Green boxes show who can see it. Try this A plain local variable lives in a register: private, gone when the thread ends, the fastest. A __shared__ array: one copy per block, seen by all its threads, on the SM. Memory from cudaMalloc is global: every SM sees it and it survives across kernel launches. A big private array can end up in local memory, which really lives in slow DRAM. Common mistake Local memory is not near the thread. \"Local\" means private. It lives in device DRAM and is only cached on chip. Real world scenario: a kitchen The chopping board in front of a cook is a register: tiny, private, instant. The shared counter of one cooking team is shared memory. The big store room everyone walks to is global memory. The recipe card pinned on the wall that nobody may change is constant memory. Lesson 2 of 5"],[7,"q8","Quiz 1 question 8: visible to all SMs, for the whole application","The question asks which memory every SM can reach directly and which lasts for the whole program. Test each option for both properties. Rakesh's worked solution Shared memory belongs to one block on one SM, so it fails the \"all SMs\" test. L1 cache is private to each SM, so it fails too. L2 cache is shared by all SMs, but it is a cache, not a memory space. You cannot allocate in it and the hardware evicts what it likes. Global memory is addressed by every thread on every SM, and an allocation lives until cudaFree or program exit. Answer: option 1, global memory In the exam A memory space is something you name and address. A cache is a hardware copy of global data that fills and empties by itself. This difference decides questions like Q8. Lesson 3 of 5"],[7,"constant","Constant memory and caches","Constant memory holds small read only values such as filter weights. The host writes it before the launch. When all 32 threads of a warp read the same address, one fetch is broadcast to the whole warp. If they read different addresses, the reads are done one after another. blur_constant.cu #include <cstdio>__constant__float c_weights[9]; // 36 bytes in constant memory, read only for kernels__global__void blur3x3(constfloat *in, float *out, int w, int h) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (x < 1 || y < 1 || x >= w - 1 || y >= h - 1) return;float s = 0.0f;for (int dy = -1; dy <= 1; ++dy)for (int dx = -1; dx <= 1; ++dx) s += c_weights[(dy + 1) * 3 + (dx + 1)] * in[(y + dy) * w + (x + dx)]; out[y * w + x] = s;}int main() {float h_w[9];for (int k = 0; k < 9; ++k) h_w[k] = 1.0f / 9.0f;cudaMemcpyToSymbol(c_weights, h_w, sizeof(h_w)); printf(\"weights copied to constant memory\\n\");return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 3__constant__ float c_weights[9]; // 36 bytes in constant memory, read only for kernels__constant__ puts the array in the 64 KB constant bank. Kernels may read it but never write it. Line 12s += c_weights[(dy + 1) * 3 + (dx + 1)] * in[(y + dy) * w + (x + dx)];Every thread of the warp reads the same weight at the same loop step, so the broadcast works perfectly. Line 18for (int k = 0; k < 9; ++k) h_w[k] = 1.0f / 9.0f;The weights are made on the CPU first. Line 19cudaMemcpyToSymbol(c_weights, h_w, sizeof(h_w));cudaMemcpyToSymbol copies host data into a __constant__ variable by its name. What it prints weights copied to constant memory Cache Where What it holds L1 / texture per SM, shares space with shared memory recent global and local loads Constant cache per SM recent constant memory reads L2 one for the whole GPU all traffic to device DRAM (40 MB on an A100) Going deeper: read only loads and __restrict__ Marking pointers const float *__restrict__ in tells the compiler that no other pointer writes the same data, so it may use the read only data path. The function __ldg(&in[i]) asks for it directly. Lesson 4 of 5"],[7,"host","Host memory: pageable, pinned and unified","Data starts in the CPU's RAM. Normal malloc memory is pageable: the operating system may move it. The GPU's copy engine cannot read such memory directly, so the driver first copies it into a hidden locked buffer. Pinned (page locked) memory from cudaMallocHost skips that extra copy and is required for copies that overlap with kernels (chapter 6.2). Copy timeExam favourite t = bytes ÷ bandwidth The PCIe link is far slower than the GPU's own DRAM, so copy once and reuse. Worth sending to the GPU tcopy in + tkernel + tcopy out < tCPU From chapter 1.2: count the copies too. pinned_vs_pageable.cu #include <cstdio>#include <cstdlib>int main() {constsize_t bytes = 256u << 20; // 256 MBfloat *pageable = (float *)malloc(bytes);float *pinned = nullptr;cudaMallocHost(&pinned, bytes); // page locked host memoryfloat *d = nullptr;cudaMalloc(&d, bytes);cudaEvent_t t0, t1;cudaEventCreate(&t0); cudaEventCreate(&t1);float ms = 0.0f;cudaEventRecord(t0);cudaMemcpy(d, pageable, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);cudaEventRecord(t1); cudaEventSynchronize(t1);cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, t0, t1); printf(\"pageable: %.2f GB/s\\n\", bytes / ms / 1e6);cudaEventRecord(t0);cudaMemcpy(d, pinned, bytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);cudaEventRecord(t1); cudaEventSynchronize(t1);cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, t0, t1); printf(\"pinned: %.2f GB/s\\n\", bytes / ms / 1e6);cudaFree(d); cudaFreeHost(pinned); free(pageable);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 8cudaMallocHost(&pinned, bytes); // page locked host memorycudaMallocHost returns page locked memory. Use it for buffers you copy often, and do not lock too much of the RAM. Line 12cudaEvent_t t0, t1;CUDA events time work on the GPU timeline. Elapsed time comes back in milliseconds. Line 20printf(\"pageable: %.2f GB/s\\n\", bytes / ms / 1e6);bytes ÷ ms ÷ 10⁶ gives GB/s. Line 28cudaFree(d); cudaFreeHost(pinned); free(pageable);Every cudaMallocHost needs a cudaFreeHost. What it prints (typical result: pinned is faster than pageable, how much depends on the machine) unified.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void addOne(float *a, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) a[i] += 1.0f;}int main() {int n = 1 << 20, dev = 0;float *a = nullptr;cudaMallocManaged(&a, n * sizeof(float)); // one pointer for CPU and GPUfor (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) a[i] = i; // pages start on the CPUcudaMemPrefetchAsync(a, n * sizeof(float), dev); // move them before the kernel needs them addOne<<<(n + 255) / 256, 256>>>(a, n);cudaDeviceSynchronize(); // wait before the CPU reads printf(\"a[10] = %.1f\\n\", a[10]);cudaFree(a);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 11cudaMallocManaged(&a, n * sizeof(float)); // one pointer for CPU and GPUcudaMallocManaged gives one pointer that works on both CPU and GPU. Pages move on demand. Line 13cudaMemPrefetchAsync(a, n * sizeof(float), dev); // move them before the kernel needs themPrefetching moves the pages before the kernel starts, so the kernel does not stop on page faults. Line 15cudaDeviceSynchronize(); // wait before the CPU readsThe CPU must wait before reading results, otherwise it reads old values. What it prints a[10] = 11.0 ipynb Notebook: copies, pinned memory and honest timingCUDA events, pageable against pinned copies, and Amdahl with the copy time.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[7,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Close memory is small and fast, far memory is big and slow. Keep the things you use again and again close by. Registers (thread), shared (block), global (all, host too), constant (all, read only, broadcast), local (private, but in DRAM). Copy time = bytes ÷ bandwidth. Pinned memory is needed for async copies. A good kernel loads each byte from DRAM once and reuses it many times from registers and shared memory. That is the idea of tiling (chapters 5.2 to 5.4). The roofline (chapter 4.2) turns this into numbers. Hopper adds distributed shared memory between the blocks of a cluster and the Tensor Memory Accelerator, which copies whole tiles from global to shared memory without using threads."],[8,"","Coalescing: reading global memory well","Sectors per warp read, stride and alignment, Quiz 1 Q5, sector model, AoS and SoA, transposes. · coalescing · sectors · stride · row major · SoA · transpose Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 3: GPU memory›Coalescing Step 3 · Topic 2 of 3 · College See how a warp's 32 loads are merged into 32 byte sectors, why strides and column walks waste bandwidth, and how to lay out data so every byte fetched is used. About 45 min5 lessons1 animation5 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Memory hands out 32 byte boxes 2Rows, columns and Quiz 1 question 5 3The stride benchmark (lecture 11) 4Data layout: AoS against SoA, and wider loads 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[8,"sectors","Memory hands out 32 byte boxes","DRAM does not deliver single floats. It delivers sectors of 32 bytes (8 floats). When the 32 threads of a warp issue a load, the hardware collects all their addresses and fetches the smallest set of sectors that covers them. This is called coalescing. Bytes the warp wantsExam favourite useful = 32 × element size Floats: 128 bytes. Doubles: 256 bytes. EfficiencyExam favourite efficiency = useful bytes ÷ (sectors × 32) Stride 1 floats: 128 ÷ (4 × 32) = 100 percent. Sectors for stride s (floats, aligned) sectors = min(32, 4 × s) Each thread lands in its own sector once s ≥ 8. Coalescing: how many 32 byte sectors does one warp read touch?Thread t reads element offset + t × stride. Step to add one thread at a time and watch the sectors fill. Step 32 of 32 Stride (elements)1 Element size Start offset (bytes)0 Green = every byte of the sector is used, orange = part used, so the rest of the 32 bytes is wasted traffic. A warp's loads are combined (coalesced) into as few sectors as possible. Sector model on a T4: about 206 ÷ min(1, 8) = 206 GB/s of useful bandwidth (workbook 11.1). Try this Stride 1 floats: 32 × 4 = 128 bytes in exactly 4 sectors, 100 percent useful (workbook 7.2). Stride 2: every other float, 8 sectors, half of every sector is wasted. Stride 32 (walking down a column): 32 sectors for 128 useful bytes, 12.5 percent. Stride 1 but starting 16 bytes into a sector: 5 sectors, 80 percent (misalignment). Python # Sectors touched by one warp: thread t reads elem bytes at offset + t*stride*elemdef warp_read(stride, elem=4, offset=0): first = {(offset + t * stride * elem) // 32for t inrange(32)} last = {(offset + t * stride * elem + elem - 1) // 32for t inrange(32)} n = len(first | last)return n, round(100 * 32 * elem / (32 * n), 1)for s in [1, 2, 8, 32]:print(\"stride\", s, \"->\", warp_read(s))print(\"16 byte offset ->\", warp_read(1, 4, 16)) Output stride 1 -> (4, 100.0) stride 2 -> (8, 50.0) stride 8 -> (32, 12.5) stride 32 -> (32, 12.5) 16 byte offset -> (5, 80.0) Real world scenario: buying sweets Buying one sweet from each of 32 different shops costs 32 trips. Buying 32 sweets from one shop costs one trip. Neighbouring threads should read neighbouring addresses so the warp visits as few shops as possible. Lesson 2 of 5"],[8,"rowcol","Rows, columns and Quiz 1 question 5","In C, a 2-D array is stored row by row (row major). Element [y][x] sits at position y × width + x. Consecutive threads should vary x, the fast moving index. Thread t reads Neighbouring lanes are Sectors per warp read (floats) A[row][t] (along a row) 4 bytes apart 4 (100 percent) A[t][col] (down a column of width 1024) 4096 bytes apart 32 (12.5 percent) Quiz 1 question 5: the store B[x × height + y] blockDim.x = 32, so a warp holds 32 consecutive x values and one y. Lane x writes B[x × 2048 + y]. Neighbouring lanes are 2048 floats = 8192 bytes apart. Every lane falls in a different 128 byte segment. 32 segments for the store (the load from A uses only 1) transpose.cu #define TILE 32// Naive: reads are coalesced, writes jump by n floats (like Quiz 1 question 5)__global__void transposeNaive(float *out, constfloat *in, int n) {int x = blockIdx.x * TILE + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * TILE + threadIdx.y;if (x < n && y < n) out[x * n + y] = in[y * n + x];}// Through shared memory: both the read and the write walk along rows__global__void transposeShared(float *out, constfloat *in, int n) {__shared__float tile[TILE][TILE + 1]; // +1 avoids bank conflicts (chapter 3.3)int x = blockIdx.x * TILE + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * TILE + threadIdx.y;if (x < n && y < n) tile[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = in[y * n + x];__syncthreads(); x = blockIdx.y * TILE + threadIdx.x; // swap the block coordinates y = blockIdx.x * TILE + threadIdx.y;if (x < n && y < n) out[y * n + x] = tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y];} What each marked line does and why Line 4__global__ void transposeNaive(float *out, const float *in, int n) {This is the Quiz 1 question 5 pattern: the read is along a row, the write goes down a column. Line 7if (x < n && y < n) out[x * n + y] = in[y * n + x];out[x × n + y]: lanes with consecutive x write n floats apart, so every lane needs its own segment. Line 12__shared__ float tile[TILE][TILE + 1]; // +1 avoids bank conflicts (chapter 3.3)The tile is 33 floats wide. Chapter 3.3 explains why the extra column matters. Line 15if (x < n && y < n) tile[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = in[y * n + x];Step 1: read a 32 × 32 tile along rows (coalesced) into shared memory. Line 16__syncthreads();Every thread must finish writing the tile before anyone reads it back. Line 17x = blockIdx.y * TILE + threadIdx.x; // swap the block coordinatesSwap the block coordinates, because the output tile sits in the mirrored position. Line 19if (x < n && y < n) out[y * n + x] = tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y];Step 2: write along rows again, reading the tile by column from fast shared memory. Lesson 3 of 5"],[8,"stride","The stride benchmark (lecture 11)","The lecture measured a copy kernel on a T4 while making threads read further and further apart. At stride 1 it reached 206 GB/s, about 64 percent of the T4's 320 GB/s data sheet value. Sector modelExam favourite useful bandwidth ≈ (stride 1 bandwidth) ÷ min(s, 8) Once threads are 8 floats apart each lane needs its own sector, and the model stops falling. Real GPUs fall further at big strides because of DRAM page and TLB effects. Stride Model (GB/s) Sectors per warp read 1 206 4 2 103 8 4 51.5 16 8 25.75 32 16 25.75 32 32 25.75 32 stride_copy.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void copyStride(float *out, constfloat *in, int n, int s) {int i = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) * s; // thread t touches element t * sif (i < n) out[i] = in[i];}int main() {constint n = 1 << 24;float *in, *out;cudaMalloc(&in, n * sizeof(float));cudaMalloc(&out, n * sizeof(float));cudaEvent_t a, b;cudaEventCreate(&a); cudaEventCreate(&b);for (int s = 1; s <= 32; s *= 2) {int threads = n / s;cudaEventRecord(a); copyStride<<<(threads + 255) / 256, 256>>>(out, in, n, s);cudaEventRecord(b); cudaEventSynchronize(b);float ms; cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, a, b);double useful = 2.0 * threads * sizeof(float); // one read and one write per thread printf(\"stride %2d: %7.1f GB/s useful\\n\", s, useful / ms / 1e6); }cudaFree(in); cudaFree(out);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 4int i = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) * s; // thread t touches element t * sThread t copies element t × s, so neighbouring lanes are s floats apart. Line 16int threads = n / s;For bigger strides we launch fewer threads, so each run copies n ÷ s elements. Line 21double useful = 2.0 * threads * sizeof(float); // one read and one write per threadUseful bytes are one read and one write of 4 bytes per thread. Wasted sector bytes are not counted. What it prints (on a T4 the lecture measured 206 GB/s at stride 1, falling as the stride grows) Lesson 4 of 5"],[8,"layout","Data layout: AoS against SoA, and wider loads","particles.cu struct ParticleAoS { float x, y, z, mass; }; // array of structures: 16 bytes per particlestruct ParticlesSoA { float *x, *y, *z, *mass; }; // structure of arrays: one array per field__global__void moveAoS(ParticleAoS *p, float dx, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) p[i].x += dx; // lanes read floats 16 bytes apart: stride 4}__global__void moveSoA(ParticlesSoA p, float dx, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) p.x[i] += dx; // lanes read neighbouring floats: stride 1} What each marked line does and why Line 1struct ParticleAoS { float x, y, z, mass; }; // array of structures: 16 bytes per particleArray of structures: x, y, z and mass of one particle sit together, 16 bytes per particle. Line 3struct ParticlesSoA { float *x, *y, *z, *mass; }; // structure of arrays: one array per fieldStructure of arrays: all x values together, then all y values and so on. Line 7if (i < n) p[i].x += dx; // lanes read floats 16 bytes apart: stride 4Reading only p[i].x makes lanes 16 bytes apart, so 3 of every 4 bytes fetched are wasted. Line 12if (i < n) p.x[i] += dx; // lanes read neighbouring floats: stride 1Now lane i reads x[i]: a perfect stride 1 read. Vector loads float4: 32 lanes × 16 bytes = 512 bytes = 16 sectors, 100 percent One instruction moves four floats per thread, so fewer instructions for the same bytes. The address must be 16 byte aligned. Remember AoS is natural for the CPU and often slow on the GPU. When kernels touch only some fields, store each field in its own array. Going deeper: L1 caching and 128 byte lines L1 and L2 track data in 128 byte lines made of four 32 byte sectors. Older GPUs (Fermi, Kepler) moved whole 128 byte lines on an L1 miss, so a scattered warp could move 32 × 128 = 4096 bytes. Modern GPUs fetch only the sectors that are needed, which is why the course counts sectors. Lesson 5 of 5"],[8,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Neighbouring threads should read neighbouring addresses. Then one delivery covers the whole warp. Sectors are 32 bytes. Efficiency = 32 × element size ÷ (sectors × 32). Stride s floats needs min(32, 4s) sectors. Row major: let threadIdx.x move along the row. Misaligned starts cost one extra sector. AoS to SoA turns stride 4 into stride 1. Transposes and column walks are fixed by staging a tile in shared memory. Nsight Compute reports \"sectors per request\" to check this. Hopper's TMA loads whole multi dimensional tiles with a single descriptor and handles strides and edges in hardware, which is how FlashAttention 3 keeps memory busy."],[9,"","Shared memory and bank conflicts","Shared memory use, barriers, 32 banks, gcd conflict rule, broadcast, padding and layout. · shared memory · banks · bank conflict · padding · __syncthreads Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 3: GPU memory›Shared memory Step 3 · Topic 3 of 3 · College Use shared memory as a fast scratch pad for a block, and learn how its 32 banks work, when lanes must wait for each other, and how padding fixes it. About 45 min4 lessons1 animation3 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Shared memory: a cache you control 2Banks and conflicts 3Padding and layout fixes 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[9,"shared","Shared memory: a cache you control","Each SM has a small, very fast memory that the threads of a block share. You declare it with __shared__, fill it from global memory, call __syncthreads() so everyone sees the full tile, and then read it many times. Unlike a normal cache, nothing is loaded or kept unless you write the code for it. reverse.cu #include <cstdio>// Reverse each block of 256 elements using a shared memory buffer__global__void reverseBlock(int *d, int n) {__shared__int s[256]; // one copy per block, on the SMint t = threadIdx.x;int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + t;if (i < n) s[t] = d[i]; // every thread loads one element__syncthreads(); // wait until the whole tile is loadedint src = blockDim.x - 1 - t;if (i < n) d[i] = s[src]; // read an element another thread loaded}int main() {constint n = 1024;int h[n], *d;for (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) h[k] = k;cudaMalloc(&d, n * sizeof(int));cudaMemcpy(d, h, n * sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice); reverseBlock<<<n / 256, 256>>>(d, n);cudaMemcpy(h, d, n * sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", h[0], h[255], h[256]);cudaFree(d);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 5__shared__ int s[256]; // one copy per block, on the SM256 ints = 1 KB of shared memory per block. Line 8if (i < n) s[t] = d[i]; // every thread loads one elementEach thread copies one element from slow global memory into the tile. Line 9__syncthreads(); // wait until the whole tile is loadedThe barrier: no thread continues until every thread of the block has reached this line. Line 10int src = blockDim.x - 1 - t;Thread t reads the element thread 255 − t wrote. Without the barrier it could read a value not loaded yet. What it prints 255 0 511 dynamic_shared.cu // Dynamic shared memory: the size is chosen at launch time__global__void scaleTile(float *d, float a) {extern__shared__float buf[]; // size comes from the third launch parameterint t = threadIdx.x, i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + t; buf[t] = d[i];__syncthreads(); d[i] = a * buf[t];}void launch(float *d, int n, int threads) {size_t bytes = threads * sizeof(float); scaleTile<<<n / threads, threads, bytes>>>(d, 2.0f);} What each marked line does and why Line 3extern __shared__ float buf[]; // size comes from the third launch parameterextern __shared__ with empty brackets means the size is decided at launch. Line 12scaleTile<<<n / threads, threads, bytes>>>(d, 2.0f);The third value inside <<< >>> is the number of dynamic shared memory bytes per block. Common mistake __syncthreads() must be reached by every thread of the block. Putting it inside an if that only some threads enter can hang the block or give wrong results. Lesson 2 of 4"],[9,"banks","Banks and conflicts","Shared memory is split into 32 banks, each 4 bytes wide. Each bank can serve one 4 byte word per tick. If the 32 lanes of a warp hit 32 different banks, the access takes one pass. If several lanes need different words in the same bank, they take turns. Bank of an addressExam favourite bank = ⌊byte address ÷ 4⌋ mod 32 Word w lives in bank w mod 32. Byte 260 is word 65, bank 1. Conflict for stride sExam favourite ways = gcd(s, 32) Odd strides are always conflict free. Broadcast same word for many lanes → no conflict One read is sent to every lane that asked for it. Real world scenario: 32 ticket windows A station has 32 ticket windows. If 32 travellers each go to a different window, everyone is served at once. If 8 travellers queue at window 0 for different tickets, the last one waits 8 turns. If 8 travellers all want the same newspaper, the clerk holds it up once and everyone reads it. Lesson 3 of 4"],[9,"padding","Padding and layout fixes","Workbook 11.2 worked example: thread t reads tile[t][0] of a 32 × 32 float tile Element [t][0] is word 32t, so bank (32t) mod 32 = 0 for every lane. 32 different words in bank 0: 32 passes. Declare the tile as [32][33]: element [t][0] is word 33t, bank t mod 32. 32 different banks, 1 pass. The cost is 32 × 33 × 4 − 32 × 32 × 4 = 128 bytes Workbook 11.3: 32 threads each store 32 powers Layout A writes A[32i + p]: at each p the lanes are 32 words apart, a 32-way conflict. 32 steps × 32 passes = 1024 passes. Layout B writes B[32p + i]: lanes 1 word apart, 1 pass per step. 32 passes, 32 times faster powers.cu // 32 threads, each computes 32 powers of its own number into shared memory__global__void powers(float *out, constfloat *x) {__shared__float A[32 * 32];__shared__float B[32 * 32];int i = threadIdx.x;float v = 1.0f;for (int p = 0; p < 32; ++p) { v *= x[i]; A[32 * i + p] = v; // layout A: lanes 32 words apart, every lane in bank 0 B[32 * p + i] = v; // layout B: lanes 1 word apart, one lane per bank }__syncthreads();for (int k = i; k < 32 * 32; k += 32) out[k] = A[k] + B[k];} What each marked line does and why Line 9A[32 * i + p] = v; // layout A: lanes 32 words apart, every lane in bank 0Layout A: thread i owns a row of 32 values, so at one moment all lanes write column p: same bank. Line 10B[32 * p + i] = v; // layout B: lanes 1 word apart, one lane per bankLayout B: the 32 lanes write one row together: 32 different banks. Line 12__syncthreads();Both tiles must be complete before they are copied out. Line 13for (int k = i; k < 32 * 32; k += 32) out[k] = A[k] + B[k];Copy out with consecutive lanes on consecutive words, which is also coalesced in global memory. Going deeper: 8 byte values A double covers two banks. Under the plain 32-bit model, lane t reading double t touches words 2t and 2t + 1, so 16 lanes fill the 32 banks and the warp needs 2 passes. Recent GPUs serve 64-bit accesses in two half warp phases, which gives the same 2 passes without any extra waiting. Lesson 4 of 4"],[9,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Shared memory is a fast shelf for a team. It has 32 doors. Spread the team over the doors, or let everyone read the same thing. bank = word mod 32, conflict = gcd(stride, 32). Pad 2-D tiles to an odd width. __syncthreads() between writing and reading a tile. Shared memory and L1 share one SRAM per SM. You can ask for a bigger shared carve out with cudaFuncSetAttribute and cudaFuncAttributePreferredSharedMemoryCarveout. More shared memory per block lowers occupancy (chapter 2.4). Swizzled layouts (XOR of row and column bits) avoid conflicts without wasting padding. CUTLASS and the Hopper TMA use them for matrix tiles."],[10,"","Floating point numbers on the GPU","IEEE-754 encoding, binary fractions, float addition, non associativity, FMA and reduced precision formats. · IEEE-754 · floating point · mantissa · exponent · FMA · FP16 · BF16 Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 4: Numbers and performance limits›Floating point Step 4 · Topic 1 of 2 · College See how a decimal number becomes 32 bits, why 5.6 cannot be stored exactly, how floats are added, and why a GPU sum can differ from a CPU sum. About 40 min5 lessons1 animation4 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Sign, exponent and mantissa 2Encoding by hand 3Adding floats, and why order matters 4Special values and precision on GPUs 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[10,"format","Sign, exponent and mantissa","A float is stored like scientific notation in base 2: a sign, a power of two, and the digits after the point. The leading 1 is not stored, which buys one extra bit for free. IEEE-754 valueExam favourite value = (−1)S × 1.f × 2E − bias bias = 127 for float (8 exponent bits), 1023 for double (11 bits). Binary fraction 0.b₁b₂b₃… = b₁ × ½ + b₂ × ¼ + b₃ × ⅛ + … 1.00101₂ = 1 + ⅛ + 1/32 = 1.15625. Exact or not a decimal fraction is exact in binary only if its denominator is a power of 2 0.625 = 5/8 is exact. 0.6 = 3/5 repeats for ever. Format Sign Exponent Mantissa About this many decimal digits FP64 double 1 11 52 16 FP32 float 1 8 23 7 TF32 (tensor cores) 1 8 10 3 BF16 1 8 7 2 to 3 FP16 half 1 5 10 3 How a decimal number is stored in IEEE-754Type a number and step: sign, binary, slide the point, biased exponent, mantissa bits, and what is really stored. Step 6 of 6 Number (type any decimal) Format float (FP32): value = (−1)sign × 1.mantissa × 2exponent − 127. This number fits exactly. Try this Workbook 5.1: 6.75 = 110.11 in binary = 1.1011 × 2², stored exponent 2 + 127 = 129. −0.375 = −1.1 × 2⁻². Sign 1, exponent 125, the word is 0xBEC00000. 5.6 has an endless binary fraction, so a float stores 5.599999904632568 instead. The same 5.6 in FP16 keeps only 10 mantissa bits: 5.6015625. BF16 keeps FP32's 8 exponent bits but only 7 mantissa bits: 5.59375. Common mistake The lecture 5 notes give 1.00101₂ = 1.5625. Adding the bits gives 1 + 0.125 + 0.03125 = 1.15625 = 37/32, which is the value the workbook uses. Lesson 2 of 5"],[10,"encode","Encoding by hand","Lecture 5 example: 34.625 as a float Integer part: 34 = 100010₂ (32 + 2). Fraction by doubling: 0.625 → 1.25 (1), 0.25 → 0.5 (0), 0.5 → 1.0 (1), so .101. 34.625 = 100010.101₂ = 1.00010101 × 2⁵. Exponent field 5 + 127 = 132 = 10000100₂. Mantissa 00010101 followed by zeros. 0 10000100 00010101000000000000000 = 0x420A8000 Why 5.6 is not exact 0.6 by doubling: 1.2 (1), 0.4 (0), 0.8 (0), 1.6 (1), then 0.6 again, so 0.1001 1001 1001… 5.6 = 1.011001100110… × 2², exponent field 129. Round to 23 bits: word 0x40B33333. The float holds 5.599999904632568 Machine epsilon ε = 2−mantissa bits FP32: 2⁻²³ ≈ 1.19 × 10⁻⁷. The gap between 1 and the next float. Relative rounding error is at most ε ÷ 2. Lesson 3 of 5"],[10,"arith","Adding floats, and why order matters","To add two floats the hardware lines up the exponents, adds the mantissas, normalises and rounds. Lining up shifts the smaller number right, and bits that fall off the end are lost. Homework: 5.75 + 0.375 5.75 = 101.11₂ = 1.0111 × 2². 0.375 = 0.011₂ = 1.1 × 2⁻². Align: shift 1.1 right by 2 − (−2) = 4 places, 0.00011 × 2². Add: 1.01110 + 0.00011 = 1.10001 × 2². 110.001₂ = 6.125 Homework: 6.5 − 4.25 6.5 = 1.101 × 2², 4.25 = 1.0001 × 2², already aligned. 1.1010 − 1.0001 = 0.1001 × 2². Normalise: 1.001 × 2¹ = 10.01₂ = 2.25 In the exam A GPU reduction adds numbers in a different order from a CPU loop, so float sums can differ in the last digits. That is expected. Compare float results with a tolerance, never with ==. fma.cu #include <cstdio>#include <cmath>__global__void fmaDemo(float *out, float a, float b, float c) { out[0] = __fmul_rn(a, b) + c; // multiply, round, then add and round again out[1] = fmaf(a, b, c); // fused: a * b + c with a single rounding}int main() {float *d, h[2];cudaMalloc(&d, 2 * sizeof(float));float a = 1.0f + 1.0f / 4096, c = -(1.0f + 1.0f / 2048); fmaDemo<<<1, 1>>>(d, a, a, c);cudaMemcpy(h, d, sizeof(h), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"separate: %.10g\\nfused: %.10g\\n\", h[0], h[1]);cudaFree(d);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 5out[0] = __fmul_rn(a, b) + c; // multiply, round, then add and round again__fmul_rn forces a separate multiply that is rounded before the add. Line 6out[1] = fmaf(a, b, c); // fused: a * b + c with a single roundingfmaf computes a × b + c exactly and rounds once. GPUs do this in one instruction, counted as 2 FLOPs. Line 12float a = 1.0f + 1.0f / 4096, c = -(1.0f + 1.0f / 2048);With a = 1 + 2⁻¹², a² = 1 + 2⁻¹¹ + 2⁻²⁴. The 2⁻²⁴ term is lost by the separate multiply but kept by the FMA. What it prints separate: 0 fused: 5.960464478e-08 Lesson 4 of 5"],[10,"special","Special values and precision on GPUs","Exponent field Mantissa Meaning 0 0 ±0 0 not 0 subnormal (tiny numbers, no hidden 1) 1 to 254 anything normal number 255 0 ±infinity (overflow, 1 ÷ 0) 255 not 0 NaN (0 ÷ 0, √−1) Double precision costs more on most GPUs. Fermi did 1 TFLOP/s single and 0.5 TFLOP/s double. Gaming GPUs run FP64 at 1/32 or 1/64 of FP32. Use double where accuracy needs it, for example long sums in scientific codes. Going deeper: Kahan summation Keep a small correction c of the bits lost at each step: y = x − c, t = s + y, c = (t − s) − y, s = t. The error stops growing with the number of terms. It costs 4 operations per element instead of 1. Lesson 5 of 5"],[10,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A float is a number in scientific notation with a fixed number of digits. Some numbers, like 0.1, cannot be written exactly, so tiny errors appear. value = (−1)^S × 1.f × 2^(E − bias). Float 1/8/23, bias 127. Align, add, normalise, round. Order changes sums. Mixed precision: store in FP16 or BF16, multiply on tensor cores, accumulate in FP32 (chapter 8.1). Watch overflow in FP16 (largest 65,504) and use loss scaling in training. FP8 (E4M3 and E5M2) on Hopper and FP4 on Blackwell use per block scale factors to keep the range. Deterministic reductions fix the summation order so results repeat bit for bit."],[11,"","FLOPs, bandwidth and the roofline","FLOPs and peak, bandwidth, arithmetic intensity, roofline and ridge point, reading benchmarks. · FLOPs · bandwidth · arithmetic intensity · roofline · ridge point · memory bound Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 4: Numbers and performance limits›Roofline Step 4 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Count the work and the bytes of a kernel, work out its arithmetic intensity, and use the roofline to predict its top speed and what will make it faster. About 50 min5 lessons1 animation11 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Counting the work: FLOPs 2Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity 3The roofline model 4Reading benchmark numbers (lecture 4 Sobel) 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[11,"flops","Counting the work: FLOPs","A FLOP is one floating point operation: an add, a multiply, or half of an FMA. FLOP/s (or FLOPS) is a rate. The lecture warned that slides mix the two, so always check the unit. Matrix multiply n × nExam favourite FLOPs = 2n³ Each of n² outputs needs n multiplies and n adds. Filter k × k FLOPs = 2k² × outputs A 5 × 5 filter on 1020 × 1020 outputs: 52,020,000. Peak of a GPUExam favourite peak = SMs × cores per SM × 2 (FMA) × clock A100: 108 × 64 × 2 × 1.41 GHz ≈ 19.5 TFLOP/s. Efficiency achieved ÷ peak A simple kernel at 3171 GFLOP/s on an H100 with about 51,000 is about 6.2 percent. Python # FLOPs of common kernelsn = 1024print(\"matmul n=1024:\", 2 * n ** 3, \"FLOPs\")print(\"5x5 filter, 1020x1020 outputs:\", 2 * 25 * 1020 * 1020)# A100 FP32 peak: 108 SMs x 64 cores x 2 FLOPs x 1.41 GHzprint(\"A100 peak GFLOP/s:\", round(108 * 64 * 2 * 1.41, 1))print(\"4096^3 matmul at peak (ms):\", round(2 * 4096 ** 3 / 19.5e12 * 1000, 2)) Output matmul n=1024: 2147483648 FLOPs 5x5 filter, 1020x1020 outputs: 52020000 A100 peak GFLOP/s: 19491.8 4096^3 matmul at peak (ms): 7.05 Lecture 5: mul_add loop x = a × x + b runs 50 times. Each iteration is 2 FLOPs. 100 FLOPs per call. In 1 second that is 100 FLOP/s Lesson 2 of 5"],[11,"ai","Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity","Every kernel both computes and moves data. If data cannot arrive fast enough, the cores sit idle however fast they are. The lecture's latency numbers for a CPU make the point: register 1 cycle, L1 4, L2 14, L3 75, DRAM 200. BandwidthExam favourite GB/s = (bytes read + bytes written) ÷ 10⁹ ÷ seconds Count both directions. Arithmetic intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes moved to or from DRAM Unit FLOP per byte. SAXPY AI = 2 ÷ 12 ≈ 0.167 Read x, read y, write y (3 × 4 bytes) for one multiply and one add. DAXPY is half that. Matrix multiply no reuse: 2 FLOPs per 2 loads → 0.25 (float), perfect reuse: AI = n ÷ 8 (float) or n ÷ 16 (double) Reuse is what raises AI. saxpy_bw.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void saxpy(int n, float a, constfloat *x, float *y) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i]; // 2 FLOPs, 12 bytes (read x, read y, write y)}int main() {constint n = 1 << 26;float *x, *y;cudaMalloc(&x, n * sizeof(float));cudaMalloc(&y, n * sizeof(float));cudaMemset(x, 0, n * sizeof(float));cudaMemset(y, 0, n * sizeof(float));cudaEvent_t t0, t1;cudaEventCreate(&t0); cudaEventCreate(&t1);cudaEventRecord(t0); saxpy<<<(n + 255) / 256, 256>>>(n, 2.0f, x, y);cudaEventRecord(t1);cudaEventSynchronize(t1);float ms = 0;cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, t0, t1);double bytes = 12.0 * n, flops = 2.0 * n; printf(\"bandwidth %.1f GB/s, %.1f GFLOP/s, AI %.3f\\n\", bytes / ms / 1e6, flops / ms / 1e6, flops / bytes);cudaFree(x); cudaFree(y);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 5if (i < n) y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i]; // 2 FLOPs, 12 bytes (read x, read y, write y)Each thread does 2 FLOPs and moves 12 bytes, so AI = 1/6 whatever the GPU. Line 17cudaEventRecord(t0);Events time the kernel alone on the GPU timeline. Line 24printf(\"bandwidth %.1f GB/s, %.1f GFLOP/s, AI %.3f\\n\", bytes / ms / 1e6, flops / ms / 1e6, flops / bytes);bytes ÷ ms ÷ 10⁶ gives GB/s. Compare it with the data sheet bandwidth to see how close you are. What it prints (the printed bandwidth depends on the GPU, AI is always 0.167) Kernel FLOPs per element Bytes per element (float) AI y = a·x 1 8 0.125 SAXPY y = a·x + y 2 12 0.167 dot product (double) 2 16 0.125 y = x³ + x 3 8 0.375 Lesson 3 of 5"],[11,"roofline","The roofline model","Attainable speedExam favourite attainable = min(peak FLOP/s, AI × bandwidth) The lower roof wins. Ridge pointExam favourite AIridge = peak ÷ bandwidth Left of it a kernel is memory bound, right of it compute bound. Time t ≥ max(FLOPs ÷ peak, bytes ÷ bandwidth) The shortest possible time is set by the slower of the two. Workbook 4.4: 10⁹ FLOPs and 2 × 10⁸ bytes on a 10 TFLOP/s, 500 GB/s GPU Maths time 10⁹ ÷ 10¹³ = 0.1 ms. Memory time 2 × 10⁸ ÷ 5 × 10¹¹ = 0.4 ms. The memory time is longer, so the kernel takes at least 0.4 ms. Speed 10⁹ ÷ 0.4 ms = 2.5 TFLOP/s, memory bound (AI 5 is below the ridge 20) In the exam For a memory bound kernel, doubling the maths units changes nothing. Only more bandwidth or more reuse helps. Doubling Fermi's cores gives plain matrix multiply no gain. ipynb Notebook: measure your own rooflineMeasure bandwidth and FP32 rate, then draw the roofline with three kernels on it.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 4 of 5"],[11,"bench","Reading benchmark numbers (lecture 4 Sobel)","Picture CPU (ms) GPU kernel (ms) Speedup Lena 512 × 512 13.59 0.188 about 72 Kid 47.27 0.336 about 141 8K frame (7680 × 4320, 1 byte per pixel): where does the time go? Frame in and out: 2 × 33.2 MB = 66.4 MB. At 13 to 16 GB/s that takes about 4.1 to 5.1 ms. The kernel itself is 1.65 ms of the 8.66 ms total, about 19 percent. The kernel moves 66.4 MB in 1.65 ms = 40.2 GB/s, only 15.7 percent of the GPU's 256 GB/s. Keep frames on the GPU: about 606 FPS, 5.25 times faster Going deeper: hierarchical roofline Nsight Compute draws one roof per memory level: L1, L2 and DRAM. A kernel can be far below the DRAM roof but at the L2 roof, which tells you that improving cache reuse, not DRAM traffic, is the next step. Lesson 5 of 5"],[11,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A kitchen with very fast cooks but a slow delivery van serves meals at the speed of the van. Only recipes that use each delivered ingredient many times keep the cooks busy. AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes. Ridge = peak ÷ bandwidth. Attainable = min(peak, AI × B). Below the ridge only bandwidth or reuse helps. Tiling raises AI by T (chapter 5.2). Fusing kernels removes the bytes of intermediate results. Lower precision halves bytes and raises tensor core peaks, which moves both roofs. Williams, Waterman and Patterson introduced the roofline in 2009. LLM decoding at batch 1 is deeply memory bound (AI near 1), which is why KV cache compression and speculative decoding matter."],[12,"","Parallel reduction","Tree reduction, pairing and divergence, barrier rule, grid level reductions, atomics and warp shuffles. · reduction · tree · atomicAdd · warp shuffle · __syncthreads · divergence Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 5: Parallel patterns›Reduction Step 5 · Topic 1 of 5 · College Add up millions of numbers in parallel with a tree, keep warps busy with the right pairing, avoid the barrier bug, and finish across blocks with atomics or warp shuffles. About 50 min5 lessons1 animation5 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1From a serial loop to a tree 2Which threads work: divergence and the barrier bug 3Beyond one block: launches and atomics 4Warp shuffles: the modern way 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[12,"tree","From a serial loop to a tree","Adding n numbers with one adder takes n − 1 steps, one after another. With many threads we can add pairs at the same time: n/2 sums, then n/4, and so on, until one value is left. That needs only log₂ n levels. LevelsExam favourite levels = ⌈log₂ n⌉ 1024 numbers need 10 levels instead of 1023 serial additions. AdditionsExam favourite total additions = n − 1 The tree does the same work, it only runs it in parallel. Barriers barriers = 1 (after loading) + levels A 1024 thread tree uses 11 __syncthreads(). Tree reduction: adding a block of numbers in log₂ n stepsEach level, half of the remaining threads add a partner's value. Watch which threads work and how the warps split. Step 3 of 3 Numbers (up to 32, padded with 0 to a power of two) Pairing Warp size to draw (real GPUs use 32) n = 8: 3 levels, 7 additions in total (one adder alone would need 7 steps). Level 3: 1 threads add, they sit in 1 warp of 4 lanes, 1 split (some lanes idle). Result: 31. Dotted lines mark warp borders. There is a __syncthreads() between levels. Try this Workbook 10.2: [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6] gives 31 after 3 levels (step 4, then 2, then 1). Interleaved pairing: the working threads are spread out, so every warp stays split (divergent) at every level. Sequential pairing on 16 numbers: working threads are packed at the front, so whole warps go idle early and only the last levels split. Python # Tree reduction with sequential addressing on 8 numbersa = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]n = len(a)stride = n // 2level = 0while stride > 0: level += 1for t inrange(stride): # threads t < stride work a[t] += a[t + stride]print(\"level\", level, \"stride\", stride, \"->\", a[:stride]) stride //= 2print(\"sum =\", a[0]) Output level 1 stride 4 -> [8, 10, 6, 7] level 2 stride 2 -> [14, 17] level 3 stride 1 -> [31] sum = 31 Real world scenario: a knockout tournament 64 teams play a knockout. In round one 32 matches happen at the same time, then 16, 8, 4, 2 and 1. Six rounds find the winner, even though 63 matches are played in total. A tree reduction is a knockout where each match adds two numbers. Lesson 2 of 5"],[12,"warps","Which threads work: divergence and the barrier bug","The pairing decides how many warps split. With interleaved pairing (thread t works if t mod 2s = 0) the working threads are spread out, so every warp keeps some idle lanes. With sequential pairing (thread t works if t < s) the working threads sit together, so whole warps finish and only the last 5 levels (s = 16 to 1) split a warp. Workbook 12.1 worked example: 1024 threads, first stride 512, barrier inside the if At stride 512 threads 0 to 511 work: warps 0 to 15 fully busy, warps 16 to 31 fully idle. No warp splits at this level. But the __syncthreads() is inside if (t < stride), so threads 512 to 1023 never reach it. The CUDA rules say every thread of the block must reach the same barrier, otherwise the behaviour is undefined. Move the barrier out of the if bad_sum.cu // WRONG: the barrier sits inside the if, so threads with t >= stride never reach it__global__void badSum(float *s) {int t = threadIdx.x;for (int stride = blockDim.x / 2; stride > 0; stride >>= 1) {if (t < stride) { s[t] += s[t + stride];__syncthreads(); // undefined behaviour: not all threads of the block arrive } }} What each marked line does and why Line 5if (t < stride) {Only threads with t below stride enter this block. Line 7__syncthreads(); // undefined behaviour: not all threads of the block arriveA barrier that some threads skip can hang the block or let threads race ahead. Lesson 3 of 5"],[12,"grid","Beyond one block: launches and atomics","A block can only sum its own values. For a big array each block writes one partial sum. Then either a second launch adds the partial sums, or each block adds its result to the total with one atomicAdd. Launches without atomics repeat: n ← ⌈n ÷ B⌉ until n = 1 1,000,000 numbers with B = 256: 3907, then 16, then 1, so 3 launches. Atomics neededExam favourite one per block = ⌈n ÷ B⌉ 2²⁰ numbers with B = 1024: 1024 atomics instead of 1,048,576. block_sum.cu #include <cstdio>// Sum of n floats. Each block reduces 2 x blockDim values, then one atomicAdd per block.__global__void blockSum(constfloat *in, float *total, int n) {extern__shared__float s[];int t = threadIdx.x;int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x * 2 + t;float v = 0.0f;if (i < n) v += in[i];if (i + blockDim.x < n) v += in[i + blockDim.x]; // each thread adds two values first s[t] = v;__syncthreads();for (int stride = blockDim.x / 2; stride > 0; stride >>= 1) {if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride]; // sequential addressing: no bank conflicts__syncthreads(); // outside the if: every thread reaches it }if (t == 0) atomicAdd(total, s[0]); // one atomic per block}int main() {constint n = 1 << 20, B = 256;float *d, *dt, h = 0.0f;cudaMalloc(&d, n * sizeof(float));cudaMalloc(&dt, sizeof(float));float *ones = newfloat[n];for (int k = 0; k < n; ++k) ones[k] = 1.0f;cudaMemcpy(d, ones, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);cudaMemset(dt, 0, sizeof(float));int blocks = (n + 2 * B - 1) / (2 * B); blockSum<<<blocks, B, B * sizeof(float)>>>(d, dt, n);cudaMemcpy(&h, dt, sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"sum = %.0f using %d blocks\\n\", h, blocks);delete[] ones; cudaFree(d); cudaFree(dt);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 5extern __shared__ float s[];Dynamic shared memory, one float per thread, size given by the third launch value. Line 10if (i + blockDim.x < n) v += in[i + blockDim.x]; // each thread adds two values firstThread coarsening: each thread adds two inputs before the tree, so half as many blocks are needed. Line 14if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride]; // sequential addressing: no bank conflictsSequential addressing: the active threads stay packed at the front. Line 15__syncthreads(); // outside the if: every thread reaches itThe barrier is outside the if, so every thread reaches it every level. Line 17if (t == 0) atomicAdd(total, s[0]); // one atomic per blockOne atomicAdd per block makes the final total without a second launch. Line 30blockSum<<<blocks, B, B * sizeof(float)>>>(d, dt, n);Each block covers 2 × 256 = 512 values. What it prints sum = 1048576 using 2048 blocks Lesson 4 of 5"],[12,"shfl","Warp shuffles: the modern way","Inside one warp, threads can read each other's registers directly with __shfl_down_sync. No shared memory and no barrier are needed, because the 32 lanes of a warp step together when the mask names them all. warp_sum.cu // Warp level sum with shuffles: no shared memory, no __syncthreads inside a warp__inline__ __device__float warpSum(float v) {for (int off = 16; off > 0; off >>= 1) v += __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, v, off); // read v from lane + offreturn v; // lane 0 holds the warp total}__global__void sumShfl(constfloat *in, float *total, int n) {__shared__float warpTotals[32];int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;float v = (i < n) ? in[i] : 0.0f; v = warpSum(v);int lane = threadIdx.x % 32, w = threadIdx.x / 32;if (lane == 0) warpTotals[w] = v;__syncthreads();if (w == 0) { v = (lane < blockDim.x / 32) ? warpTotals[lane] : 0.0f; v = warpSum(v);if (lane == 0) atomicAdd(total, v); }} What each marked line does and why Line 3for (int off = 16; off > 0; off >>= 1)Offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, 1: five steps for 32 lanes. Line 4v += __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, v, off); // read v from lane + offLane i adds the value held by lane i + off. The mask 0xffffffff says all 32 lanes take part. Line 14if (lane == 0) warpTotals[w] = v;After warpSum, lane 0 of each warp holds that warp's total. Line 17v = (lane < blockDim.x / 32) ? warpTotals[lane] : 0.0f;Warp 0 then reduces the (at most 32) warp totals the same way. Remember Floating point addition is not associative (chapter 4.1), so the tree order gives a slightly different float sum from a CPU loop. Compare with a tolerance. Going deeper: CUB and Thrust For production code, cub::DeviceReduce::Sum and thrust::reduce choose the block size, coarsening and shuffles for you and are tuned for each GPU. Writing your own is how you learn what they do. ipynb Notebook: parallel reductionAtomic sum, interleaved tree and sequential tree, all checked against NumPy.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[12,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Adding numbers in pairs, then pairs of sums, finishes in a few rounds, like a knockout tournament. log₂ n levels, n − 1 additions, barriers outside ifs, sequential addressing, one atomic per block, shuffles inside warps. The classic NVIDIA reduction sequence: interleaved, sequential addressing, first add during load, unroll the last warp, complete unrolling, many elements per thread. Each step removes a bottleneck and the last versions reach close to full memory bandwidth. A reduction reads every byte once with AI near 0.25, so it is memory bound. The target is the bandwidth roof. Decoupled look back (Merrill and Garland) does single pass scans and reductions across the whole grid."],[13,"","Tiled matrix multiplication","Plain against tiled loads, Quiz 1 Q2 to Q4, two barriers, ragged edges and tile limits. · tiled matmul · shared memory · phases · Quiz 1 · __syncthreads · ragged edges Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 5: Parallel patterns›Tiled matmul Step 5 · Topic 2 of 5 · College Cut global memory traffic by a factor of T by loading tiles of A and B into shared memory, get the two barriers right, and handle matrices that are not a multiple of the tile size. About 55 min5 lessons1 animation9 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The plain kernel and its traffic 2Tiles in shared memory 3Two barriers per phase: Quiz 1 question 4 4Ragged edges and tile size limits 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[13,"naive","The plain kernel and its traffic","One thread computes one element of C = A × B. It walks along a row of A and down a column of B, so it loads 2N values from global memory to do N multiply-adds. Every value of A is loaded N times in total, once by each thread in its row of C. matmul_naive.cu __global__void matMulNaive(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (row < N && col < N) {float sum = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N; k++) sum += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // 2 global loads per multiply-add C[row * N + col] = sum; }} What each marked line does and why Line 2int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;y picks the row of C, x picks the column, as in chapter 2.2. Line 7sum += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // 2 global loads per multiply-addA[row × N + k] walks along a row. B[k × N + col] walks down a column, but neighbouring threads have neighbouring col, so that read is coalesced across the warp. Line 8C[row * N + col] = sum;One store per thread. Loads per thread (plain)Exam favourite 2N N from A and N from B. Loads per block (plain)Exam favourite threads per block × 2N Quiz 1 Q2: 256 × 2048 = 524,288. Total loads (plain) 2N³ Not N² as lecture 7 slide 8 says. For N = 1024 that is 2048 times more. Common mistake Lecture 7 slide 8 states the plain kernel reads Width² values. Each of the Width² threads reads 2 × Width values, so the total is 2 × Width³. Lesson 2 of 5"],[13,"tiles","Tiles in shared memory","A block of T × T threads computes a T × T tile of C. In each phase it loads one T × T tile of A and one of B into shared memory, each thread loading one value of each. Then every thread does T multiply-adds from shared memory. Each loaded value is used T times. PhasesExam favourite phases = ⌈N ÷ T⌉ N = 1024, T = 16: 64 phases. Loads per thread (tiled)Exam favourite 2 × ⌈N ÷ T⌉ One A value and one B value per phase. Total loads (tiled)Exam favourite 2N²⌈N ÷ T⌉ = 2N³ ÷ T when T divides N Global traffic drops by a factor of T. Arithmetic intensity AI ≈ T ÷ 4 (float) Plain matmul has 0.25. T = 16 gives 4. Quiz 1 Q3: N = 1024, 16 × 16 tiles, loads per block 256 threads per block, 64 phases. Each thread loads 1 value of A and 1 of B per phase: 2 × 64 = 128. 256 × 128. 32,768 loads per block, 16 times fewer than 524,288 Lesson 3 of 5"],[13,"sync","Two barriers per phase: Quiz 1 question 4","The tiled loop needs two __syncthreads() per phase. The first makes sure the whole tile is in shared memory before anyone reads it. The second makes sure everyone has finished reading before the next phase overwrites it. quiz1_q4.cu // Quiz 1 question 4: the tiled kernel WITHOUT barriers (wrong on purpose)__global__void matMul(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {__shared__float tileA[16][16];__shared__float tileB[16][16];int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;float sum = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N / 16; k++) { tileA[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = A[row * N + (k * 16 + threadIdx.x)]; tileB[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = B[(k * 16 + threadIdx.y) * N + col];for (int t = 0; t < 16; t++) sum += tileA[threadIdx.y][t] * tileB[t][threadIdx.x]; } C[row * N + col] = sum;} What each marked line does and why Line 9tileA[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = A[row * N + (k * 16 + threadIdx.x)];Each thread writes one element of each tile. Line 12sum += tileA[threadIdx.y][t] * tileB[t][threadIdx.x];Thread (5, 10) reads tileA[10][t] for t = 0 to 15, including tileA[10][3], which thread (3, 10) writes. Without a barrier it may read before that write. Line 13}No barrier here either, so a fast thread can start the next phase and overwrite tileB while slower threads still read it. Rakesh's worked solution Statement a: thread (5, 10) can start the inner loop before thread (5, 3) has written its value. With no barrier nothing stops it, so a can happen: true. Statement b: thread (3, 12) can overwrite tileB[3][12] for the next k while another thread is still reading it. Also possible: true. Both true: answer 3. The fix is the two barriers in matmul_tiled.cu matmul_tiled.cu #define T 16// Works for any N: tiles that hang over the edge are padded with zeros__global__void matMulTiled(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {__shared__float As[T][T];__shared__float Bs[T][T];int tx = threadIdx.x, ty = threadIdx.y;int row = blockIdx.y * T + ty;int col = blockIdx.x * T + tx;float sum = 0.0f;for (int p = 0; p < (N + T - 1) / T; ++p) {int ka = p * T + tx, kb = p * T + ty; As[ty][tx] = (row < N && ka < N) ? A[row * N + ka] : 0.0f; Bs[ty][tx] = (kb < N && col < N) ? B[kb * N + col] : 0.0f;__syncthreads(); // tile fully loaded before anyone reads itfor (int k = 0; k < T; ++k) sum += As[ty][k] * Bs[k][tx];__syncthreads(); // everyone done before the next phase overwrites it }if (row < N && col < N) C[row * N + col] = sum;} What each marked line does and why Line 4__global__ void matMulTiled(const float *A, const float *B, float *C, int N) {This version also handles N that is not a multiple of T. Line 11for (int p = 0; p < (N + T - 1) / T; ++p) {⌈N ÷ T⌉ phases. Line 13As[ty][tx] = (row < N && ka < N) ? A[row * N + ka] : 0.0f;Out of range elements are loaded as 0, so they add nothing to the sum. Line 15__syncthreads(); // tile fully loaded before anyone reads itBarrier 1: the tile must be complete before it is read (statement a). Line 18__syncthreads(); // everyone done before the next phase overwrites itBarrier 2: nobody may overwrite the tile while others still read it (statement b). Line 20if (row < N && col < N) C[row * N + col] = sum;Only threads inside C write their result. Lesson 4 of 5"],[13,"ragged","Ragged edges and tile size limits","Workbook 7.1: n = 5 with 4 × 4 tiles Phases and blocks per side: ⌈5 ÷ 4⌉ = 2, so 2 × 2 = 4 blocks. Threads 4 × 16 = 64, of which 64 − 25 = 39 do no output. Reads tiled 2 × 25 × 2 = 100, plain 2 × 125 = 250. 2.5 times fewer reads Shared memory for two tiles bytes = 2 × T² × 4 T = 32: 8 KB. 48 KB allows T up to 78. Thread limit T² ≤ 1024 So T ≤ 32 when each thread computes one output. With T = 32 on an A100 (19.5 TFLOP/s, 1555 GB/s) a 4096 matrix still needs about 11 ms for memory and 7 ms for maths: memory bound. Faster kernels give each thread several outputs (chapter 8.3). ipynb Notebook: naive and tiled matrix multiplyBoth kernels in Numba with a count of global reads and a GFLOP/s timing.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[13,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Instead of every student walking to the library for every book, a group borrows a shelf of books once, and everyone in the group reads them at the table. Plain: 2N per thread, 2N³ total. Tiled: 2⌈N/T⌉ per thread, 2N³/T total. Two __syncthreads per phase. T ≤ 32 for one output per thread. Register tiling: each thread computes a 4 × 4 or 8 × 8 block of C, which raises AI far beyond T/4. Double buffering loads the next tile while computing the current one. CUTLASS splits the work in a hierarchy: thread block tiles, warp tiles, and tensor core fragments, with cp.async or TMA pipelines. cuBLAS reaches 80 to 90 percent of peak this way."],[14,"","Stencils, halos and heat simulation","Stencil patterns, halos, reads per output, 3-D tile intensity, heat step stability. · stencil · halo · heat equation · stability · 3-D · shared memory Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 5: Parallel patterns›Stencils Step 5 · Topic 3 of 5 · College Update every point from its neighbours, load each tile once with a halo around it, and keep a heat simulation stable. About 45 min5 lessons1 animation10 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a stencil is 2Halos in shared memory 33-D tiles: why big tiles still stay memory bound 4The heat equation and stability 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[14,"what","What a stencil is","A stencil updates every point from a fixed pattern of neighbours. Blurs, edge filters, heat and wave simulations, and the Game of Life are all stencils. Every output can be computed at the same time, so one thread per output works well. Stencil Points Example 1-D radius R 2R + 1 moving sum, R = 3 gives 7 2-D plus (radius 1) 5 heat in a plate 2-D box radius r (2r + 1)² blur, r = 2 gives 25 3-D plus (radius 1) 7 heat in a block 3-D box radius 1 27 smoothing a volume Neighbours in 2-DExam favourite N = i − W, S = i + W, W = i − 1, E = i + 1 Row major with width W. Pixel (row 10, col 20) of a 640 wide image is 6420. Neighbours in 3-DExam favourite ± 1, ± N, ± N² Index i = (z × N + y) × N + x. Real world scenario: passing a rumour Each pupil in a classroom grid listens to the four pupils next to them and repeats an average of what they heard. After a few rounds the news has spread. Every pupil can listen at the same moment, which is why a GPU suits it. Lesson 2 of 5"],[14,"halo","Halos in shared memory","Neighbouring outputs share most of their inputs. A block loads its B inputs into shared memory once, plus R extra values on each side called the halo, then every thread reads its 2R + 1 inputs from the fast tile. Tile sizeExam favourite B + 2R values R = 3, B = 256: 262. Global reads per outputExam favourite (B + 2R) ÷ B with the tile, 2R + 1 without R = 3, B = 256: 1.023 against 7, about 6.8 times fewer. stencil1d.cu #define R 3#define B 256// out[i] = sum of in[i - R] .. in[i + R]. The input is padded with R values on each side.__global__void stencil1d(constint *in, int *out) {__shared__int tile[B + 2 * R];int g = blockIdx.x * B + threadIdx.x + R; // position in the padded inputint s = threadIdx.x + R; // position in the tile tile[s] = in[g];if (threadIdx.x < R) { tile[s - R] = in[g - R]; // left halo tile[s + B] = in[g + B]; // right halo }__syncthreads();int sum = 0;for (int k = -R; k <= R; ++k) sum += tile[s + k]; out[g - R] = sum;} What each marked line does and why Line 7int g = blockIdx.x * B + threadIdx.x + R; // position in the padded inputg skips the R padding values at the start of the input. Line 9tile[s] = in[g];Every thread loads its own centre value. Line 10if (threadIdx.x < R) {The first R threads also load the halos, R on the left and R on the right. Line 14__syncthreads();Wait until the whole tile, halos included, is in shared memory. Line 17sum += tile[s + k];All 2R + 1 reads come from shared memory. Lesson 3 of 5"],[14,"3d","3-D tiles: why big tiles still stay memory bound","Without tiling AI = 13 ÷ 28 ≈ 0.46 7 multiplies and 6 adds, 7 floats loaded. With T × T × T tilesExam favourite AI(T) = 13 × (T − 2)³ ÷ (4T³) Only the inner (T − 2)³ points make outputs. Halo shareExam favourite (T³ − (T − 2)³) ÷ T³ T = 8: 57.8 percent of loaded points are border. AI can never pass 13 ÷ 4 = 3.25, and T = 8 already uses 512 threads. With one thread per point, a 3-D stencil stays memory bound on every modern GPU. Real codes use 2-D tiles that march through z, keeping three planes in registers. Lesson 4 of 5"],[14,"heat","The heat equation and stability","3-D explicit heat stepExam favourite Tnew = (1 − 6λ) T + λ × (sum of 6 neighbours) λ = α Δt ÷ h². Weights add to 1, so a constant field stays constant. StabilityExam favourite λ ≤ 1/6 in 3-D, 1/4 in 2-D, 1/2 in 1-D Above it the centre weight is negative and values oscillate and grow. Largest time step Δt ≤ h² ÷ (6α) α = 0.0001, h = 0.01 gives Δt ≤ 0.167 s. Common mistake Lecture 6 slide 14 uses 0.25 for the centre and 0.1 for each of the 6 neighbours. They add to 0.85, so a field of 100 drops to 85 every step. With neighbour weight 0.1 the centre must be 1 − 6 × 0.1 = 0.4. heat3d.cu // One explicit step of 3-D heat: Tnew = (1 - 6L) T + L (sum of 6 neighbours)__global__void heat3d(constfloat *T, float *Tn, int N, float L) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int z = blockIdx.z * blockDim.z + threadIdx.z;if (x < 1 || y < 1 || z < 1 || x >= N - 1 || y >= N - 1 || z >= N - 1) return;int i = (z * N + y) * N + x; // x fastest, then y, then zfloat nb = T[i - 1] + T[i + 1] + T[i - N] + T[i + N] + T[i - N * N] + T[i + N * N]; Tn[i] = (1.0f - 6.0f * L) * T[i] + L * nb; // stable only when L <= 1/6}void run(float *dA, float *dB, int N, float L, int steps) {dim3 blk(8, 8, 8), grd((N + 7) / 8, (N + 7) / 8, (N + 7) / 8);for (int s = 0; s < steps; ++s) { heat3d<<<grd, blk>>>(dA, dB, N, L);float *t = dA; dA = dB; dB = t; // ping-pong: new becomes old }} What each marked line does and why Line 7int i = (z * N + y) * N + x; // x fastest, then y, then zx is the fastest index, so a warp reads neighbouring x values: coalesced. Line 8float nb = T[i - 1] + T[i + 1] + T[i - N] + T[i + N] + T[i - N * N] + T[i + N * N];The six neighbours: x ± 1, y ± 1 (± N) and z ± 1 (± N²). Line 9Tn[i] = (1.0f - 6.0f * L) * T[i] + L * nb; // stable only when L <= 1/6Weights 1 − 6L and L add to 1. Line 16float *t = dA; dA = dB; dB = t; // ping-pong: new becomes oldPing-pong: swap the pointers so the new grid becomes the old one. No copy is needed. ipynb Notebook: a 3 × 3 stencil on an imageThe blur kernel in this notebook is a stencil. Count its global reads and compare with a tiled version.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[14,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Each point looks at its neighbours and updates. Load a patch once with a border around it, and let everyone read the patch. Tile B + 2R, reads (B + 2R)/B against 2R + 1. 3-D AI = 13(T − 2)³/(4T³) ≤ 3.25. Heat stable for λ ≤ 1/6 in 3-D. 2.5-D blocking keeps three z planes in registers and marches up the grid. Temporal blocking does several time steps per load, trading extra halo for less traffic. Weather, seismic and CFD codes are mostly stencils. Implicit schemes avoid the stability limit by solving a linear system each step, which brings in sparse solvers and multigrid."],[15,"","Convolution and Sobel edge detection","Convolution sizes and work, Sobel, constant memory filters, halo tiles and savings. · convolution · Sobel · filter · halo tile · constant memory · padding Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 5: Parallel patterns›Convolution Step 5 · Topic 4 of 5 · College Compute 2-D convolutions by hand and on the GPU, detect edges with Sobel, put the filter in constant memory, and cut global reads with halo tiles. About 50 min4 lessons1 animation7 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Convolution: slide, multiply, add 2Sobel edge detection (lecture 4) 3Halo tiles for convolution 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[15,"conv","Convolution: slide, multiply, add","A 2-D convolution slides a small grid of weights (the filter or kernel) over a picture. At each place it multiplies the weights by the pixels under them and adds the products. One GPU thread computes one output pixel. 2-D convolution by hand: slide, multiply, addOne GPU thread computes one output. Step to move the filter one place and see its multiply-adds. Step 16 of 16 Picture (5 × 5) Filter Zero padding (keeps the size) Output size = 5 − 2 + 1 = 4. 4 multiply-adds per output, 64 for the whole picture. Output (3, 3) = 1 × 19 + -1 × 25 = -6. Try this Workbook 8.2: every output of the filter with rows (1, 0) and (0, −1) on 1 to 25 is −6, because the picture is a smooth slope. Box filter on 1 to 25: the first output is 1 + 2 + 6 + 7 = 16. Sobel Gx on an edge: large values where dark meets bright, 0 on flat parts. Workbook 8.2: sharpen with zero padding. The middle 40 stays 40, the corner 10 becomes 15 and the corner 70 becomes 225, because the padding counts as 0. Output sizeExam favourite out = ⌊(W + 2P − K) ÷ S⌋ + 1 W input width, K filter size, P padding, S stride. \"Same\" padding P = (K − 1) ÷ 2 3 × 3 needs 1, 5 × 5 needs 2. WorkExam favourite multiply-adds = out² × K² × Cin × Cout AlexNet layer 1: 55² × 11² × 3 × 96 = 105,415,200. Python # Output size and work of a convolution layerdef out_size(W, K, P=0, S=1):return (W + 2 * P - K) // S + 1print(\"AlexNet layer 1:\", out_size(227, 11, 0, 4))o = out_size(227, 11, 0, 4)print(\"multiply-adds:\", o * o * 96 * 11 * 11 * 3)print(\"224, 3x3, pad 1, stride 2:\", out_size(224, 3, 1, 2)) Output AlexNet layer 1: 55 multiply-adds: 105415200 224, 3x3, pad 1, stride 2: 112 Common mistake Lecture 8 sharpens the pixel 30 at (2, 1) to 35. With neighbours 25, 35, 20 and 40 the result is 5 × 30 − 120 = 30. On a smooth slope sharpening changes nothing. Lesson 2 of 4"],[15,"sobel","Sobel edge detection (lecture 4)","Sobel uses two 3 × 3 filters. Gx responds to changes from left to right, Gy to changes from top to bottom. A flat area gives 0, an edge gives a big value. Edge strengthExam favourite M1 = |Sx| + |Sy|, M2 = √(Sx² + Sy²) M1 is cheaper and is what the lecture code uses. Both are clamped to 255. sobel.cu #include <cstdlib>__constant__int GX[9] = {-1, 0, 1, -2, 0, 2, -1, 0, 1};__constant__int GY[9] = {-1, -2, -1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 2, 1};__global__void sobel(constunsignedchar *in, unsignedchar *out, int w, int h) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (x < 1 || y < 1 || x >= w - 1 || y >= h - 1) return;int sx = 0, sy = 0;for (int dy = -1; dy <= 1; ++dy)for (int dx = -1; dx <= 1; ++dx) {int p = in[(y + dy) * w + (x + dx)];int k = (dy + 1) * 3 + (dx + 1); sx += GX[k] * p; sy += GY[k] * p; }int m = abs(sx) + abs(sy); // M1 = |Sx| + |Sy|, cheaper than a square root out[y * w + x] = m > 255 ? 255 : m; // clamp to the 8 bit range} What each marked line does and why Line 3__constant__ int GX[9] = {-1, 0, 1, -2, 0, 2, -1, 0, 1};The two filters live in constant memory: every thread reads the same weight at the same moment, so it is broadcast. Line 9if (x < 1 || y < 1 || x >= w - 1 || y >= h - 1) return;Border pixels have no full 3 × 3 neighbourhood, so they are skipped. Line 13int p = in[(y + dy) * w + (x + dx)];Row major neighbour read. A warp reads 32 neighbouring x values, so it is coalesced. Line 18int m = abs(sx) + abs(sy); // M1 = |Sx| + |Sy|, cheaper than a square rootM1 = |Sx| + |Sy|. Line 19out[y * w + x] = m > 255 ? 255 : m; // clamp to the 8 bit rangeClamp because the output is one byte. Picture CPU GPU kernel Speedup Lena 512 × 512 13.59 ms 0.188 ms about 72 Kid 47.27 ms 0.336 ms about 141 8K frame not given 1.65 ms (8.66 ms with copies) copies are 81 percent Lesson 3 of 4"],[15,"tiles","Halo tiles for convolution","Neighbouring outputs share most of their inputs, as with stencils. A block of B × B threads loads a (B + 2r) × (B + 2r) tile into shared memory once, then each thread reads its K × K inputs from the tile. Tile sideExam favourite B + 2r, with r = (K − 1) ÷ 2 B = 32, 3 × 3: 34 × 34. Loads per outputExam favourite (B + 2r)² ÷ B² Against K² without a tile. B = 16, 5 × 5: 1.5625 against 25. Saving 1 − (B + 2r)² ÷ (K² B²) Best possible for large B: 1 − 1/K². 3 × 3 gives 88.9 percent. Workbook 8.3: N = 1024, B = 16, 3 × 3 Plain: 9 reads per output × 1024² = 9,437,184. Blocks 64 × 64 = 4096, each loads 18 × 18 = 324. Tiled: 4096 × 324 = 1,327,104. 85.9 percent fewer reads conv_tiled.cu #define BS 16#define R 1__constant__float W[(2 * R + 1) * (2 * R + 1)];// Each block loads a (BS + 2R) x (BS + 2R) tile, then computes BS x BS outputs__global__void convTiled(constfloat *in, float *out, int w, int h) {__shared__float tile[BS + 2 * R][BS + 2 * R];int ox = blockIdx.x * BS, oy = blockIdx.y * BS; // top left output of this blockfor (int i = threadIdx.y; i < BS + 2 * R; i += BS)for (int j = threadIdx.x; j < BS + 2 * R; j += BS) {int gx = ox + j - R, gy = oy + i - R; tile[i][j] = (gx >= 0 && gy >= 0 && gx < w && gy < h) ? in[gy * w + gx] : 0.0f; }__syncthreads();int x = ox + threadIdx.x, y = oy + threadIdx.y;if (x >= w || y >= h) return;float s = 0.0f;for (int a = 0; a <= 2 * R; ++a)for (int b = 0; b <= 2 * R; ++b) s += W[a * (2 * R + 1) + b] * tile[threadIdx.y + a][threadIdx.x + b]; out[y * w + x] = s;} What each marked line does and why Line 4__constant__ float W[(2 * R + 1) * (2 * R + 1)];Filter weights in constant memory, filled by the host with cudaMemcpyToSymbol. Line 8__shared__ float tile[BS + 2 * R][BS + 2 * R];The tile is (BS + 2R) wide, so the block has fewer threads than tile cells. Line 10for (int i = threadIdx.y; i < BS + 2 * R; i += BS)The loops let 16 × 16 threads cover an 18 × 18 tile: some threads load two values. Line 13tile[i][j] = (gx >= 0 && gy >= 0 && gx < w && gy < h) ? in[gy * w + gx] : 0.0f;Outside the picture we load 0 (zero padding). Line 15__syncthreads();All of the tile must be loaded before any thread reads its neighbours. Line 21s += W[a * (2 * R + 1) + b] * tile[threadIdx.y + a][threadIdx.x + b];K × K reads from shared memory instead of global memory. Lesson 4 of 4"],[15,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Slide a small stamp across a picture, multiply and add at each place. Sobel finds edges by comparing one side with the other. out = (W + 2P − K)/S + 1. Work out² K² Cin Cout. Tile (B + 2r)², loads per output (B + 2r)²/B². Weights in constant memory. Deep learning convolutions are turned into matrix multiplies (im2col, chapter 8.2) or use Winograd and FFT methods, so tensor cores can run them. cuDNN picks the fastest algorithm per layer. Depthwise separable convolutions (chapter 1.3) cut the work by about K² and are memory bound. Implicit GEMM builds the im2col matrix on the fly in shared memory instead of storing it."],[16,"","Scan, compaction and histograms","Scans, work efficiency, compaction, histograms with privatised atomics. · scan · prefix sum · Blelloch · stream compaction · histogram · atomics Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 5: Parallel patterns›Scan and histogram Step 5 · Topic 5 of 5 · College Build running totals in log₂ n parallel steps, use them to filter lists without clashes, and count values into bins with atomics that do not queue up. About 45 min5 lessons1 animation4 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Prefix sums: inclusive and exclusive 2A block scan in CUDA 3What scans are for: compaction 4Histograms and atomic contention 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[16,"scan","Prefix sums: inclusive and exclusive","A scan (prefix sum) turns a list into running totals. It looks serial, because each total needs the one before it, but it can be done in log₂ n parallel steps. Inclusive scanExam favourite yi = x0 + x1 + … + xi [3, 1, 7, 0] → [3, 4, 11, 11]. Exclusive scanExam favourite yi = x0 + … + xi−1, y0 = 0 [3, 1, 7, 0] → [0, 3, 4, 11]. The inclusive scan shifted right by one. Parallel prefix sum (scan)Every output is the sum of all inputs before it. Step through a parallel scan level by level. Step 3 of 3 Numbers (up to 16, padded with 0 to a power of two) Algorithm 8 numbers. Hillis and Steele does 17 additions in 3 levels (more work than the 7 of a serial loop, but only 3 steps). Scans are used for stream compaction, radix sort and allocating output space in parallel. Try this Hillis and Steele: at level d every element adds the one 2ᵈ to its left. 3 levels for 8 numbers. Blelloch: build partial sums up a tree, clear the last one, then sweep down swapping and adding. Result is exclusive: 0, 3, 4, 11, 11, 15, 16, 22. Python # Inclusive and exclusive scan, serial and Hillis-Steelea = [3, 1, 7, 0, 4, 1, 6, 3]inc, s = [], 0for v in a: s += v inc.append(s)exc = [0] + inc[:-1]print(\"inclusive\", inc)print(\"exclusive\", exc)x, off, adds = a[:], 1, 0while off < len(x): x = [x[i] + (x[i - off] if i >= off else0) for i inrange(len(x))] adds += len(x) - off off *= 2print(\"Hillis-Steele\", x, \"adds\", adds) Output inclusive [3, 4, 11, 11, 15, 16, 22, 25] exclusive [0, 3, 4, 11, 11, 15, 16, 22] Hillis-Steele [3, 4, 11, 11, 15, 16, 22, 25] adds 17 Algorithm Steps Additions Note Serial loop n − 1 n − 1 one thread Hillis and Steele log₂ n n log₂ n − (n − 1) simple, extra work Blelloch 2 log₂ n about 2(n − 1) work efficient, exclusive Lesson 2 of 5"],[16,"code","A block scan in CUDA","scan_block.cu // Inclusive Hillis and Steele scan of one block (n = blockDim.x <= 1024), double buffered__global__void scanBlock(constint *in, int *out, int n) {extern__shared__int buf[]; // 2 * n ints: two halves we swap betweenint t = threadIdx.x, src = 0, dst = n; buf[t] = (t < n) ? in[t] : 0;__syncthreads();for (int off = 1; off < n; off <<= 1) {int v = buf[src + t];if (t >= off) v += buf[src + t - off]; // add the value off places to the left buf[dst + t] = v;__syncthreads();int tmp = src; src = dst; dst = tmp; // swap halves, never read and write one array }if (t < n) out[t] = buf[src + t];} What each marked line does and why Line 3extern __shared__ int buf[]; // 2 * n ints: two halves we swap betweenTwo halves of shared memory. A step reads one half and writes the other. Line 7for (int off = 1; off < n; off <<= 1) {off = 1, 2, 4, …: log₂ n steps. Line 9if (t >= off) v += buf[src + t - off]; // add the value off places to the leftThreads with t < off have nothing to the left at this distance and keep their value. Line 11__syncthreads();Every thread must write before anyone reads the next step. Line 12int tmp = src; src = dst; dst = tmp; // swap halves, never read and write one arraySwapping halves avoids the race where a thread reads a value its neighbour already overwrote in this step. Common mistake Updating one array in place with buf[t] += buf[t − off] is a race: thread t may read buf[t − off] after thread t − off has already added to it. Double buffering or a barrier between the read and the write fixes it. Going deeper: scans larger than one block Scan each block, write each block's total to an array, scan that array, then add each block's scanned total to all its elements. CUB's DeviceScan does it in a single pass with decoupled look back. Lesson 3 of 5"],[16,"uses","What scans are for: compaction","Filtering a list on the GPU is hard because each thread does not know where to write its kept value. An exclusive scan of 0/1 flags gives every kept value its output position, so all threads can write at the same time without clashing. Use How the scan helps Stream compaction positions of kept items Radix sort positions of keys with bit 0 and bit 1 Sparse matrices (CSR) row start offsets from row lengths Allocating variable output each thread's write offset Lesson 4 of 5"],[16,"hist","Histograms and atomic contention","A histogram counts how many values fall in each bin. Many threads may hit the same bin at once, so the increment must be an atomic operation. When many threads hit one address, the atomics are done one after another (contention). Global atomics without privatisation one atomic per element n = 10⁶ bytes → 10⁶ global atomics, all on 256 addresses. Privatised (shared memory) histogramExam favourite global atomics = blocks × bins at most Each block counts in fast shared memory, then merges once. histogram.cu #define BINS 256// Histogram of bytes: private copy per block in shared memory, then merge__global__void histogram(constunsignedchar *data, int n, unsignedint *hist) {__shared__unsignedint local[BINS];for (int b = threadIdx.x; b < BINS; b += blockDim.x) local[b] = 0;__syncthreads();for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x)atomicAdd(&local[data[i]], 1u); // fast shared memory atomic__syncthreads();for (int b = threadIdx.x; b < BINS; b += blockDim.x)if (local[b]) atomicAdd(&hist[b], local[b]); // one global atomic per bin per block} What each marked line does and why Line 5__shared__ unsigned int local[BINS];A private histogram for this block in shared memory. Line 6for (int b = threadIdx.x; b < BINS; b += blockDim.x) local[b] = 0;Clear it with a block stride loop: 256 bins and any number of threads. Line 8for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x)Grid stride loop over the data (chapter 2.2). Line 9atomicAdd(&local[data[i]], 1u); // fast shared memory atomicShared memory atomics are much cheaper than global ones, and only threads of this block compete. Line 12if (local[b]) atomicAdd(&hist[b], local[b]); // one global atomic per bin per blockMerge: at most 256 global atomics per block instead of one per element. Real world scenario: a class vote Asking 1000 pupils to walk to one ballot box makes a long queue. Letting each class count its own votes, then sending one sheet per class to the head teacher, removes the queue. ipynb Notebook: histograms and the Blelloch scanGlobal against private histograms, and a full up sweep and down sweep scan.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[16,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Running totals and tallies. With many helpers the totals can be built in a few rounds, and each class can count its own votes first. Inclusive and exclusive scan, Hillis and Steele (log n steps, n log n work), Blelloch (2 log n steps, 2n work). Histogram: private shared memory copy, then one global atomic per bin per block. Bank conflicts in Blelloch are removed with padding. Warp scans use __shfl_up_sync. Histograms with very skewed data (one hot bin) suffer most contention, aggregating in registers first helps. Single pass scans with decoupled look back (Merrill and Garland 2016) run at memory bandwidth. Onesweep radix sort builds on them and is used in CUB."],[17,"","Warp divergence and warp scheduling","Divergence cost, Quiz 1 Q9, branch free tricks, warp schedulers, hiding waits. · warp divergence · SIMT · Quiz 1 · round robin · GTO · latency hiding Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 6: Execution and overlap›Divergence Step 6 · Topic 1 of 2 · College See what happens when lanes of a warp disagree at a branch, solve Quiz 1 question 9, and compare round robin with greedy then oldest scheduling tick by tick. About 45 min5 lessons2 animations6 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Warp divergence 2Quiz 1 question 9 3Warp schedulers 4How many warps hide the wait? 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[17,"div","Warp divergence","All lanes of a warp share one instruction stream. At an if where lanes disagree, the warp runs the then path with the other lanes switched off (masked), then the else path with the first group masked. Both paths cost time for the whole warp. Warp timeExam favourite time = sum of the lengths of every path at least one lane takes Paths no lane takes are skipped. EfficiencyExam favourite efficiency = (sum over lanes of their path length) ÷ (32 × warp time) 8 lanes on a 10 tick path and 24 on a 30 tick path: 800 ÷ 1280 = 62.5 percent. Worst case 32 different paths: 32 × L, efficiency 1/32 = 3.1 percent A 32 times slowdown. Warp divergence: when lanes of one warp take different pathsA warp issues one instruction for all its lanes. If lanes disagree at a branch, the warp runs each path in turn and masks the lanes that did not take it. Step 4 of 4 Branch Threads in the block32 K (for tid < K)40 Ticks for path A (case 0)10 Ticks for every other path10 if (tid % 4 == 1) A else B (Quiz 1 Q9). A warp takes the sum of the lengths of every path any of its lanes needs. Efficiency = useful lane ticks ÷ (32 × warp ticks). Sum of thread ids taking A: 120. Try this Quiz 1 Q9: lanes 1, 5, 9, …, 29 take branch A. Their ids add up to 120. The warp runs both branches. Workbook 7.3: tid % 2 with 10 ticks each: 20 ticks instead of 10, efficiency 50 percent. Branch on the warp number: whole warps go the same way, no warp diverges. if (tid < 40) in a 64 thread block: only warp 1 splits. A 4-way switch: 40 ticks, 25 percent. Python # Time and efficiency of one warp: each path any lane takes costs its full lengthdef warp_cost(path_of_lane, length): used = set(path_of_lane) time = sum(length[p] for p in used) useful = sum(length[p] for p in path_of_lane)return time, round(100 * useful / (32 * time), 1)L = {\"A\": 10, \"B\": 10}print(\"tid % 2 :\", warp_cost([\"A\"if t % 2 == 0else\"B\"for t inrange(32)], L))print(\"tid % 4==1:\", warp_cost([\"A\"if t % 4 == 1else\"B\"for t inrange(32)], L))print(\"8 vs 24 :\", warp_cost([\"A\"] * 8 + [\"B\"] * 24, {\"A\": 10, \"B\": 30}))print(\"Q9 sum of ids in A:\", sum(t for t inrange(32) if t % 4 == 1)) Output tid % 2 : (20, 50.0) tid % 4==1: (20, 50.0) 8 vs 24 : (40, 62.5) Q9 sum of ids in A: 120 Remember Divergence only happens inside a warp. If whole warps take the same side, as with if ((tid / 32) % 2 == 0), there is no divergence cost at all. Lesson 2 of 5"],[17,"q9","Quiz 1 question 9","Rakesh's worked solution One warp of 32 threads, tid = 0 to 31. Branch A is taken when tid % 4 == 1: tid = 1, 5, 9, 13, 17, 21, 25, 29 (8 lanes). This is an arithmetic series: 8 terms, first 1, last 29. Sum = 8 × (1 + 29) ÷ 2 = 8 × 15. 120 quiz1_q9.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void addArrays(constfloat *a, constfloat *b, float *c, int N) {int tid = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (tid < N) {if (tid % 4 == 1) c[tid] = a[tid] - b[tid]; // Branch A: lanes 1, 5, 9, ..., 29else c[tid] = a[tid] + b[tid]; // Branch B: the other 24 lanes }}int main() {constint N = 32;float h[N], ha[N], hb[N], *a, *b, *c;for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) { ha[i] = i + 1; hb[i] = 2 * (i + 1); }cudaMalloc(&a, sizeof(ha)); cudaMalloc(&b, sizeof(hb)); cudaMalloc(&c, sizeof(h));cudaMemcpy(a, ha, sizeof(ha), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice);cudaMemcpy(b, hb, sizeof(hb), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice); addArrays<<<1, 32>>>(a, b, c, N);cudaMemcpy(h, c, sizeof(h), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"c[1] = %.0f, c[2] = %.0f\\n\", h[1], h[2]);cudaFree(a); cudaFree(b); cudaFree(c);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 6if (tid % 4 == 1)Inside one warp, 8 lanes say yes and 24 say no, so the warp diverges. Line 7c[tid] = a[tid] - b[tid]; // Branch A: lanes 1, 5, 9, ..., 29Branch A runs first with 24 lanes masked off. Line 9c[tid] = a[tid] + b[tid]; // Branch B: the other 24 lanesThen branch B runs with the 8 lanes of A masked off. Line 16for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) { ha[i] = i + 1; hb[i] = 2 * (i + 1); }a = 1 to 32 and b = 2 to 64, as in the quiz. What it prints c[1] = -2, c[2] = 9 branch_free.cu // Same result without a branch: every lane runs the same instructions__global__void addOrSub(constfloat *a, constfloat *b, float *c, int N) {int tid = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (tid >= N) return;float sign = (tid % 4 == 1) ? -1.0f : 1.0f; // compiles to a select, not a jump c[tid] = a[tid] + sign * b[tid];}// Divergence only between warps, never inside one__global__void perWarp(float *x, int N) {int tid = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (tid >= N) return;if ((tid / 32) % 2 == 0) x[tid] *= 2.0f; // whole warps choose the same pathelse x[tid] += 1.0f;} What each marked line does and why Line 5float sign = (tid % 4 == 1) ? -1.0f : 1.0f; // compiles to a select, not a jumpA select picks −1 or +1 without jumping. Every lane runs the same two instructions. Line 13if ((tid / 32) % 2 == 0) x[tid] *= 2.0f; // whole warps choose the same pathtid / 32 is the warp number, so all 32 lanes of a warp agree and nothing is masked. Going deeper: predication For very short branches the compiler often uses predicated instructions: every lane computes both results and keeps the one its predicate selects. No jump happens, so the cost is just the extra instructions. Lesson 3 of 5"],[17,"sched","Warp schedulers","Each SM sub partition has a warp scheduler that issues one instruction per tick from a warp that is ready. Which ready warp it picks changes how well waiting is hidden. Policy Rule Strength Weakness Round robin (RR) take turns in order fair all warps reach their loads together and wait together Greedy then oldest (GTO) keep issuing the same warp until it stalls, then the oldest ready one spreads loads out, good cache locality less fair, a warp can starve Two level RR inside a small active group, switch group when all stall between the two needs tuning of the group size Rules (workbook 7.4) one instruction per tick, a load at tick t lets the warp go again at t + L finish = tick of the last load + L. Best possible finishExam favourite finish ≥ (total instructions − 1) + L 4 warps × 12 instructions, L = 12: 47 + 12 = 59. GTO reaches it. Lesson 4 of 5"],[17,"hide","How many warps hide the wait?","If each warp does c ticks of work and then waits W ticks, other warps must fill the gap. While one warp waits, the others each need c ticks of their own work. Warps to hide a waitExam favourite warps = ⌈(W + c) ÷ c⌉ Work 1 tick, wait 3: 4 warps. One warp leaves 75 percent idle, two leave 50 percent. Remember More independent instructions per warp (unrolling, ILP) cut the warps needed: c from 10 to 20 at W = 400 saves 20 warps. ipynb Notebook: warps, divergence, races and atomicsMeasure a divergent branch and sum a warp with shuffles.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[17,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A group of 32 must walk together. If some want to go left and some right, the group visits both places one after the other. Warp time = sum of used path lengths. Efficiency = lane ticks ÷ 32 × time. Q9: ids 1, 5, …, 29 sum to 120. GTO beats RR when loads dominate. Independent thread scheduling (Volta and later) keeps a program counter per thread, so diverged lanes can interleave, but they still issue separately. Reconverge with __syncwarp() before warp level operations. Research schedulers (cache conscious wavefront scheduling, CAWS) choose warps to protect L1 locality. Dynamic warp formation regroups lanes from different warps that take the same path."],[18,"","Streams, asynchrony and overlap","Async calls, streams, overlap speedup, pinned memory, events and graphs. · streams · cudaMemcpyAsync · pinned memory · overlap · events · pipeline Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 6: Execution and overlap›Streams Step 6 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Learn which CUDA calls make the CPU wait, split work into streams so copies and kernels run at the same time, and work out the speedup of a pipeline. About 40 min4 lessons1 animation3 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Who waits for whom 2Overlapping copies and compute 3Events: timing and dependencies 4From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 4"],[18,"async","Who waits for whom","A kernel launch only puts work in a queue and returns at once. The CPU can do other things while the GPU runs. Some calls make the CPU wait. Call Blocks the CPU? Notes kernel<<>>() no queued in the default stream cudaMemcpy yes starts after earlier work in the stream, returns when the copy is done cudaMemcpyAsync no needs pinned host memory to really overlap cudaStreamSynchronize(s) yes until stream s is empty cudaDeviceSynchronize() yes until all GPU work is done Work inside one stream runs in order. Work in different streams may run at the same time, on different engines: the copy engines move data over PCIe while the SMs run kernels. Common mistake In class it was said that asynchrony is dangerous because a second kernel could overwrite the first. Work in one stream always runs in order. The real danger is the CPU reading or reusing a host buffer before the GPU has finished with it. Synchronise before reading results. Lesson 2 of 4"],[18,"overlap","Overlapping copies and compute","Split the data into chunks and give each chunk its own stream. While one chunk computes, the next can be copied in and the previous copied out. SerialExam favourite t = N × (tin + tkernel + tout) One stream, nothing overlaps. Overlapped, equal stages tExam favourite t = (N + 2) × t, speedup 3N ÷ (N + 2) N = 4: 2 times. N = 16: 2.67. Never more than 3 (the number of stages). Unequal stages t ≈ fill + N × slowest stage The slowest stage sets the pace. 2, 5, 2 ms with 10 chunks: 2 + 50 + 2 = 54 ms. streams.cu #include <cstdio>#define NS 4__global__void scale(float *d, int n, float a) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) d[i] *= a;}int main() {constint N = 1 << 22, chunk = N / NS;constsize_t cbytes = chunk * sizeof(float);float *h, *d;cudaMallocHost(&h, N * sizeof(float)); // pinned: needed for async copiescudaMalloc(&d, N * sizeof(float));for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) h[i] = 1.0f;cudaStream_t s[NS];for (int k = 0; k < NS; ++k) cudaStreamCreate(&s[k]);for (int k = 0; k < NS; ++k) {int off = k * chunk;cudaMemcpyAsync(d + off, h + off, cbytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, s[k]); scale<<<(chunk + 255) / 256, 256, 0, s[k]>>>(d + off, chunk, 2.0f);cudaMemcpyAsync(h + off, d + off, cbytes, cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost, s[k]); }cudaDeviceSynchronize(); // wait for all streams before reading h printf(\"h[0] = %.1f, h[N-1] = %.1f\\n\", h[0], h[N - 1]);for (int k = 0; k < NS; ++k) cudaStreamDestroy(s[k]);cudaFreeHost(h); cudaFree(d);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 13cudaMallocHost(&h, N * sizeof(float)); // pinned: needed for async copiesPinned memory: cudaMemcpyAsync from pageable memory would not overlap. Line 18for (int k = 0; k < NS; ++k) cudaStreamCreate(&s[k]);Four independent queues. Line 22cudaMemcpyAsync(d + off, h + off, cbytes, cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, s[k]);Each chunk is copied in, scaled and copied out in its own stream, so chunks can overlap. Line 23scale<<<(chunk + 255) / 256, 256, 0, s[k]>>>(d + off, chunk, 2.0f);The fourth launch value names the stream. Line 26cudaDeviceSynchronize(); // wait for all streams before reading hWait for every stream before the CPU reads h. What it prints h[0] = 2.0, h[N-1] = 2.0 ipynb Notebook: streams and overlapChunked copies and kernels on 4 streams, compared with the pipeline formula.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 3 of 4"],[18,"events","Events: timing and dependencies","An event is a marker placed in a stream. It records when the GPU reached that point. Two events give an elapsed time. Another stream can wait for an event without stopping the CPU. events.cu // Make stream b wait for work in stream a, without blocking the CPUvoid dependent(cudaStream_t a, cudaStream_t b, float *x, float *y, int n) {cudaEvent_t done;cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&done, cudaEventDisableTiming);cudaMemcpyAsync(y, x, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyDeviceToDevice, a);cudaEventRecord(done, a); // marks the point in stream acudaStreamWaitEvent(b, done, 0); // later work in b starts only after that pointcudaEventDestroy(done);} What each marked line does and why Line 4cudaEventCreateWithFlags(&done, cudaEventDisableTiming);DisableTiming makes a lighter event when it is only used for ordering. Line 6cudaEventRecord(done, a); // marks the point in stream acudaEventRecord places the marker after the copy in stream a. Line 7cudaStreamWaitEvent(b, done, 0); // later work in b starts only after that pointWork queued in b after this call waits for the marker. The CPU continues straight away. Going deeper: CUDA graphs Each launch costs a few microseconds of CPU time. For many small kernels, capture the sequence once into a CUDA graph and replay it with one call. The driver then knows all dependencies in advance. Workbook 6.3: 2²⁰ floats over 4 streams at 12 GB/s Chunk = 2²⁰ ÷ 4 = 262,144 floats = 1 MiB. Blocks of 256: 262,144 ÷ 256 = 1024. Copy time 1,048,576 bytes ÷ 12 × 10⁹ B/s. ≈ 87 µs per chunk copy Lesson 4 of 4"],[18,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A laundry with a washer, a dryer and a folding table. While load 2 washes, load 1 dries. Three machines working at once finish many loads almost 3 times faster. Streams are in order queues. Overlap needs pinned memory and separate streams. Equal stages: (N + 2)t, speedup ≤ 3. Synchronise before reading results. Check overlap in Nsight Systems: copies and kernels should appear on separate rows at the same time. Watch for implicit syncs from cudaMalloc, cudaFree or the legacy default stream. Large training jobs overlap gradient all reduce (NCCL) with the backward pass using streams. Programmatic dependent launch on Hopper starts the next kernel before the previous one fully ends."],[19,"","Race conditions and locks","Races, the three lock rules, Dekker, Peterson, Bakery and Filter. · race condition · critical section · Dekker · Peterson · Bakery · Filter lock Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 7: Synchronisation›Races and locks Step 7 · Topic 1 of 2 · College See why one line of code can lose updates, learn the three rules any lock must follow, and step through Dekker, Peterson, Bakery and Filter locks. About 45 min5 lessons2 animations3 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong 2The critical section and Dekker's three attempts 3Peterson's algorithm and why it is correct 4Locks for N threads: Bakery and Filter 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[19,"race","Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong","To you it is one line. To the hardware it is three steps: load the value into a register, add 1 to the register, store the register back. When two threads do this at the same time, their steps can mix. Time Thread 1 Thread 2 counter in memory 1 load, r1 = 0 0 2 load, r2 = 0 0 3 r1 = 1 0 4 r2 = 1 0 5 store 1 1 6 store 1 1, but it should be 2 A race condition is when the result depends on the timing of threads that share data and at least one of them writes. Step through it yourself. Change the number of threads and the order, and see how many updates are lost. counter = counter + 1 is three steps: load, add, storeSeveral threads add 1 to one counter. Step through one micro step at a time and watch updates get lost. Step 6 of 6 Threads2 How they run Seed for the random order7 L = load into a register, + = add 1 in the register, S = store back, a = atomicAdd. Expected 2, got 1: 1 update lost. A thread stored a value computed from an old load. Try this Lecture 10 table: both threads load 0, both store 1. The counter ends at 1 instead of 2. Workbook 10.1: 10 threads of one warp in lock step all load 0, so the result is 1 and 9 updates are lost (8 threads shown here). Try different seeds: with 3 threads the result can be 1, 2 or 3, only about 0.4 percent of orders give 3. atomicAdd makes the three steps indivisible: always correct. Orders of n threads with s steps eachExam favourite (n × s)! ÷ (s!)n Pick which thread takes each time slot. Steps inside one thread keep their order. Two threads, three steps 6! ÷ (3! × 3!) = 20 Only the 2 fully serial orders give 2. That is 10 percent. Three threads, three stepsExam favourite 9! ÷ (3!)3 = 1680 Only 3! = 6 serial orders give 3. That is about 0.4 percent. Python # How many ways can the steps of several threads interleave?from math import factorialdef orders(steps): total = factorial(sum(steps))for s in steps: total //= factorial(s)return totalprint(\"2 threads x 3 steps:\", orders([3, 3]))print(\"3 threads x 3 steps:\", orders([3, 3, 3]))print(\"3 steps and 4 steps:\", orders([3, 4]))correct = factorial(3) # only the 3! fully serial orders give 3print(\"chance of 3 with 3 threads:\", round(100 * correct / orders([3, 3, 3]), 2), \"%\") Output 2 threads x 3 steps: 20 3 threads x 3 steps: 1680 3 steps and 4 steps: 35 chance of 3 with 3 threads: 0.36 % Inside a GPU warp the threads run in lock step: all load, then all add, then all store. Every thread loads the same old value, so the result is just 1. Python # Workbook 10.1: 10 threads of one warp run counter = counter + 1 in lock stepn = 10counter = 0regs = [counter for _ inrange(n)] # step 1: every thread loads 0regs = [r + 1for r in regs] # step 2: every thread adds 1for r in regs: # step 3: every thread stores 1 counter = rprint(\"result:\", counter, \" lost updates:\", n - counter) Output result: 1 lost updates: 9 counter.cu #include <cstdio>__global__void unsafe_inc(int *c) { *c = *c + 1; // load, add, store: three separate steps}__global__void safe_inc(int *c) {atomicAdd(c, 1); // one indivisible read, add and write}int main() {int *d, h;cudaMalloc(&d, sizeof(int));cudaMemset(d, 0, sizeof(int)); unsafe_inc<<<1, 10>>>(d);cudaMemcpy(&h, d, sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"unsafe: %d\\n\", h);cudaMemset(d, 0, sizeof(int)); safe_inc<<<1, 10>>>(d);cudaMemcpy(&h, d, sizeof(int), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost); printf(\"atomic: %d\\n\", h);cudaFree(d);return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 3__global__ void unsafe_inc(int *c) {Every thread reads the same counter, adds 1 and writes back. Updates are lost. Line 4*c = *c + 1; // load, add, store: three separate stepsThis single line becomes a load, an add and a store. Line 8atomicAdd(c, 1); // one indivisible read, add and writeatomicAdd does the read, the add and the write as one step that nobody can split. Line 15unsafe_inc<<<1, 10>>>(d);One warp of 10 threads. In lock step they all read 0. Line 19safe_inc<<<1, 10>>>(d);Same launch with the atomic version. What it prints unsafe: 1 atomic: 10"],[19,"race","Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong","Common mistake The unsafe result is not guaranteed to be 1. It depends on how the hardware schedules the threads. A race means the answer is unpredictable, which is worse than always wrong. Lesson 2 of 5"],[19,"rules","The critical section and Dekker's three attempts","A critical section is the piece of code that touches the shared data. Only one thread may run it at a time. Any correct solution must meet three rules. Rule Meaning If it breaks Mutual exclusion At most one thread is inside at any moment. Two threads change the data together. Progress If the section is free and someone wants in, someone gets in. A thread that does not want in cannot block others. Deadlock, or waiting for a thread that has gone home. Bounded waiting After I ask, others can go first only a limited number of times. Starvation: I wait for ever. Think of one bathroom at a roadside restaurant. Never two people inside. If it is empty and someone waits, they go in. Nobody waits for ever while others keep jumping the queue. Lecture 10 tries three simple ideas for two threads. Each uses busy waiting: a while loop that spins until it may go on. Pick an attempt below and choose a schedule of 0s and 1s (which thread takes the next step). Attempt Idea Mutual exclusion Progress What goes wrong 1. Take turns a shared turn variable yes no Strict alternation. If thread 2 stops wanting in, thread 1 enters once and then waits for ever. 2. Check, then set look at the other flag, then raise mine no yes Both check before either sets. Both enter. 3. Set, then check raise my flag, then look yes no Both raise flags, then each waits for the other. Deadlock. In the exam Slide 8 says attempt 2 can deadlock. It cannot: a thread sets its flag only after passing its loop, so nobody waits after the flags are set. The real flaw is that both can pass the check, which breaks mutual exclusion. The class also concluded that the three attempts suffer from the same drawback. They fail in three different ways: attempt 2 checks too early, attempt 3 claims too early, attempt 1 is safe but too rigid. The fix combines the flags of attempt 3 (safety) with the turn variable of attempt 1 (a tie breaker). Dekker found the first correct version in the 1960s. dekker.cu volatilebool flag[2] = {false, false};volatileint turn = 0;void dekker_enter(int me) { // me is 0 or 1int other = 1 - me; flag[me] = true; // I want inwhile (flag[other]) { // the other wants in tooif (turn != me) { // and it is not my turn flag[me] = false; // step backwhile (turn != me) ; // wait for my turn flag[me] = true; // try again } }}void dekker_leave(int me) { turn = 1 - me; // give the turn away flag[me] = false;} What each marked line does and why Line 6flag[me] = true; // I want inRaise my flag first, as in attempt 3. Line 7while (flag[other]) { // the other wants in tooSomeone else also wants in, so there is a tie to break. Line 8if (turn != me) { // and it is not my turnThe turn variable breaks the tie, as in attempt 1. Line 9flag[me] = false; // step backLower my flag so the other thread can go in. This removes the deadlock of attempt 3. Line 10while (turn != me) ; // wait for my turnSpin until the turn comes back to me. Line 17turn = 1 - me; // give the turn awayOn the way out, hand the turn to the other thread. Lesson 3 of 5"],[19,"peterson","Peterson's algorithm and why it is correct","Peterson (1981) found a shorter correct lock for two threads. Each thread says \"I want in\" and then \"but you go first\". Whoever said \"you go first\" last is the one who waits. peterson.cu __device__volatileint flag[2] = {0, 0};__device__volatileint turn = 0;__device__void peterson_enter(int me) {int other = 1 - me; flag[me] = 1; // 1. I want in turn = other; // 2. but you go first__threadfence(); // both writes visible before the reads belowwhile (flag[other] && turn == other) ; // 3. wait only if the other wants in and it is its turn}__device__void peterson_leave(int me) {__threadfence(); // writes made inside the critical section go first flag[me] = 0; // 4. done} What each marked line does and why Line 6flag[me] = 1; // 1. I want inStep 1: raise my flag. Line 7turn = other; // 2. but you go firstStep 2: give the turn to the other thread. Both threads write turn, and the last writer waits. Line 8__threadfence(); // both writes visible before the reads belowA fence stops the hardware from letting the read below go ahead of the two writes above. Line 9while (flag[other] && turn == other) ; // 3. wait only if the other wants in and it is its turnStep 3: wait only while the other wants in and it is the other thread's turn. Line 13__threadfence(); // writes made inside the critical section go firstEverything written in the critical section must be visible before I lower my flag. Line 14flag[me] = 0; // 4. doneStep 4: lower my flag on the way out. Proof of mutual exclusion Suppose both threads are inside. Each wrote its flag, then turn. Say P0 wrote turn last, so turn = 1 from then on. P1 set flag[1] before it wrote turn, which was before P0 wrote turn. So when P0 tested, flag[1] was true and turn was 1. Then P0 would still be waiting. Contradiction. The other case is the same with 0 and 1 swapped. At most one thread inside Progress. If P1 does not want in, flag[1] is false and P0 walks straight in. If both want in, turn is 0 or 1, so exactly one of the two loop conditions is false. Somebody always gets in. Bounded waiting. If P1 leaves and tries again at once, it first writes turn = 0, which makes itself wait and lets P0 go. So P0 waits for at most one turn of P1. In the exam In class the conclusion was that the deadlock danger is still looming. That is not correct. With reads and writes that happen in program order, Peterson meets all three rules. Swapping steps 1 and 2 (turn first, then flag) does break mutual exclusion, so the order matters. Going deeper: Why volatile is not enough on real hardware volatile only stops the compiler from keeping the variable in a register. Modern CPUs and GPUs may still reorder memory operations. A store can sit in a store buffer while a later load runs ahead. Then each thread reads the other flag as false before its own flag is visible, and both enter. You need a fence between the writes and the read: __threadfence() in CUDA, or std::atomic with sequential consistency in C++. In practice programmers use hardware atomic instructions, which is the next chapter. Lesson 4 of 5"],[19,"nlocks","Locks for N threads: Bakery and Filter","Peterson works for two threads. Lecture 11 shows two ways to handle N threads. Lamport's Bakery lock. Like the ticket machine at a bakery counter: take a ticket one larger than any ticket in use and wait until every smaller ticket has been served. Two threads can pick the same number at the same moment, so equal tickets are broken by thread id. Filter lock. N − 1 gates in a row. At each gate the thread that arrived last (the victim) waits while anyone else is at that gate or further. Each gate holds back at least one thread, so after N − 1 gates only one remains. locks_n.cu #define N 4volatilebool choosing[N];volatileint number[N];void bakery_enter(int i) { choosing[i] = true;int m = 0;for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) if (number[j] > m) m = number[j]; number[i] = m + 1; // a ticket one larger than any in use choosing[i] = false;for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) {while (choosing[j]) ; // j is still picking its ticketwhile (number[j] != 0 && (number[j] < number[i] || (number[j] == number[i] && j < i))) ; }}void bakery_leave(int i) { number[i] = 0; }volatileint level[N];volatileint victim[N];staticbool someone_at(int i, int k) {for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)if (j != i && level[j] >= k) returntrue;returnfalse;}void filter_enter(int i) {for (int k = 1; k < N; k++) { // N - 1 gates level[i] = k; // I am at gate k victim[k] = i; // I arrived last at this gatewhile (someone_at(i, k) && victim[k] == i) ; }}void filter_leave(int i) { level[i] = 0; } What each marked line does and why Line 6choosing[i] = true;choosing[i] says that thread i is in the middle of picking a ticket. Line 9number[i] = m + 1; // a ticket one larger than any in useTake a ticket one larger than every ticket in use. Line 12while (choosing[j]) ; // j is still picking its ticketDo not compare with j while j is still picking. Its ticket might end up smaller than mine. Line 13while (number[j] != 0 &&Wait while j holds a smaller ticket, or the same ticket with a smaller id. Line 18void bakery_leave(int i) { number[i] = 0; }Leaving: throw the ticket away. Line 31level[i] = k; // I am at gate kI am now trying to pass gate k. Line 32victim[k] = i; // I arrived last at this gateI arrived last at gate k, so I am the one who waits if needed. Line 33while (someone_at(i, k) && victim[k] == i) ;Wait while someone else is at gate k or beyond and I am still the victim. Filter: at most N − k threads get past gate k Gate 0: at most N threads, which is true. Assume at most N − k + 1 threads reach gate k − 1. Suppose all of them pass gate k. Let A be the last of them to write victim[k]. Every other one has level ≥ k, so A must wait. Contradiction. So at most N − k pass gate k. At the last gate k = N − 1 that is 1 thread. Mutual exclusion for N threads Lock Threads Fairness Cost per entry Peterson 2 waits at most one turn constant Bakery N first come, first served O(N), tickets grow without limit Filter N starvation free, but no fixed bound O(N²) checks in the worst case In the exam Slide 3 says the Filter lock has bounded waiting. It is starvation free (every waiting thread gets in in the end), but a thread can be overtaken any number of times at a gate, so there is no fixed bound r. The Bakery lock is the one with first come, first served order. ipynb Notebook: a race you can seeA plain counter loses updates on a GPU, an atomic counter does not.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 5"],[19,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Two people write the score on one whiteboard. Each reads 5, adds 1 and writes 6. One point is lost. They need a rule so that only one person holds the marker at a time. counter++ is load, add, store. A critical section needs mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting. Dekker attempt 1 fails progress, 2 fails mutual exclusion, 3 deadlocks. Peterson and Bakery meet all three. Prove locks by looking at who wrote a shared variable last. Check small cases by trying every interleaving, as the notes did (62 states for Peterson). On real hardware add fences or use atomics. Model checkers such as TLA+ and SPIN explore every state of a lock. Weak memory models (C++, PTX) need careful proofs. Real systems use hardware compare and swap, ticket locks and queue locks such as MCS."],[20,"","Barriers, atomics and GPU locks","Barriers, atomics, CAS and ticket locks, privatisation, priority locks, grid sync. · __syncthreads · atomicCAS · atomicAdd · privatisation · cooperative groups · grid.sync Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 7: Synchronisation›Barriers and atomics Step 7 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Choose between a barrier and a lock, build a lock from atomicCAS without hanging the GPU, cut atomic traffic with privatisation, and see why a simple priority counter fails. About 45 min5 lessons3 animations2 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Barrier or lock 2Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock 3Fewer atomics: privatisation 4Priority locks and grid wide barriers 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[20,"barrier","Barrier or lock","There are two very different ways to make threads wait for each other. A barrier such as __syncthreads() is a meeting point. Nobody in the block goes past it until everyone in the block has arrived. It is like a traffic light that stays red until the whole group is at the line. A lock lets only one thread at a time into a critical section. The others wait outside, like a queue for a single restroom. Threads that do not need the section are not held up. Barrier or lock: who waits, and for how longEach thread first does some work of its own length. Then it either meets the others at a barrier, or takes a lock for its critical section. Step 7 of 7 Threads4 What happens after the work Length of the critical section2 Seed for the work lengths3 Work lengths 5, 1, 1, 4. Everyone waits until the slowest thread (length 5) reaches the barrier, then all continue together. A barrier makes the whole block wait, and only works inside one block. Try this Barrier: the fast threads wait (grey) for the slowest one. Then all go on together. Spin lock: only one thread at a time is in the critical section (orange). The others spin. Spin lock with 8 threads: the order of entry is luck, so a thread that arrived early can be passed many times. Ticket lock with the same threads: they enter in the order they arrived. This is the Bakery idea built from atomicAdd. __syncthreads() Lock from atomicCAS Purpose everyone reaches a point one at a time Who waits all threads of the block only threads that want the lock Scope one block the whole device (global memory), or shared memory Typical use tiled matmul, reductions, stencils global counters, shared queues, rare updates The lecture ends with a simple rule: who shares a variable decides which tool you need. Who shares the data Where it lives How to synchronise one thread registers, local memory nothing threads of one block shared memory __syncthreads(), or shared atomics threads of different blocks global memory atomics, locks, or end the kernel and start another Common mistake Every thread of the block must reach every __syncthreads(). A barrier inside an if that only some threads enter is undefined behaviour: the kernel may hang, or seem to work and give wrong results. The reduction chapter shows the lecture 12 example. Lesson 2 of 5"],[20,"atomics","Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock","An atomic operation reads, changes and writes a memory word as one step. No other thread can see or change the word in the middle. The hardware does this, so it is correct even with thousands of threads. Function What it does as one step Returns atomicAdd(p, v) *p = *p + v the old value atomicSub, atomicMin, atomicMax subtract, keep the smaller, keep the larger the old value atomicExch(p, v) *p = v the old value atomicAnd, atomicOr, atomicXor bit operations the old value atomicCAS(p, cmp, v) if *p == cmp then *p = v the old value Compare and swapExam favourite old = *p, if old = cmp then *p = v, return old If old = cmp, you won. Any other value means somebody else changed it first. All threads try to change the lock from 0 to 1. Exactly one gets back 0 and goes in. Everyone else gets back 1 and tries again. lock.cu __device__int lock = 0; // 0 = free, 1 = taken__device__volatileint total = 0;__device__void acquire(int *l) {while (atomicCAS(l, 0, 1) != 0) ; // try to change 0 into 1}__device__void release(int *l) {__threadfence(); // writes in the critical section go firstatomicExch(l, 0); // free the lock}__global__void add_block_sums(constint *part) {if (threadIdx.x == 0) { // only one thread per block takes the lock acquire(&lock); total = total + part[blockIdx.x]; // the critical section release(&lock); }__syncthreads(); // the rest of the block waits here} What each marked line does and why Line 2__device__ volatile int total = 0;volatile so the compiler reads total from memory each time and does not reuse a stale copy. Line 5while (atomicCAS(l, 0, 1) != 0) ; // try to change 0 into 1Spin until this thread is the one that changed 0 into 1. Line 9__threadfence(); // writes in the critical section go firstMake the writes of the critical section visible before anyone else can get the lock. Line 10atomicExch(l, 0); // free the lockatomicExch writes 0 as one step and frees the lock. Line 14if (threadIdx.x == 0) { // only one thread per block takes the lockOnly thread 0 of each block competes. 32 lanes of one warp spinning on the same lock could hang older GPUs. Line 16total = total + part[blockIdx.x]; // the critical sectionInside the lock one thread reads and writes total safely. Line 19__syncthreads(); // the rest of the block waits hereThe other threads of the block wait here for thread 0. In the exam The slide's critical_kernel lets every thread call acquire_lock. Before Volta (2017) the 32 lanes of a warp move in lock step. One lane wins the lock, but the warp keeps running the spin loop for the losers and never lets the winner reach release_lock, so the kernel hangs. Newer GPUs avoid the hang, but the lock then makes every thread of the grid wait in a single line, which is very slow. Take the lock with one thread per block, and add a fence before the release. A plain CAS spin lock has no queue, so a thread can lose again and again. A ticket lock fixes this with two counters. It is the Bakery idea built from atomics. ticket.cu __device__unsignedint next_ticket = 0;__device__unsignedint now_serving = 0;__device__void ticket_acquire() {unsignedint my = atomicAdd(&next_ticket, 1); // take a ticketwhile (atomicAdd(&now_serving, 0) != my) ; // an atomic read, until my number is called__threadfence();}__device__void ticket_release() {__threadfence();atomicAdd(&now_serving, 1); // call the next ticket} What each marked line does and why Line 5unsigned int my = atomicAdd(&next_ticket, 1); // take a ticketatomicAdd returns the old value, so every thread gets a different ticket. Line 6while (atomicAdd(&now_serving, 0) != my) ; // an atomic read, until my number is calledAdding 0 is a way to read the counter atomically. Line 7__threadfence();The fence keeps reads in the critical section from running before the lock is held. Line 12atomicAdd(&now_serving, 1); // call the next ticketMoving the counter on lets the next ticket in, in arrival order. Going deeper: Memory fences and their scope A fence makes this thread's earlier writes"],[20,"atomics","Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock","visible before its later ones. __threadfence_block() covers the threads of the same block, __threadfence() the whole GPU, and __threadfence_system() the GPU and the CPU (for mapped host memory). A fence does not make anyone wait. It only fixes the order in which other threads can see the writes. Lesson 3 of 5"],[20,"privat","Fewer atomics: privatisation","Atomics are always correct, but atomics on the same address are done one after another. Thousands of threads adding to one global counter form a long queue. Lecture 12 shows the problem. In unsafe_inc every thread reads a shared variable, adds 1 and writes it back, so 10 threads in one warp give 1. The fix uses atomicAdd on the shared variable. With many blocks the best plan is privatisation: each block counts in its own shared memory first, then one thread adds the block total to global memory. count_ones.cu __global__void count_ones(constint *x, int n, int *count) {__shared__int local;if (threadIdx.x == 0) local = 0;__syncthreads();int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n && x[i] == 1) atomicAdd(&local, 1); // fast atomic inside the block__syncthreads();if (threadIdx.x == 0) atomicAdd(count, local); // one global atomic per block} What each marked line does and why Line 2__shared__ int local;One private counter per block, in fast shared memory. Line 3if (threadIdx.x == 0) local = 0;Start the block counter at 0, not at the global value. Otherwise blocks would count each other's work. Line 4__syncthreads();Everyone must see the 0 before adding. Line 6if (i < n && x[i] == 1) atomicAdd(&local, 1); // fast atomic inside the blockShared memory atomic: no lost updates and much cheaper than a global one. Line 7__syncthreads();Wait until all threads of the block have added. Line 8if (threadIdx.x == 0) atomicAdd(count, local); // one global atomic per blockOne global atomic per block instead of one per thread. Workbook P10.2.5: 2²⁰ values, blocks of 1024, one atomic per block Blocks = 1,048,576 ÷ 1024 = 1024. Each block reduces its values in shared memory and does one global atomicAdd. One atomic per element would be 1,048,576. 1024 global atomics instead of 1,048,576 Remember compute-sanitizer --tool racecheck finds races on shared memory, such as the unsafe_inc example. Run it whenever a result changes from run to run. Lesson 4 of 5"],[20,"priority","Priority locks and grid wide barriers","Lecture 11 builds a priority lock from one \"now serving\" counter, like the token display at a clinic. A thread waits until the counter equals its priority, works, and then adds 1. Problem Why Fix Not mutual exclusion All threads with the same priority pass together. Add a real lock inside each level. Levels get skipped Three threads at level 2 each add 1, so the counter goes from 2 to 5. Move the counter on once per level, when the last thread of that level is done. Deadlock by residency If the priority 0 blocks are not running yet because spinning blocks fill the GPU, nobody moves the counter. Do not spin on other blocks. Use a grid barrier or separate kernels. Starvation A steady stream of high priority work keeps low priority work waiting. Ageing: raise the priority of work that has waited long. In the exam In the block priority code of slide 7, the counter must be read as volatile or atomically, or the compiler may move the read out of the loop and spin for ever. CUDA does not promise which blocks are running or in what order they start. That this code usually works is luck. Cooperative groups name a group of threads (a warp tile, a block, a cluster or the whole grid) and synchronise exactly that group. grid.sync() is a barrier for every thread of the launch, which __syncthreads() cannot do. grid_sync.cu #include <cooperative_groups.h>namespace cg = cooperative_groups;__global__void two_phase(float *a, float *b, int n) { cg::grid_group grid = cg::this_grid(); // every thread of the launchint i = grid.thread_rank();if (i < n) a[i] = a[i] * 2.0f; // phase 1 grid.sync(); // every block has finished phase 1if (i < n) b[i] = a[(i + 1) % n]; // phase 2 reads results of other blocks}void launch(float *a, float *b, int n, int blocks, int threads) {void *args[] = {&a, &b, &n};cudaLaunchCooperativeKernel((void *)two_phase, blocks, threads, args);} What each marked line does and why Line 5cg::grid_group grid = cg::this_grid(); // every thread of the launchA handle to every thread of the launch. Line 6int i = grid.thread_rank();The thread's number across the whole grid. Line 8grid.sync(); // every block has finished phase 1A barrier for the whole grid. Every block must be running at the same time for this to work. Line 9if (i < n) b[i] = a[(i + 1) % n]; // phase 2 reads results of other blocksNow it is safe to read values that other blocks wrote in phase 1. Line 14cudaLaunchCooperativeKernel((void *)two_phase, blocks, threads, args);A cooperative launch checks that all blocks fit on the GPU at once and fails otherwise. Largest cooperative gridExam favourite blocks ≤ (blocks per SM) × (number of SMs) The occupancy chapter gives blocks per SM. T4: 40 SMs, 4 blocks of 256 per SM, so at most 160 blocks. Lesson 5 of 5"],[20,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A class trip waits at the bus until every child is back. That is a barrier. The single key to the store room, passed from hand to hand, is a lock. __syncthreads() for one block, atomics for different blocks. atomicCAS returns the old value. Take locks with one thread per block, fence before release, and prefer atomicAdd or privatisation to locks. Measure contention: global atomics on one address are serialised. Replace with warp shuffles plus one atomic per warp or block. grid.sync() needs a cooperative launch with all blocks resident. The PTX memory model gives acquire, release and scope to every atomic (cuda::atomic_ref in libcu++). Lock free queues, work stealing and split arrive and wait barriers (cuda::barrier, mbarrier on Hopper) build on these ideas."],[21,"","Low precision numbers and quantisation","Float formats, range and detail, mixed precision, INT8 quantisation. · FP16 · BF16 · TF32 · FP8 · INT8 · quantisation · mixed precision Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 8: Tensor cores and fast matmul›Number formats Step 8 · Topic 1 of 3 · Pro Learn how FP32, TF32, FP16, BF16, FP8 and INT8 store a number, what each one loses, why sums stay in FP32, and how to quantise weights to 8 bits. About 40 min5 lessons2 animations9 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why use fewer bits? 2How a float is stored, and the formats side by side 3Which format when, and why the sum stays wide 4Quantisation step by step 5From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 5"],[21,"why","Why use fewer bits?","A neural network is a chain of layers. Lecture 9 splits them into two families. Family Examples Time goes into Tensor cores help? Math limited convolution, fully connected, recurrent calculating a lot Memory limited bias add, normalisation, ReLU, dropout moving numbers not at all Fewer bits per number help both. The memory limited layers move half the bytes in FP16 and a quarter in INT8. The math limited layers get faster hardware: a tensor core does many more 16 bit multiplies than 32 bit ones in the same time. It works because networks forgive small errors. In class the example was accuracy falling from 92 to 91 percent, which is fine, against 92 to 82 percent, which is not. Scientific codes, such as nuclear or biological simulations, cannot accept that and stay in FP64. Fewer bytes, higher intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes Halve the bytes and the arithmetic intensity doubles. Batch 1 FC layer: AI ≈ 1 in FP16, ≈ 2 with INT8 weights. Lesson 2 of 5"],[21,"formats","How a float is stored, and the formats side by side","Every floating point number has a sign bit, E exponent bits and M mantissa bits. Value of a normal numberExam favourite x = (−1)s × 1.m1m2…mM (binary) × 2e − bias The leading 1 is not stored, so M bits give M + 1 bits of precision. BiasExam favourite bias = 2E − 1 − 1 5 bits: 15. 8 bits: 127. 11 bits: 1023. Gap after 1.0 ε = 2−M FP32 1.2 × 10⁻⁷, FP16 9.8 × 10⁻⁴, BF16 7.8 × 10⁻³. Decimal digits ≈ (M + 1) × log10 2 ≈ 0.3 (M + 1) FP32 about 7, FP16 about 3, BF16 about 2.4. Format E M Largest Gap after 1.0 Digits FP64 11 52 1.8 × 10³⁰⁸ 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ 16 FP32 8 23 3.4 × 10³⁸ 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ 7.2 TF32 8 10 3.4 × 10³⁸ 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ 3.3 FP16 5 10 65,504 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ 3.3 BF16 8 7 3.4 × 10³⁸ 7.8 × 10⁻³ 2.4 FP8 E4M3 4 3 448 0.125 about 1 FP8 E5M2 5 2 57,344 0.25 under 1 Exponent bits give range: each extra bit doubles how many powers of two are available. Mantissa bits give detail: they split each interval such as [1, 2) into 2M equal steps. Type any number below and see what each format keeps. π in FP16 3.14159 = 1.5708 × 2¹. Sign 0. Exponent 1 + 15 = 16 = 10000₂. Mantissa: keep 10 bits of 0.5708, which is 1001001000₂. Value 1.5703125 × 2 = 3.140625. 3.140625, about 3 correct digits (FP32 gives 3.1415927) Lesson 3 of 5"],[21,"which","Which format when, and why the sum stays wide","Format Used for Watch out for FP32 master weights in training, science, anything delicate slow on tensor cores TF32 FP32 code on Ampere and later, about 8 times faster on an A100 not a storage format: memory still holds FP32 FP16 inference, training with loss scaling tiny range: gradients underflow, 70,000 overflows BF16 the default for training large models only about 2 to 3 digits FP8, FP4 Hopper and Blackwell training and inference needs a scale factor per tensor or per block INT8, INT4 inference after quantisation needs a scale factor, accuracy must be checked TF32 keeps FP32 in memory. The tensor core rounds the inputs to 10 mantissa bits inside, multiplies, and adds the results in FP32. The professor joked that it should have been called TF19, since it uses 1 + 8 + 10 bits. Mixed precision. A and B are stored in a narrow format, but the running sum is kept in FP32. Why it matters: add 1 to an FP16 sum again and again. Once the sum reaches 2048, the gap between FP16 numbers is 2, so 2048 + 1 rounds back to 2048 and the sum stops growing. FP16 versus FP32 accumulator (dot product of 4096 FP16 numbers, from the notes) True answer −18.16. FP16 accumulator: −18.36, error 0.20. FP32 accumulator: error 0.000026. About 7600 times smaller error with the wide accumulator Remember Loss scaling for FP16 training: multiply the loss by a large number such as 1024 before back propagation so that small gradients do not round to 0, then divide the gradients by the same number. BF16 has the range of FP32 and does not need it. In the exam Slide 11's bars (TF32, FP16 and BF16 at 4 times, FP8 at 8, FP4 at 16) are labelled illustrative. On an A100, TF32 is 8 times and FP16 16 times the FP32 CUDA core rate. Always use the data sheet of the GPU in front of you. Lesson 4 of 5"],[21,"quant","Quantisation step by step","In class the idea was \"treat everything from 0.9 to 1.7 as 1\". Real quantisation is a straight line map from real numbers to small integers. QuantiseExam favourite q = clip(round(x ÷ s) + z, qmin, qmax) s is the scale, z the zero point. Bring backExam favourite x̂ = s × (q − z) The rounding error is at most s ÷ 2 unless the value was clipped. Symmetric INT8 s = R ÷ 127, z = 0 For values between −R and R. Asymmetric (UINT8) s = (max − min) ÷ 255, z = round(−min ÷ s) For values that are not centred on 0, such as ReLU outputs. Workbook 9.3: store 0.3 with R = 1 s = 1 ÷ 127 = 0.007874. 0.3 ÷ 0.007874 = 38.1, round to 38. Back: 38 × 0.007874 = 0.2992. Error 0.0008, storage 1 byte instead of 4 quantise.cu #include <cuda_fp16.h>#include <cuda_bf16.h>#include <cstdint>__global__void quantise(constfloat *w, int8_t *q, int n, float s) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) {float r = rintf(w[i] / s); // round to the nearest integer r = fminf(fmaxf(r, -127.0f), 127.0f); // clip to the INT8 range q[i] = (int8_t)r; }}__global__void dequantise(const int8_t *q, float *w, int n, float s) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) w[i] = s * q[i];}__global__void to_16bit(constfloat *x, __half *h, __nv_bfloat16 *b, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) { h[i] = __float2half(x[i]); // FP16: 5 exponent bits, 10 mantissa bits b[i] = __float2bfloat16(x[i]); // BF16: 8 exponent bits, 7 mantissa bits }} What each marked line does and why Line 5__global__ void quantise(const float *w, int8_t *q, int n, float s) {One thread per weight. s is computed once on the host. Line 8float r = rintf(w[i] / s); // round to the nearest integerrintf rounds to the nearest integer, with ties to even. Line 9r = fminf(fmaxf(r, -127.0f), 127.0f); // clip to the INT8 rangeClip so that a weight outside the range does not wrap around. Line 10q[i] = (int8_t)r;The integer is stored in a single byte. Line 16if (i < n) w[i] = s * q[i];Dequantise: multiply back by the scale. Line 22h[i] = __float2half(x[i]); // FP16: 5 exponent bits, 10 mantissa bitsConversions to the two 16 bit formats are built in. Going deeper: Per channel scales and FP8 scaling One scale for a whole tensor is set by its largest value, so small channels get coarse steps. Real systems keep one scale per output channel or per block of 32 to 128 values. FP8 training on Hopper keeps a scale factor per tensor that is updated from the largest values seen recently, so numbers stay inside the 448 limit of E4M3. Lesson 5 of 5"],[21,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A format is a ruler. The exponent decides how long it is, the mantissa how fine the marks are. FP16 is short with fine marks, BF16 is very long with coarse marks. x = (−1)s × 1.m × 2e − bias. FP16 fails on size, BF16 on detail. Accumulate in FP32. INT8: s = R ÷ 127, q = round(x ÷ s). Pick the format per tensor: weights INT8 or FP8, activations BF16, sums FP32. Check overflow and underflow with real data. Batch 1 inference gains roughly in proportion to the bytes saved. Research uses 4 bit weights (GPTQ, AWQ), block scaled FP4 on Blackwell, stochastic rounding for training, and outlier handling such as SmoothQuant."],[22,"","Tensor cores, GEMM and WMMA","FC layer intensity, GEMM, im2col, tensor core peaks, WMMA, waves. · tensor cores · WMMA · GEMM · im2col · arithmetic intensity · wave quantisation Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 8: Tensor cores and fast matmul›Tensor cores Step 8 · Topic 2 of 3 · Pro Find out when a layer is limited by maths or by memory, turn convolutions into matrix multiplies, see where tensor core speed comes from, write a WMMA kernel and avoid half empty waves. About 50 min6 lessons4 animations9 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1When is a layer limited by maths? 2Everything is a matrix multiply 3Tensor cores and where their speed comes from 4Programming tensor cores with WMMA 5Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation 6From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 6"],[22,"fclayer","When is a layer limited by maths?","Lecture 9 starts with a simple test. Work out how long the maths takes and how long the data takes. The slower one sets the pace. Time for maths and for data Tmath = ops ÷ BWmath, Tmem = bytes ÷ BWmem With overlap, the larger one decides the speed. Math limited whenExam favourite ops ÷ bytes > BWmath ÷ BWmem Left: the arithmetic intensity of the operation. Right: the ops per byte ratio of the GPU, the ridge point of the roofline. A fully connected layer computes Y = WX. W is Nout × Nin, X is Nin × B, Y is Nout × B. Work ops = 2 Nin Nout B Each output is a dot product of length Nin: one multiply and one add per term. Bytes in FP16 bytes = 2 (Nin B + Nin Nout + Nout B) Read X, read W, write Y. 4096 × 4096 layerExam favourite AI = 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B) Batch 1 gives about 1. The limit for a huge batch is 2048. A100: from which batch is the layer math limited? Ridge = 312 × 10¹² ÷ 1.555 × 10¹² ≈ 201 FLOP/byte. Need 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B) > 201. 4096 B > 823,296 + 402 B, so 3694 B > 823,296. B > 222.9, so from a batch of 223 The roofline: is a kernel limited by maths or by memory?Pick a GPU and a kernel's arithmetic intensity (FLOPs per byte moved). Step to draw the two roofs, the ridge and the kernel. Step 4 of 4 GPU Arithmetic intensity (FLOP per byte)0.25 A100 FP32 (19,500 GFLOP/s, 1555 GB/s). Attainable = min(peak, AI × bandwidth) = min(19,500, 0.25 × 1555) = 388.75 GFLOP/s, 1.994 percent of peak. AI 0.25 is left of the ridge 12.54: memory bound. Only more bandwidth or more reuse (higher AI) helps. Try this Plain matrix multiply (AI 0.25) on an A100: 0.25 × 1555 = 389 GFLOP/s, 50 times below the peak (workbook 5.4). SAXPY (AI 1/6) on Fermi: 24 GFLOP/s. Memory bound, more cores would not help. Tiling with T = 16 gives AI = 4 on Fermi: still left of the ridge at 6.94. AI = 20 on the A100 passes the ridge at 12.5: now the maths units are the limit. Python # Arithmetic intensity of a 4096 x 4096 fully connected layer in FP16def ai(nin, nout, batch): flops = 2 * nin * nout * batch bytes_ = 2 * (nin * batch + nin * nout + nout * batch)return flops / bytes_ridge = 312e12 / 1.555e12# A100 FP16 tensor cores against HBMprint(\"A100 ridge:\", round(ridge, 1), \"FLOP/byte\")for b in (1, 32, 222, 223, 512):print(\"batch\", b, \" AI\", round(ai(4096, 4096, b), 1)) Output A100 ridge: 200.6 FLOP/byte batch 1 AI 1.0 batch 32 AI 31.5 batch 222 AI 200.3 batch 223 AI 201.1 batch 512 AI 409.6 This is why a chatbot answering one user at a time is limited by memory bandwidth (batch 1, AI about 1), and why serving systems batch many users together and shrink weights to 8 or 4 bits. Training uses big batches and runs where tensor cores shine. Lesson 2 of 6"],[22,"gemm","Everything is a matrix multiply","GEMM (general matrix multiply) computes C ← αAB + βC with A of size M × K, B of size K × N and C of size M × N. It costs 2MNK FLOPs. Layer M N K Fully connected output features batch input features Convolution (implicit GEMM) output channels batch × output height × output width input channels × filter height × filter width Attention sequence sequence or head size head size or sequence A convolution becomes a GEMM by cutting out every window of the picture and laying it out as a row. This is called im2col. Step through the worked example from the notes. Workbook 9.2: RGB 224 × 224, 64 filters of 3 × 3, padding 1 M = 64 output channels. N = 224 × 224 = 50,176 output pixels. K = 3 × 3 × 3 = 27. FLOPs = 2 × 64 × 50,176 × 27 = 173,408,256 Remember im2col copies each pixel into up to 9 rows, so the matrix is 9 times bigger than the picture. Libraries such as cuDNN never build it in memory. They compute the addresses on the fly, which is why it is called implicit GEMM. Lesson 3 of 6"],[22,"tc","Tensor cores and where their speed comes from","A tensor core computes a whole small matrix product D = A × B + C in one operation. On Volta each tensor core does a 4 × 4 × 4 product per clock: 64 fused multiply adds, where an ordinary CUDA core does 1. Peak of the tensor coresExam favourite peak = SMs × tensor cores per SM × FMAs per clock × 2 × clock An FMA counts as 2 FLOPs. GPU SMs × TCs FMAs per clock Clock Peak V100 FP16 80 × 8 64 1.53 GHz 125 TFLOP/s T4 FP16 40 × 8 64 1.59 GHz 65 TFLOP/s (FP32: 8.1) A100 FP16 / BF16 108 × 4 256 1.41 GHz 312 TFLOP/s A100 TF32 108 × 4 128 1.41 GHz 156 TFLOP/s (FP32 CUDA cores: 19.5) H100 SXM BF16 132 × 4 512 1.83 GHz 989 TFLOP/s On an A100, FP16 tensor cores are 16 times the FP32 CUDA cores. About 8 times comes from the hardware (TF32 against FP32) and 2 times from the narrower numbers. Both happen together. Remember Real kernels rarely see the data sheet peak. Under a heavy matmul an H100 hits its 700 W power limit and lowers its clock, so 750 TFLOP/s may be closer to what is reachable than 750 ÷ 989 = 76 percent suggests. Going deeper: What Ampere added BF16, TF32 and FP64 on tensor cores. cp.async copies global memory straight into shared memory while the SM keeps computing, which allows deep pipelines. Structured sparsity: if 2 of every 4 weights are zero, tensor cores can skip them for up to 2 times the speed. Libraries: cuBLAS, cuDNN, CUTLASS, cuSPARSE, cuFFT. Lesson 4 of 6"],[22,"wmma","Programming tensor cores with WMMA","WMMA (warp matrix multiply accumulate) lets the 32 threads of a warp work together on one 16 × 16 × 16 product. Each thread holds a piece of each matrix in its registers, called a fragment. Which piece is not specified, so code must never rely on it. wmma_gemm.cu #include <mma.h>#include <cuda_fp16.h>usingnamespace nvcuda;constint WM = 16, WN = 16, WK = 16; // shape of one mma_sync__global__void wmma_gemm(consthalf *A, consthalf *B, float *C, int M, int N, int K) {int warpM = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / 32; // tile row owned by this warpint warpN = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y; // tile columnif (warpM * WM >= M || warpN * WN >= N) return; // the whole warp leaves togetherwmma::fragment<wmma::matrix_a, WM, WN, WK, half, wmma::row_major> a_frag;wmma::fragment<wmma::matrix_b, WM, WN, WK, half, wmma::col_major> b_frag;wmma::fragment<wmma::accumulator, WM, WN, WK, float> c_frag;wmma::fill_fragment(c_frag, 0.0f);for (int k = 0; k < K; k += WK) { // walk along Kconsthalf *a_tile = A + (warpM * WM) * K + k; // A row major, leading dimension Kconsthalf *b_tile = B + (warpN * WN) * K + k; // B column major, leading dimension Kwmma::load_matrix_sync(a_frag, a_tile, K);wmma::load_matrix_sync(b_frag, b_tile, K);wmma::mma_sync(c_frag, a_frag, b_frag, c_frag); // c += a * b on the tensor cores }float *c_tile = C + (warpM * WM) * N + warpN * WN;wmma::store_matrix_sync(c_tile, c_frag, N, wmma::mem_row_major);}void launch(consthalf *A, consthalf *B, float *C, int M, int N, int K) {dim3 block(128, 4); // 4 warps along x, 4 along ydim3 grid((M / 16 + 3) / 4, (N / 16 + 3) / 4); wmma_gemm<<<grid, block>>>(A, B, C, M, N, K);} What each marked line does and why Line 1#include <mma.h>The WMMA API lives in the mma.h header, in the namespace nvcuda::wmma. Line 4const int WM = 16, WN = 16, WK = 16; // shape of one mma_syncThe fixed shape of one tensor core operation. Line 6__global__ void wmma_gemm(const half *A, const half *B, float *C, int M, int N, int K) {Inputs in half (FP16), output in float (FP32): mixed precision. Line 7int warpM = (blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x) / 32; // tile row owned by this warpEach warp, not each thread, owns one 16 × 16 output tile. Dividing the x index by 32 numbers the warps. Line 9if (warpM * WM >= M || warpN * WN >= N) return; // the whole warp leaves togetherAll 32 threads of a warp must take part in every WMMA call, so out of range warps leave as a whole. Line 11wmma::fragment<wmma::matrix_a, WM, WN, WK, half, wmma::row_major> a_frag;The A tile fragment, stored row major. Line 13wmma::fragment<wmma::accumulator, WM, WN, WK, float> c_frag;The FP32 accumulator fragment, spread over the 32 threads' registers. Line 14wmma::fill_fragment(c_frag, 0.0f);Start the accumulator at 0. This is C in D = AB + C. Line 16for (int k = 0; k < K; k += WK) { // walk along KMarch along K, 16 at a time. This is the tiling loop of the matmul chapter. Line 17const half *a_tile = A + (warpM * WM) * K + k; // A row major, leading dimension KTop left corner of the current A tile. The last argument of load_matrix_sync is the leading dimension. Line 19wmma::load_matrix_sync(a_frag, a_tile, K);The warp loads the A tile into its fragment together. Line 21wmma::mma_sync(c_frag, a_frag, b_frag, c_frag); // c += a * b on the tensor coresOne call: 16³ = 4096 multiply adds = 8192 FLOPs on the tensor cores. Line 24wmma::store_matrix_sync(c_tile, c_frag, N, wmma::mem_row_major);Write the finished tile back to C in row major order. Line 28dim3 block(128, 4); // 4 warps along x, 4 along y128 × 4 threads = 16 warps = 16 output tiles per block. Workbook 9.5: M = N = K = 4096, one warp per 16 × 16 tile Tiles: (4096 ÷ 16)² = 65,536. Steps along K: 4096 ÷ 16 = 256. Calls: 65,536 × 256 = 16,777,216. 16,777,216 × 8192 = 137,438,953,472 = 2 × 4096³ FLOPs Real kernels give each block a bigger tile. In the slide a block of 8 warps computes a 128 × 128 tile: 2048 outputs per warp, 8 WMMA tiles per warp, and 64 FP32 accumulators per"],[22,"wmma","Programming tensor cores with WMMA","thread. Strips of A and B pass through shared memory, while the C tile stays in registers until the end. In the exam Slide 16 says shared memory is used for the matrix C. In real kernels such as CUTLASS, C stays in registers during the main loop and only passes through shared memory when it is written out at the end. Level What you write Libraries cuBLAS (cublasGemmEx), cuDNN, PyTorch with FP16, BF16 or allow_tf32 Templates CUTLASS and CuTe WMMA the API above: portable and simple PTX mma.sync warp level with exact register layouts (Ampere) PTX wgmma Hopper: 4 warps issue asynchronous tensor core work fed by TMA Lesson 5 of 6"],[22,"waves","Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation","Two effects make time jump in steps instead of growing smoothly. Warp granularity lane efficiency = T ÷ (32 ⌈T ÷ 32⌉) 33 threads need 2 warps: 51.6 percent of lanes used. 65 threads: 67.7 percent. WavesExam favourite waves = ⌈blocks ÷ (blocks per SM × SMs)⌉ One wave fills every SM once. Wave efficiencyExam favourite n ÷ (W × ⌈n ÷ W⌉), with W = blocks per wave 217 blocks on an A100: 217 ÷ 324 = 67 percent. Workbook 9.6: 4096 × 4096 output on an A100, 216 blocks per wave 128 × 128 tiles: 32 × 32 = 1024 blocks, ⌈1024 ÷ 216⌉ = 5 waves. Efficiency 1024 ÷ 1080 = 94.8 percent. 128 × 256 tiles: 512 blocks, ⌈512 ÷ 216⌉ = 3 waves, 512 ÷ 648 = 79.0 percent. The bigger tile does more work per byte but wastes more of the last wave Remember Fixes: sizes that are multiples of 32 threads and of the wave size, a tile size that divides the problem evenly, persistent kernels, or stream-K, which splits the leftover work along K across the idle SMs (as CUTLASS does). The same effect exists on AMD GPUs. Lesson 6 of 6"],[22,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths An ordinary core is a person with a calculator doing one a × b + c at a time. A tensor core is a machine that multiplies two small grids of numbers in one go. Math limited when AI > peak ÷ bandwidth. FC layer: AI = 4096B ÷ (4096 + 2B), math limited on an A100 from batch 223. Peak = SMs × TCs × FMAs × 2 × clock. One mma_sync = 8192 FLOPs. Use cuBLAS or CUTLASS first. For your own kernel: 128 × 128 block tiles, warps of 16 × 16 fragments, FP32 accumulators, sizes that divide into full waves. Check tensor core use in Nsight Compute. Hopper wgmma with TMA and warp specialisation, Blackwell tcgen05 with tensor memory, FlashAttention fusing softmax into the GEMM, and stream-K scheduling are the current research frontier."],[23,"","How fast matmul kernels are built","H100 peak, naive kernel, tile intensity, swizzling, wgmma, pipelines, the ladder. · matmul · H100 · tiling · swizzle · TMA · wgmma · performance ladder Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 8: Tensor cores and fast matmul›Fast matmul Step 8 · Topic 3 of 3 · Pro Follow a matmul on the H100 from 0.24 to 764 TFLOP/s: coalescing, tiles, tensor cores, TMA, swizzling, wgmma and pipelines, with the maths behind each step. About 50 min6 lessons5 animations3 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The H100 and its speed of light 2The naive kernel and a 13 times lesson 3Tiles: more work per byte 4Hopper tricks: TMA, swizzling, wgmma, pipelines 5The performance ladder 6From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 6"],[23,"machine","The H100 and its speed of light","The blog that lecture 9 recommends builds a matmul for the H100 step by step. First, the machine. Part H100 SXM5 SMs 132 (the full chip has 144, some are switched off) Each SM 4 quadrants, each with a warp scheduler, FP32 units, a tensor core and load and store units Warps issuing per clock 4 per SM (one per quadrant), out of up to 64 resident Registers 64K 32 bit registers per SM Shared memory and L1 up to 228 KB per SM, the same SRAM split by configuration L2 50 MB, shared by all SMs HBM3 80 GB at about 3.35 TB/s Only 4 warps per SM issue in a given clock, but 64 can wait their turn. The extra warps hide memory latency, as in the scheduling chapter. A block should have at least 4 warps so all four schedulers have work. Why the memory pyramid exists: SRAM (registers, caches) needs 6 transistors per bit, so it is fast but small. DRAM needs one transistor and one capacitor per bit, so it is dense, cheap and slow. Opening a DRAM row is the expensive part, and reading neighbouring columns of an open row is cheap. That is the physical reason for coalescing. Where does a variable live?Pick a declaration and step: where it is stored, who can see it, how long it lives, and how fast it is. Step 4 of 4 Declaration __shared__ float tile[256]; Stored in: shared memory (on the SM) Seen by: all threads of one block Lives for: the block Speed: about 20 to 30 ticks Each block gets its own copy. Threads of other blocks cannot see it. Green boxes show who can see it. Try this A plain local variable lives in a register: private, gone when the thread ends, the fastest. A __shared__ array: one copy per block, seen by all its threads, on the SM. Memory from cudaMalloc is global: every SM sees it and it survives across kernel launches. A big private array can end up in local memory, which really lives in slow DRAM. Workbook 9.4: H100 SXM5 BF16 peak Tensor cores: 132 × 4 = 528. Each does 512 BF16 FMAs = 1024 FLOPs per clock. 528 × 1024 × 1.83 × 10⁹. ≈ 989 TFLOP/s Remember Under a heavy matmul the H100 reaches its 700 W power limit and lowers its clock. At 1.5 GHz the peak is 989 × 1.5 ÷ 1.83 ≈ 811 TFLOP/s, so a kernel at 750 TFLOP/s is at about 92.5 percent of what is reachable. Lesson 2 of 6"],[23,"naive","The naive kernel and a 13 times lesson","One thread per output, blocks of 32 × 32 threads, N = 4096. The only difference between the two kernels below is which index the x thread number feeds. naive.cu __global__void matmul_fast_order(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // neighbouring threads take neighbouring columnsif (row < N && col < N) {float acc = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N; ++k) acc += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // B along a row: coalesced C[row * N + col] = acc; }}__global__void matmul_slow_order(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {int row = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // neighbouring threads take neighbouring rowsint col = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (row < N && col < N) {float acc = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N; ++k) acc += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // A with a stride of N: not coalesced C[row * N + col] = acc; }} What each marked line does and why Line 3int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // neighbouring threads take neighbouring columnsThreads next to each other in a warp take columns next to each other. Line 7acc += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // B along a row: coalescedB[k * N + col] is read at consecutive addresses across the warp, and A[row * N + k] is the same address for the whole warp: both are cheap. Line 8C[row * N + col] = acc;The writes to C are consecutive too. Line 13int row = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; // neighbouring threads take neighbouring rowsNow neighbouring threads take neighbouring rows. Line 18acc += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; // A with a stride of N: not coalescedA[row * N + k] jumps N floats between neighbouring threads, so every thread needs its own memory sector. Kernel Measured on H100 Time for 2 × 4096³ FLOPs x feeds the column (coalesced) 3171 GFLOP/s 43 ms x feeds the row (uncoalesced) 243 GFLOP/s 565 ms, 13 times slower One line of index arithmetic costs 13 times. The rule from the coalescing chapter: what matters is what neighbouring threads read at the same moment, not what one thread reads over time. Going deeper: Tile quantisation and PTX against SASS A 33 × 33 matrix with 32 × 32 tiles needs 2 × 2 tiles: 4096 threads for 1089 outputs, so 73 percent of threads do nothing. In the SASS of the naive kernel, ptxas unrolled the loop 16 times and moved all loads to the top so they overlap with the FMAs. Researchers have gained 15 percent by rewriting SASS by hand to avoid bank conflicts. Lesson 3 of 6"],[23,"tiles","Tiles: more work per byte","The naive kernel has an arithmetic intensity of 0.25 (2 FLOPs per 8 bytes loaded). It reaches 3.17 TFLOP/s only because the caches serve repeated loads. To get further, each block keeps a tile of A and of B in shared memory and reuses them. Intensity of a BM × BN tileExam favourite AI = (2 ÷ b) × BMBN ÷ (BM + BN) b = bytes per number. BK cancels, so only the tile shape matters. Square is best BM + BN ≥ 2√(BMBN) For a fixed area the sum is smallest when BM = BN (AM-GM). Square FP32 tile T × TExam favourite AI = T ÷ 4 32 × 32: 8. 128 × 128: 32. BF16 doubles these: AI = T ÷ 2. Thread tiling. The same idea one level down: each thread keeps a TM × TN block of results in registers. Per k step it loads TM + TN numbers from shared memory and does TM × TN FMAs. thread_tile.cu // Inner loop of a thread tile: each thread keeps TM x TN results in registerstemplate <int TM, int TN>__device__void thread_tile(constfloat *As, constfloat *Bs, float acc[TM][TN],int ty, int tx, int BK, int BM, int BN) {float a[TM], b[TN];for (int k = 0; k < BK; ++k) {for (int i = 0; i < TM; ++i) a[i] = As[k * BM + ty * TM + i]; // A is stored transposed in shared memoryfor (int j = 0; j < TN; ++j) b[j] = Bs[k * BN + tx * TN + j];for (int i = 0; i < TM; ++i)for (int j = 0; j < TN; ++j) acc[i][j] += a[i] * b[j]; // TM x TN FMAs from TM + TN loads }} What each marked line does and why Line 2template <int TM, int TN>TM and TN are fixed at compile time so the arrays stay in registers. Line 7for (int i = 0; i < TM; ++i) a[i] = As[k * BM + ty * TM + i]; // A is stored transposed in shared memoryA is stored transposed in shared memory so these reads are contiguous and can use 128 bit loads. Line 9for (int i = 0; i < TM; ++i)An outer product of a column of A and a row of B. Line 11acc[i][j] += a[i] * b[j]; // TM x TN FMAs from TM + TN loadsTM × TN FMAs for TM + TN loads. 8 × 8 gives 64 FMAs for 16 loads. The blog's best kernel before tensor cores uses 128 × 128 block tiles with BK = 16, thread tiles of results in registers, 128 bit loads, and two __syncthreads() per k step. This is warp tiling. It reaches about 32 TFLOP/s on the H100, the best the plain FP32 cores manage in the blog, and far below what tensor cores offer. In the exam With the numbers used here (756 TFLOP/s BF16 on the PCIe card, 2.0 TB/s) the ridge is about 378 FLOP/byte. The blog quotes about 410 with slightly different specs. Either way a kernel needs hundreds of FLOPs per DRAM byte, so tiles must also be reused through L2 and shared memory. Lesson 4 of 6"],[23,"hopper","Hopper tricks: TMA, swizzling, wgmma, pipelines","TMA (tensor memory accelerator) is a copy engine inside each SM. One thread asks it to move a whole 64 × 64 tile from global to shared memory. A barrier in shared memory counts the bytes that arrive, so the waiting threads continue only when the whole tile has landed. Swizzling solves bank conflicts. A 128 byte row is 8 chunks of 16 bytes, and each chunk covers 4 banks. Reading one column of 8 rows hits the same 4 banks 8 times. Store chunk c of row r in slot c XOR r instead, and the column spreads over all 8 slots. swizzle.cu // 128 byte swizzle: XOR the row within a group of 8 rows (bits 7 to 9) into the 16 byte chunk index (bits 4 to 6)__device__ __forceinline__ unsigned swizzle128(unsigned byte_offset) {unsigned row8 = (byte_offset >> 7) & 7;return byte_offset ^ (row8 << 4);} What each marked line does and why Line 3unsigned row8 = (byte_offset >> 7) & 7;Bits 7 to 9 of the byte offset give the row inside a group of 8 rows of 128 bytes. Line 4return byte_offset ^ (row8 << 4);XOR the row into bits 4 to 6, the index of the 16 byte chunk. Applying it twice gives back the original offset. Worked XOR example Row 5, chunk 2: 010 XOR 101 = 111 = 7, stored in slot 7. Row 5, chunk 7: 111 XOR 101 = 010 = 2. So chunks 2 and 7 of row 5 swap places. XOR with a fixed value is its own inverse wgmma. On Hopper a warp group (4 warps, 128 threads) issues asynchronous tensor core instructions of shape m64nNk16. A 64 × 64 output needs four m64n64k16 calls for K = 64, and the accumulator is spread as 64 × 64 ÷ 128 = 32 FP32 registers per thread. Producer and consumer pipeline. One warp group only issues TMA loads into a ring of shared memory buffers. One or two other warp groups only run tensor core maths. Two barriers per buffer (full and empty) keep them in step. This is the stream overlap idea from chapter 6.2, inside one SM. Trick What it fixes Persistent kernel one long lived block per SM loops over tiles, so stores overlap the next loads and no wave is half empty Hilbert curve order of tiles neighbouring SMs use the same strips of A and B, so L2 hits go up Thread block clusters and multicast 2 SMs share loaded tiles through distributed shared memory Two consumer warp groups a 128 × 256 tile without running out of registers Lesson 5 of 6"],[23,"ladder","The performance ladder","Each step fixes one bottleneck. Step through the numbers reported in the blog for BF16 on an H100. The biggest jump, from 32 to 317 TFLOP/s, is simply using tensor cores fed by TMA. Everything after that keeps the tensor cores busy: bigger tiles (higher AI), overlap (pipelines), no idle SMs (persistent kernels), better cache reuse (clusters, Hilbert order) and cheaper waiting (faster barriers). The final 764 TFLOP/s is about 77 percent of the 989 peak, on par with cuBLAS. Course idea Where it shows up in the ladder Coalescing (3.2) the 13 times gap of the naive kernel Shared memory and banks (3.3) tiles and swizzling Tiled matmul (5.2) block tiles, warp tiles, thread tiles Occupancy and waves (2.4, 8.2) persistent kernels Streams and overlap (6.2) producer and consumer pipelines Barriers (7.2) mbarrier and faster barriers Tensor cores (8.2) the 10 times jump Lesson 6 of 6"],[23,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Build the fastest kitchen: first stop the waiter running back and forth (coalescing), then keep ingredients on the counter (tiles), then hire the stamping machine (tensor cores), then make sure it never waits (pipelines). Naive coalesced 43 ms, uncoalesced 565 ms. Tile AI = (2 ÷ b) BMBN ÷ (BM + BN). H100 BF16 peak 989, ridge about 378. Swizzle slot = chunk XOR row. Profile each step in Nsight Compute: memory throughput, bank conflicts, tensor core utilisation, stall reasons. Change one thing at a time and keep the numbers. CUTLASS 3 and CuTe describe layouts and swizzles as algebra. ThunderKittens, Triton and FlashAttention 3 apply the same Hopper tricks. Blackwell moves accumulators into tensor memory with tcgen05."],[24,"","Debugging and profiling CUDA code","Error checks, compute-sanitizer, cuda-gdb, Nsight, PTX and SASS. · compute-sanitizer · cuda-gdb · Nsight Compute · Nsight Systems · PTX · SASS Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 9: Tools and Python on the GPU›Debug and profile Step 9 · Topic 1 of 2 · College Catch errors at the right line, find bad memory accesses with compute-sanitizer, step through a kernel with cuda-gdb, read an Nsight Compute report, and look inside PTX and SASS. About 45 min6 lessons2 animations11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1A workflow for wrong or slow code 2compute-sanitizer 3Stepping through a kernel with cuda-gdb 4Profiling with Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute 5Reading PTX and SASS 6From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 6"],[24,"workflow","A workflow for wrong or slow code","GPU bugs are hard to see. Thousands of threads run at once, errors appear later than the line that caused them, and a wrong write often does not crash. Lecture 12 gives a fixed order of tools. Check every callWrap each CUDA call in a check and call cudaGetLastError after each launch. Wrong result?compute-sanitizer first, then printf on a few threads, then cuda-gdb. Slow?Nsight Systems for the whole timeline, then Nsight Compute on the slowest kernel. Measure againChange one thing and re-measure. bad_mem.cu #include <cstdio>#include <cstdlib>#define CUDA_CHECK(call) \\do { \\cudaError_t err = (call); \\if (err != cudaSuccess) { \\ fprintf(stderr, \"CUDA error %s at %s:%d\\n\", \\cudaGetErrorString(err), __FILE__, __LINE__); \\ exit(1); \\ } \\ } while (0)__global__void badAccess(int *data) {int i = threadIdx.x; data[i + 10] = i; // writes data[10] to data[41]if (i < 2) printf(\"thread %d writes index %d\\n\", i, i + 10);}int main() {int *d; CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d, 10 * sizeof(int))); // valid indices 0 to 9 only badAccess<<<1, 32>>>(d); CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError()); // errors in the launch itself CUDA_CHECK(cudaDeviceSynchronize()); // errors while the kernel ran CUDA_CHECK(cudaFree(d));return0;} What each marked line does and why Line 4#define CUDA_CHECK(call) \\A macro so that every call reports the file and line where it failed. Line 6cudaError_t err = (call); \\Every CUDA runtime call returns an error code. Line 9cudaGetErrorString(err), __FILE__, __LINE__); \\cudaGetErrorString turns the code into readable text. Line 12} while (0)The do and while (0) wrapper makes the macro behave like one statement. Line 16data[i + 10] = i; // writes data[10] to data[41]The bug from lecture 12: thread i writes index i + 10. Line 17if (i < 2) printf(\"thread %d writes index %d\\n\", i, i + 10);printf works inside kernels. Filter it, or thousands of threads flood the output. Line 22CUDA_CHECK(cudaMalloc(&d, 10 * sizeof(int))); // valid indices 0 to 9 onlyOnly 10 ints are allocated. Line 24CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError()); // errors in the launch itselfCatches errors in the launch itself, such as too many threads per block. Line 25CUDA_CHECK(cudaDeviceSynchronize()); // errors while the kernel ranCatches errors that happen while the kernel runs. Kernel errors show up only at a synchronising call. Device printf output is buffered on the GPU and printed at the next synchronisation. The order between threads is not fixed. Setting CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 makes every launch synchronous, so an error is reported at the launch that caused it. Common mistake This program usually runs without any message. The stray writes land in memory the program happens to own, so nothing crashes. That is exactly why out of bounds bugs are dangerous and why the sanitizer comes first. Lesson 2 of 6"],[24,"sanitizer","compute-sanitizer","compute-sanitizer runs your program and watches every memory access. It has four tools. Tool Finds Example in this course memcheck (default) out of bounds or misaligned accesses, bad frees, leaks badAccess above racecheck shared memory races (a missing __syncthreads) unsafe_inc in chapter 7.2 initcheck reads of global memory that was never written reading cudaMalloc memory before setting it synccheck illegal barriers (a __syncthreads inside a divergent if) the reduction in chapter 5.1 Run the tools nvcc -g -lineinfo bad_mem.cu -o bad_memcompute-sanitizer ./bad_memcompute-sanitizer --tool racecheck ./unsafe_inccompute-sanitizer --tool initcheck ./a.outcompute-sanitizer --tool synccheck ./reducecompute-sanitizer --leak-check full ./a.out What memcheck reports (shortened) ========= Invalid __global__ write of size 4 bytes ========= at badAccess(int *) ... bad_mem.cu:16 ========= by thread (0,0,0) in block (0,0,0) ========= Address ... is out of bounds (one report per bad thread, shortened here) In the exam Slide 6 says threads 0 to 21 write out of bounds. Thread i writes data[i + 10], so even thread 0 writes data[10], one past the end. All 32 threads write out of bounds, to indices 10 to 41. Lesson 3 of 6"],[24,"gdb","Stepping through a kernel with cuda-gdb","cuda-gdb is the GNU debugger extended to GPUs. You can stop inside a kernel, step line by line, print device variables, and switch the focus to any block, thread, warp or lane. Compile with -g (host) and -G (device). -G turns off most device optimisations, so debug builds are slow. bounds_bug.cu #define N 64__global__void boundsBugAdd(constint *a, constint *b, int *c) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; c[x + 1] = a[x] + b[x]; // bug: writes one element too far}__global__void boundsFixedAdd(constint *a, constint *b, int *c) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (x < N) c[x] = a[x] + b[x]; // fixed index and a guard} What each marked line does and why Line 4int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;x is the global thread index. Line 5c[x + 1] = a[x] + b[x]; // bug: writes one element too farThe bug: c[x + 1]. The last thread writes one element past the end. Line 10if (x < N) c[x] = a[x] + b[x]; // fixed index and a guardThe fix: write c[x] and guard with if (x < N), the standard pattern. The session from the slides nvcc -g -G bounds_bug.cu -o bounds_bugcuda-gdb ./bounds_bug(cuda-gdb) set cuda break_on_launch application(cuda-gdb) run(cuda-gdb) step(cuda-gdb) p x(cuda-gdb) cuda block (1,0,0) thread (31,0,0)(cuda-gdb) step(cuda-gdb) p x(cuda-gdb) info cuda threads What the two prints show p x in block 0, thread 0 prints 0 p x in block 1, thread 31 prints 63 (1 × 32 + 31) Workbook 12.2: block 1, thread 31, blockDim 32 x = 1 × 32 + 31 = 63. The thread writes c[x + 1] = c[64]. The arrays hold 64 elements: indices 0 to 63. Index 64 is out of bounds Breakpoints can be set by line (break 6), by file and line, by kernel name (break boundsBugAdd) or on every launch. info cuda warps and info cuda kernels list what is running. Lesson 4 of 6"],[24,"profile","Profiling with Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute","Tool Shows Use it Nsight Systems (nsys) the whole program timeline: CPU, API calls, copies, kernels per stream, NVTX ranges first: which kernel or copy takes the time? Nsight Compute (ncu) one kernel in depth: throughput, occupancy, traffic per memory level, stall reasons, roofline second: why is that kernel slow? compute-sanitizer correctness for wrong results, not speed nvvp, nvprof the old profilers not supported on compute capability 8.0 and newer Profile from the command line (works on Colab) nsys profile --stats=true ./a.outncu --kernel-name matrixMul ./a.outncu --set full -o mm_report ./a.outncu --kernel-name matrixMul python matrix_mul.py The lecture reads one report line by line. Metric Value Meaning SM frequency 880 MHz the profiler locks the clock to the base value Elapsed cycles 10,633 10,633 ÷ 880 MHz = 12.08 µs, matching the duration Duration 12.06 µs a tiny kernel: a launch costs about 5 µs Memory throughput 44.35 % the busiest memory unit at 44 % of its peak DRAM throughput 42.92 % of the T4's 320 GB/s SM active cycles 8,132 8132 ÷ 10,633 = 76 %: SMs idle a quarter of the time Compute (SM) throughput 36.98 % the busiest compute pipe at 37 % Stall reasons in Warp State Statistics tell you what the warps wait for: long scoreboard (global memory), barrier (__syncthreads), MIO throttle (shared memory pressure), not selected (another warp was chosen, which is fine). Other tools: CUDA events for timing a region, NVTX to name code ranges in the timeline, nvidia-smi for overall use, power and temperature, and CUPTI, the API all of these are built on. ipynb Notebook: CUDA C matmul and the profilersOne .cu file with naive and tiled kernels, events, compute-sanitizer, nsys and ncu.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 5 of 6"],[24,"ptx","Reading PTX and SASS","nvcc compiles device code in two stages. PTX is a virtual assembly for an imaginary GPU with unlimited registers. It is readable and stable across generations. SASS is the real machine code of one GPU generation, produced from PTX by ptxas or by the driver at run time. vec_add.cu __global__void vecAdd(constfloat *a, constfloat *b, float *c, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) c[i] = a[i] + b[i];} What each marked line does and why Line 2int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;The global index: blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x. Line 3if (i < n) c[i] = a[i] + b[i];Guard, two loads, one add, one store. Below is real PTX that a CUDA compiler (clang) produced for this kernel for sm_80. nvcc output looks very similar. Find each part of the C++ line in it. vec_add.ptx .visible .entry _Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi( .param .u64 _Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_0, .param .u64 _Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_1, .param .u64 _Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_2, .param .u32 _Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_3){ .reg .pred %p<2>; .reg .b32 %r<6>; .reg .f32 %f<4>; .reg .b64 %rd<11>; ld.param.u32 %r1, [_Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_3]; mov.u32 %r2, %ctaid.x; mov.u32 %r3, %ntid.x; mov.u32 %r4, %tid.x; mad.lo.s32 %r5, %r2, %r3, %r4; setp.ge.s32 %p1, %r5, %r1; @%p1 bra $L__BB0_2; ld.param.u64 %rd4, [_Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_0]; ld.param.u64 %rd5, [_Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_2]; cvta.to.global.u64 %rd6, %rd5; ld.param.u64 %rd7, [_Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_1]; cvta.to.global.u64 %rd8, %rd7; cvta.to.global.u64 %rd9, %rd4; mul.wide.s32 %rd10, %r5, 4; add.s64 %rd1, %rd6, %rd10; add.s64 %rd2, %rd8, %rd10; add.s64 %rd3, %rd9, %rd10; ld.global.f32 %f1, [%rd3]; ld.global.f32 %f2, [%rd2]; add.f32 %f3, %f1, %f2; st.global.f32 [%rd1], %f3;$L__BB0_2: ret;} What each marked line does and why Line 13ld.param.u32 %r1, [_Z6vecAddPKfS0_Pfi_param_3];Load the parameter n. Line 14mov.u32 %r2, %ctaid.x;%ctaid.x is blockIdx.x (CTA = cooperative thread array, the hardware name for a block). Line 15mov.u32 %r3, %ntid.x;%ntid.x is blockDim.x. Line 16mov.u32 %r4, %tid.x;%tid.x is threadIdx.x. Line 17mad.lo.s32 %r5, %r2, %r3, %r4;One multiply add builds the global index i. Line 18setp.ge.s32 %p1, %r5, %r1;Set a predicate: is i ≥ n? Line 19@%p1 bra $L__BB0_2;If so, jump to the end. This is the if (i < n) guard. Line 26mul.wide.s32 %rd10, %r5, 4;i × 4 bytes, widened to a 64 bit offset. Line 30ld.global.f32 %f1, [%rd3];Load a[i] from global memory. Line 31ld.global.f32 %f2, [%rd2];Load b[i]. Line 32add.f32 %f3, %f1, %f2;The one useful instruction: the add. Line 33st.global.f32 [%rd1], %f3;Store c[i]. SASS Meaning S2R R0, SR_CTAID.X read a special register (blockIdx.x) IMAD integer multiply add, for example the global index or an address LDG.E load from global memory with a 64 bit address FADD, FFMA float add, fused multiply add STG.E store to global memory ISETP, @P0 BRA compare into a predicate, then a branch taken only where the predicate holds See PTX and SASS yourself nvcc -ptx -arch=sm_75 mat_mul.cunvcc -cubin -arch=sm_75 mat_mul.cunvdisasm mat_mul.cubincuobjdump -sass ./mm Remember Lecture 7 showed the PTX of the naive matmul: two ld.global loads for every mad. Global loads cost hundreds of cycles, the multiply add only a few. That is the case for tiling and shared memory, read straight from the compiler output. Lesson 6 of 6"],[24,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A smoke alarm (CUDA_CHECK), a camera that watches every door (compute-sanitizer), a pause button (cuda-gdb) and a stopwatch with a map (Nsight). Check every call. memcheck, racecheck, initcheck, synccheck. cuda-gdb with -g -G. nsys for the timeline, ncu for one kernel. Under 60 % on both units means latency bound. Build with -lineinfo so Nsight Compute maps hot spots to source lines without -G. Use NVTX ranges. Compare against the roofline in ncu. CUPTI lets you build your own tools. Research profilers attribute stalls to source lines and suggest fixes. Performance regression tests run ncu metrics in continuous integration."],[25,"","GPU programming from Python","Numba, CuPy, Triton and PyTorch, mapped to CUDA C. · Numba · CuPy · Triton · PyTorch · Python GPU · JIT Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 9: Tools and Python on the GPU›GPU Python Step 9 · Topic 2 of 2 · College Write the same kernels from Python: Numba with every CUDA idea one to one, CuPy for NumPy style arrays, Triton for block level kernels, and PyTorch for streams and tensor cores. About 40 min6 lessons3 animations11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Five ways to reach the GPU from Python 2Numba: CUDA kernels written in Python 3CuPy: NumPy on the GPU 4Triton: think in blocks 5PyTorch: streams, pinned memory and tensor cores 6From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 6"],[25,"routes","Five ways to reach the GPU from Python","The course writes CUDA C++ because it shows threads, shared memory and streams directly. The same ideas are available from Python, at different levels. Library You write Good for CuPy NumPy style arrays on the GPU, plus element wise and raw CUDA kernels speeding up NumPy code with almost no change Numba kernels in Python with @cuda.jit, cuda.grid, shared arrays, atomics learning CUDA from Python, custom kernels PyCUDA CUDA C source strings, driver level control full control from Python Triton block level kernels, the compiler handles threads and tiling fast custom deep learning kernels (used by PyTorch) PyTorch tensors, torch.cuda streams, pinned memory deep learning, and most real GPU work in Python All of them compile just in time: the kernel is built at run time for the GPU that is actually present. Lesson 2 of 6"],[25,"numba","Numba: CUDA kernels written in Python","Every CUDA C idea has a Numba twin. CUDA C Numba __global__ void add(...) @cuda.jit above a Python function blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x cuda.grid(1) cudaMalloc + cudaMemcpy host to device cuda.to_device(a) cudaMalloc only cuda.device_array_like(a) add<<>>(...) add[blocks, threads](...) cudaMemcpy device to host d_c.copy_to_host() __shared__ float s[256] cuda.shared.array(256, dtype=float32) __syncthreads() cuda.syncthreads() atomicAdd(&out[0], v) cuda.atomic.add(out, 0, v) numba_add.py from numba import cudaimport numpy as np@cuda.jitdef add(a, b, c): i = cuda.grid(1)if i < c.size: c[i] = a[i] + b[i]n = 1_000_000a = np.arange(n, dtype=np.float32)b = 2 * ad_a, d_b = cuda.to_device(a), cuda.to_device(b)d_c = cuda.device_array_like(a)threads = 256blocks = (n + threads - 1) // threadsadd[blocks, threads](d_a, d_b, d_c)c = d_c.copy_to_host()print(c[:4]) What each marked line does and why Line 4@cuda.jitCompile this function for the GPU the first time it is called. Line 6i = cuda.grid(1)The global index, the same as blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x. Line 7if i < c.size:The same guard as in CUDA C, because the last block may stick out. Line 13d_a, d_b = cuda.to_device(a), cuda.to_device(b)Copy to the GPU: cudaMalloc plus cudaMemcpy. Line 14d_c = cuda.device_array_like(a)Allocate on the GPU only, no copy. Line 16blocks = (n + threads - 1) // threadsCeiling division gives enough blocks. Line 17add[blocks, threads](d_a, d_b, d_c)The launch: square brackets play the role of the triple angle brackets. Line 18c = d_c.copy_to_host()Copy back. This waits for the kernel to finish. What it prints [0. 3. 6. 9.] Shared memory, barriers and atomics work the same way. This block sum is the reduction of chapter 5.1 in Python. numba_sum.py from numba import cuda, float32import numpy as npTPB = 256@cuda.jitdef block_sum(x, out): s = cuda.shared.array(TPB, dtype=float32) t = cuda.threadIdx.x i = cuda.grid(1) s[t] = x[i] if i < x.size else0.0 cuda.syncthreads() stride = TPB // 2while stride > 0:if t < stride: s[t] += s[t + stride] cuda.syncthreads() stride //= 2if t == 0: cuda.atomic.add(out, 0, s[0])n = 1000d_x = cuda.to_device(np.ones(n, dtype=np.float32))d_out = cuda.to_device(np.zeros(1, dtype=np.float32))block_sum[(n + TPB - 1) // TPB, TPB](d_x, d_out)print(d_out.copy_to_host()[0]) What each marked line does and why Line 8s = cuda.shared.array(TPB, dtype=float32)A shared array, one per block, sized at compile time. Line 11s[t] = x[i] if i < x.size else 0.0Threads past the end load 0, the identity for addition. Line 12cuda.syncthreads()Everyone must finish loading before anyone reads a neighbour. Line 14while stride > 0:The tree: halve the stride each round. Line 17cuda.syncthreads()The barrier is outside the if, so every thread reaches it. Line 20cuda.atomic.add(out, 0, s[0])One atomic per block adds the block total to the result. What it prints 1000.0 Test without a GPU: Numba's CUDA simulator NUMBA_ENABLE_CUDASIM=1 python numba_sum.py What you will see 1000.0 Remember The simulator runs each GPU thread as a Python thread on the CPU. It is very slow but lets you test and debug kernels, including with print and pdb, on any laptop. The listings above were checked this way. ipynb Notebook: your first CUDA kernelsVector add in CUDA C and in Numba, with every line explained.Open it in Google Colab (Runtime, Change runtime type, T4 GPU) or in Jupyter on a machine with an NVIDIA GPU. Every code line is explained in the cell under it. Download notebook Lesson 3 of 6"],[25,"cupy","CuPy: NumPy on the GPU","CuPy arrays live in GPU memory and support most of NumPy. Each operation launches a ready made kernel. When you need something special, write it as an element wise expression or as raw CUDA C. cupy_demo.py import cupy as cpx = cp.arange(1_000_000, dtype=cp.float32)y = 2 * x + 1total = float(y.sum())saxpy = cp.ElementwiseKernel('float32 a, float32 x, float32 y', 'float32 z','z = a * x + y', 'saxpy')z = saxpy(cp.float32(2.0), x, y)square = cp.RawKernel(r\"\"\"extern\"C\"__global__void square(constfloat *x, float *y, int n) {int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (i < n) y[i] = x[i] * x[i];}\"\"\", 'square')out = cp.empty_like(x)square(((x.size + 255) // 256,), (256,), (x, out, cp.int32(x.size)))host = cp.asnumpy(out) What each marked line does and why Line 3x = cp.arange(1_000_000, dtype=cp.float32)Created directly in GPU memory. Line 4y = 2 * x + 1Each operator launches an element wise kernel (two here: multiply, then add). Line 5total = float(y.sum())The sum runs on the GPU. float() copies the single number back to the CPU. Line 7saxpy = cp.ElementwiseKernel(An element wise kernel from a one line C expression: inputs, output, body, name. Line 12square = cp.RawKernel(r\"\"\"Raw CUDA C, compiled at run time. The same kernel you would write in a .cu file. Line 18square(((x.size + 255) // 256,), (256,), (x, out, cp.int32(x.size)))Launch with a grid tuple, a block tuple and the arguments. Line 19host = cp.asnumpy(out)Copy the result back as a NumPy array. Remember Every separate operation reads and writes the whole array. 2 * x + 1 is two passes over memory. cp.fuse or an ElementwiseKernel does it in one pass, which matters because element wise work is memory bound. Lesson 4 of 6"],[25,"triton","Triton: think in blocks","In CUDA you write what one thread does. In Triton you write what one program does to a whole block of elements. The compiler maps the block to threads, vector loads and shared memory. PyTorch uses Triton to generate many of its fused kernels. triton_add.py import torchimport tritonimport triton.language as tl@triton.jitdef add_kernel(x_ptr, y_ptr, out_ptr, n, BLOCK: tl.constexpr): pid = tl.program_id(axis=0) offs = pid * BLOCK + tl.arange(0, BLOCK) mask = offs < n x = tl.load(x_ptr + offs, mask=mask) y = tl.load(y_ptr + offs, mask=mask) tl.store(out_ptr + offs, x + y, mask=mask)x = torch.rand(98432, device='cuda')y = torch.rand(98432, device='cuda')out = torch.empty_like(x)grid = (triton.cdiv(x.numel(), 1024),)add_kernel[grid](x, y, out, x.numel(), BLOCK=1024) What each marked line does and why Line 5@triton.jitCompile this as a Triton kernel. BLOCK is fixed at compile time. Line 7pid = tl.program_id(axis=0)Which program this is, like blockIdx.x. Line 8offs = pid * BLOCK + tl.arange(0, BLOCK)BLOCK indices at once, as a vector. Line 9mask = offs < nThe guard, for the whole block in one line. Line 10x = tl.load(x_ptr + offs, mask=mask)A masked load: lanes past the end are not read. Line 12tl.store(out_ptr + offs, x + y, mask=mask)A masked store. Line 17grid = (triton.cdiv(x.numel(), 1024),)Number of programs: the ceiling of n ÷ 1024. Lesson 5 of 6"],[25,"torch","PyTorch: streams, pinned memory and tensor cores","The ideas of chapter 6.2 and 8.1 appear in PyTorch with friendlier names. torch_overlap.py import torchx = torch.randn(4096, 4096).pin_memory()s = torch.cuda.Stream()with torch.cuda.stream(s): d = x.to('cuda', non_blocking=True) y = (d.half() @ d.half()).float()torch.cuda.synchronize()print(y.shape, y.dtype) What each marked line does and why Line 3x = torch.randn(4096, 4096).pin_memory()pin_memory puts the tensor in page locked host memory so the copy can run asynchronously. Line 4s = torch.cuda.Stream()A CUDA stream: an in order queue on the GPU. Line 5with torch.cuda.stream(s):Work inside this block goes into stream s. Line 6d = x.to('cuda', non_blocking=True)non_blocking=True returns at once, like cudaMemcpyAsync. Line 7y = (d.half() @ d.half()).float()An FP16 matmul runs on tensor cores, with FP32 accumulation inside. Line 8torch.cuda.synchronize()Wait for the GPU before using the result on the CPU. CUDA idea PyTorch cudaMallocHost tensor.pin_memory() cudaMemcpyAsync tensor.to(\"cuda\", non_blocking=True) cudaStream_t torch.cuda.Stream() cudaDeviceSynchronize torch.cuda.synchronize() TF32 tensor cores torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 = True custom kernel torch.compile (generates Triton) or a C++ extension Lesson 6 of 6"],[25,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths CuPy is like swapping a hand saw for a power saw with the same handle: NumPy code, GPU speed. Numba lets you build your own tool. Triton gives you a workshop that sets up the tools for you. Numba: @cuda.jit, cuda.grid(1), to_device, add[blocks, threads], copy_to_host, shared.array, syncthreads, atomic.add. Triton: program_id, arange, mask, load, store. Profile Python GPU code with nsys and ncu like any program. Watch for hidden copies between host and device, and for many tiny kernels: fuse them. torch.compile turns Python models into fused Triton kernels. JAX and XLA do the same from traced programs. Mojo and CUDA Python bring lower level control into Python syntax."],[26,"","Quiz 1 solved step by step","Quiz 1: all ten answers with steps, traps and variants. · Quiz 1 · solutions · matmul loads · segments · divergence · warps Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 10: Exam practice›Quiz 1 solved Step 10 · Topic 1 of 3 · Pro All ten Quiz 1 questions worked out with the reasoning, the traps and a widget for each, followed by new variants of every question for practice. About 40 min6 lessons7 animations11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The paper and the answers 2Q1, Q6 and Q8: peak, kernels and memory 3Q2 to Q4: loads and races in matrix multiply 4Q5: segments for a transposed store 5Q7, Q9 and Q10: tracing, divergence and warps 6From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 6"],[26,"paper","The paper and the answers","Quiz 1 had 10 questions on the first half of the course. The official answers, recomputed below, are: Q Topic Answer Chapter 1 peak FP32 throughput of one SM 2 (12 GFLOPS) 1.1, 4.2 2 global loads per block, naive matmul 524,288 5.2 3 global loads per block, tiled matmul 32,768 5.2 4 races in a tiled kernel without barriers 3 (both true) 5.2, 7.2 5 128 byte segments for a transposed store 32 3.2 6 return type of a kernel 5 (__global__ void) 1.4 7 trace a matrix vector kernel 13 2.1 8 memory visible to all SMs for the whole run 1 (global memory) 3.1 9 sum of thread ids in a divergent branch 120 6.1 10 warps launched 32,768 2.3 Python # Recompute every Quiz 1 answerprint(\"Q1:\", 4 * 2 * 1.5, \"GFLOPS per SM -> option 2\")N, threads = 1024, 16 * 16print(\"Q2:\", threads * 2 * N)print(\"Q3:\", (N // 16) * threads * 2)print(\"Q5:\", 32, \"segments (each thread lands\", 2048 * 4, \"bytes from its neighbour)\")A = [[1, 2, 1, 0], [0, 1, 3, 1], [2, 0, 1, 2], [1, 1, 0, 1]]x = [1, 2, 3, 4]print(\"Q7:\", sum(A[2][j] * x[j] for j inrange(4)))print(\"Q9:\", sum(t for t inrange(32) if t % 4 == 1))print(\"Q10:\", (1 << 20) // 256 * (256 // 32)) Output Q1: 12.0 GFLOPS per SM -> option 2 Q2: 524288 Q3: 32768 Q5: 32 segments (each thread lands 8192 bytes from its neighbour) Q7: 13 Q9: 120 Q10: 32768 Lesson 2 of 6"],[26,"q1","Q1, Q6 and Q8: peak, kernels and memory","Q1. 80 SMs, 64 cores each, 1.5 GHz, and \"every cycle each SM achieves the peak of 4 FMA operations per cycle\". Peak of one SM? Q1 Read the SM statement: one SM does 4 FMA per cycle. Each FMA = 2 FLOPs, so 8 FLOPs per cycle. 8 × 1.5 × 10⁹ = 12 × 10⁹. 12 GFLOPS, inside 10.5 to 12.5: answer 2 In the exam The other reading, 4 FMA per core, gives 64 × 4 × 2 × 1.5 = 768 GFLOPS per SM, which matches no option. When a question gives two numbers for the same thing, use the one that describes exactly what is asked (here the SM) and check the options. Q6. A kernel is marked __global__ and must return void: the CPU launches it and continues at once, so there is nobody waiting to receive a value. Results go back through memory. Answer 5. Qualifier Runs on Called from __global__ GPU CPU (a launch), must return void __device__ GPU GPU code only, any return type __host__ CPU CPU (the default) __shared__ not a function: a variable in shared memory Q8. Global memory is reachable by every SM and keeps its contents until freed or the program ends. Shared memory belongs to one block, and L1 and L2 are caches, not places you allocate. Answer 1. Lesson 3 of 6"],[26,"q2","Q2 to Q4: loads and races in matrix multiply","q2_naive.cu __global__void matMulNaive(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;if (row < N && col < N) {float sum = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N; k++) { sum += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col]; } C[row * N + col] = sum; }} What each marked line does and why Line 7sum += A[row * N + k] * B[k * N + col];Each pass of the loop loads one A and one B value from global memory. Line 9C[row * N + col] = sum;One store per thread. Q2: N = 1024, blocks of 16 × 16 Each thread runs the loop N = 1024 times. Each pass loads 2 values: 2048 loads per thread. 256 threads per block. 256 × 2048 = 524,288 loads per block Q3: tiled with 16 × 16 tiles The k loop runs N ÷ 16 = 64 phases. In each phase every thread loads 1 value of A and 1 of B into shared memory. 64 × 256 × 2. 32,768 loads per block, 16 times fewer Q4. The quiz kernel loads tiles and computes, with no barrier at all. q4_fixed.cu __global__void matMul(constfloat *A, constfloat *B, float *C, int N) {__shared__float tileA[16][16];__shared__float tileB[16][16];int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;float sum = 0.0f;for (int k = 0; k < N / 16; k++) { tileA[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = A[row * N + (k * 16 + threadIdx.x)]; tileB[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = B[(k * 16 + threadIdx.y) * N + col];__syncthreads(); // missing in the quiz: statement a becomes falsefor (int t = 0; t < 16; t++) { sum += tileA[threadIdx.y][t] * tileB[t][threadIdx.x]; }__syncthreads(); // missing in the quiz: statement b becomes false } C[row * N + col] = sum;} What each marked line does and why Line 8tileA[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = A[row * N + (k * 16 + threadIdx.x)];Each thread writes one element of each tile. Line 10__syncthreads(); // missing in the quiz: statement a becomes falseWithout this barrier a thread can start reading tileA[5][3] before thread (5, 3) wrote it. Statement a is true in the quiz. Line 14__syncthreads(); // missing in the quiz: statement b becomes falseWithout this barrier a fast thread starts the next phase and overwrites tileB while slower threads still read it. Statement b is true. In the exam Answer 3: both statements are true for the quiz code, because it has no __syncthreads. The fixed version above needs one barrier after loading and one after computing. Lesson 4 of 6"],[26,"q5","Q5: segments for a transposed store","q5.cu __global__void ValUpdate(float *A, float *B, int width, int height) {int x = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;int y = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;if (x < width && y < height) {float val = A[y * width + x]; // a warp reads 32 neighbours: 1 segment B[x * height + y] = val; // a warp writes 2048 floats apart: 32 segments }} What each marked line does and why Line 5float val = A[y * width + x]; // a warp reads 32 neighbours: 1 segmentThreads of one warp have x = 0 to 31 and the same y: 32 consecutive floats = 128 bytes = 1 segment. Line 6B[x * height + y] = val; // a warp writes 2048 floats apart: 32 segmentsThe same threads write B[x × 2048 + y]: neighbours are 2048 floats = 8192 bytes apart, so each one needs its own segment. Q5 A warp is 32 threads with consecutive x and one y. Store address for thread x: (x × 2048 + y) × 4 bytes. Neighbouring threads are 8192 bytes apart, far more than 128. 32 segments for one warp store The fix is the transpose trick of chapter 3.2: read a tile into shared memory, then write it out row by row, so both the read and the write are coalesced. Lesson 5 of 6"],[26,"q7","Q7, Q9 and Q10: tracing, divergence and warps","Q7. One thread per row of y = A x. Thread 2 owns row 2. Q9. With if (tid % 4 == 1), branch A is taken by tids 1, 5, 9, …, 29. Q9 The tids form 1 + 4k for k = 0 to 7: 8 terms. Sum of an arithmetic series = terms × (first + last) ÷ 2. 8 × (1 + 29) ÷ 2. 120 Q10. N = 2²⁰ threads in blocks of 256. Q10 Blocks = (2²⁰ + 255) ÷ 256 = 4096. Warps per block = 256 ÷ 32 = 8. 4096 × 8. 32,768 warps (the latencies in the question are not needed) Lesson 6 of 6"],[26,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Read each question twice, then draw one warp or one thread. Most quiz questions are about counting the right thing. Peak = units × 2 × clock. Naive matmul loads 2N per thread, tiled 2N ÷ T. Segments: look at neighbouring threads. Kernels return void. Warps = blocks × ⌈threads ÷ 32⌉. Check each answer two ways: by formula and by a tiny Python loop, as in the code above. The same counting is how researchers model kernels before writing them: count loads, bytes and FLOPs, then place the kernel on the roofline."],[27,"","Challenge problems","Hard multi step problems with full solutions. · challenge · hard problems · occupancy · bank conflicts · scan · tile size Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 10: Exam practice›Challenge problems Step 10 · Topic 2 of 3 · Pro Hard multi step problems in the style of graduate GPU and parallel computing courses, each with a full worked solution, a widget to check your picture, and code that recomputes every number. About 60 min8 lessons8 animations3 formulas11 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How to use these problems 2Problem 1: when does batching pay off? 3Problem 2: the occupancy puzzle 4Problem 3: banks, strides and padding 5Problem 4: work against steps in scan 6Problem 5: choose the tile under a budget 7Problem 6: divergence, overlap and scaling 8From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 8"],[27,"intro","How to use these problems","These problems are harder than the chapter exercises. Each one joins two or three ideas, the way graduate exams do. They are original problems written in the style of courses such as UIUC ECE 408, Stanford CS149 and CMU 15-418, not copies of their papers. Try each problem on paper first. Then open the steps. Use the widget to check your picture. The code below recomputes every answer on this page. Python # Recompute the answers of this chapterr = 989 / 3.35import math# AI = n B / (n + 2B) > r gives B > r n / (n - 2r)print(\"P1 H100 batch:\", math.floor(r * 4096 / (4096 - 2 * r)) + 1)r2 = 312 / 1.555print(\"P1 1024 layer on A100:\", math.floor(r2 * 1024 / (1024 - 2 * r2)) + 1)blocks = min(65536 // (40 * 256), 2048 // 256, 32, 164 // (20 + 1))print(\"P2 blocks:\", blocks, \" occupancy:\", blocks * 8 / 64)print(\"P4 Hillis-Steele adds:\", 1024 * 10 - 1023, \" Blelloch adds:\", 2 * 1023)print(\"P5 BM + BN limit:\", 48 * 1024 // (2 * 32 * 4))print(\"P6 divergence efficiency:\", round((8 * 20 + 24 * 10) / (32 * 30), 3))print(\"P7 Amdahl:\", round(1 / (0.05 + 0.95 / 1024), 2), \" Gustafson:\", round(0.05 + 0.95 * 1024, 2)) Output P1 H100 batch: 345 P1 1024 layer on A100: 330 P2 blocks: 6 occupancy: 0.75 P4 Hillis-Steele adds: 9217 Blelloch adds: 2046 P5 BM + BN limit: 192 P6 divergence efficiency: 0.417 P7 Amdahl: 19.64 Gustafson: 972.85 Lesson 2 of 8"],[27,"p1","Problem 1: when does batching pay off?","Problem. (a) An H100 SXM does 989 TFLOP/s in BF16 and moves 3.35 TB/s. From which batch is a 4096 × 4096 fully connected layer math limited? (b) Repeat for a 1024 × 1024 layer on an A100 (312 TFLOP/s, 1.555 TB/s). (c) Explain why the smaller layer needs a bigger batch. Solution (a) Ridge = 989 ÷ 3.35 = 295.2 FLOP/byte. Need 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B) > 295.2. (4096 − 590.4) B > 295.2 × 4096, so B > 344.9. B ≥ 345 Solution (b) and (c) For an n × n layer, AI = n B ÷ (n + 2B). With n = 1024 and ridge 200.6: 1024 B > 200.6 (1024 + 2B). 622.7 B > 205,455, so B > 329.9. Fewer weights means less reuse to spread the activation traffic over. B ≥ 330 for the 1024 layer, against 223 for the 4096 layer Lesson 3 of 8"],[27,"p2","Problem 2: the occupancy puzzle","Problem. A kernel on an A100 uses 256 threads per block, 40 registers per thread and 20 KB of shared memory per block. Which limit binds, and what is the occupancy? What is the cheapest change that reaches 100 percent? Solution Registers: 40 × 256 = 10,240 per block. 65,536 ÷ 10,240 = 6.4, so 6 blocks. Threads: 2048 ÷ 256 = 8 blocks. Block limit: 32. Shared memory: 20 KB + 1 KB reserved = 21 KB. 164 ÷ 21 = 7.8, so 7 blocks. The smallest is 6 (registers): 6 × 8 = 48 warps of 64. 75 percent, limited by registers To reach 8 blocks the kernel needs at most 65,536 ÷ (8 × 256) = 32 registers per thread (for example with __launch_bounds__(256, 8)), and at most 164 ÷ 8 − 1 = 19.5 KB of shared memory per block. Forcing registers down can cause spills to local memory, so measure before and after. Lesson 4 of 8"],[27,"p3","Problem 3: banks, strides and padding","Problem. Thread t of a warp reads s[t × k] from a float array in shared memory (32 banks of 4 bytes). (a) Give the conflict degree for k = 1, 2, 6, 8, 32. (b) Prove the rule. (c) A 32 × 32 tile is read by column. Show why padding each row to 33 floats removes the conflict. Conflict degree for stride kExam favourite degree = gcd(k, 32) Thread t goes to bank (t k) mod 32. Solution Threads t and u share a bank when (t − u) k is a multiple of 32, which means (t − u) is a multiple of 32 ÷ gcd(k, 32). So each bank is hit by gcd(k, 32) threads: k = 1 gives 1, k = 2 gives 2, k = 6 gives 2, k = 8 gives 8, k = 32 gives 32. Column read of a 32 × 32 tile: element (t, c) sits at 32 t + c, stride 32, a 32 way conflict. With rows of 33: address 33 t + c, stride 33, and gcd(33, 32) = 1. Padding to 33 makes every column read conflict free Lesson 5 of 8"],[27,"p4","Problem 4: work against steps in scan","Problem. An inclusive scan of n = 1024 values. (a) How many additions do Hillis and Steele and Blelloch each do? (b) How many parallel steps? (c) With 1024 threads, which one finishes first and which one is better on a GPU with far fewer lanes than n? Hillis and Steele adds = n log₂ n − (n − 1), steps = log₂ n Step d updates n − 2d elements. BlellochExam favourite adds = 2 (n − 1), steps = 2 log₂ n Up sweep then down sweep. Solution Hillis and Steele: 1024 × 10 − 1023 = 9217 additions in 10 steps. Blelloch: 2 × 1023 = 2046 additions in 20 steps. With enough lanes, Hillis and Steele wins on steps. With few lanes the total work decides, and Blelloch does 4.5 times less. Short arrays in one block: Hillis and Steele. Large arrays: Blelloch, which is work efficient Lesson 6 of 8"],[27,"p5","Problem 5: choose the tile under a budget","Problem. A FP32 matmul block keeps a BM × 32 strip of A and a 32 × BN strip of B in shared memory, double buffered, within 48 KB. (a) What is the largest BM + BN? (b) Which square tile maximises AI, and what AI does it reach? (c) At 2 TB/s, what speed can it reach from DRAM traffic alone? Solution Shared memory = 2 buffers × (BM + BN) × 32 × 4 bytes ≤ 49,152. So BM + BN ≤ 192. For a fixed sum, BM × BN is largest when BM = BN = 96. AI = (2 ÷ 4) × 96 × 96 ÷ 192 = 24 FLOP/byte. 24 × 2 TB/s = 48 TFLOP/s ceiling from DRAM traffic In practice tiles are powers of two (64 or 128) for simple indexing, so 64 × 128 (AI 21.3) or a smaller BK are chosen. That is the kind of trade off the matmul ladder makes at every step. Lesson 7 of 8"],[27,"p6","Problem 6: divergence, overlap and scaling","Problem A. In one warp, threads with tid < 8 run path A of 20 instructions, the others path B of 10. What is the warp time and the lane efficiency? How would you rearrange the data to remove the loss? Solution A Both paths run one after the other: 20 + 10 = 30 instruction slots. Useful lane slots: 8 × 20 + 24 × 10 = 400. Available: 32 × 30 = 960. 41.7 percent. Sort or group the work so each warp takes one path Problem B. 8 chunks pass through copy in (4 ms), kernel (6 ms) and copy out (2 ms), one engine each. Serial and overlapped time? Solution B Serial: 8 × 12 = 96 ms. The kernel is the slowest stage: 4 ms to fill, 8 × 6 ms of kernels, 2 ms to drain. 4 + 48 + 2 = 54 ms. Speedup 96 ÷ 54 ≈ 1.78 Problem C. A program is 95 percent parallel. What speedup do Amdahl and Gustafson predict on 1024 cores, and why do they differ? Solution C Amdahl (fixed problem): 1 ÷ (0.05 + 0.95 ÷ 1024) = 19.6. Gustafson (problem grows with the machine): 0.05 + 0.95 × 1024 = 972.9. Amdahl keeps the work fixed, so the serial 5 percent dominates. Gustafson grows the parallel part, which is how GPUs are really used Lesson 8 of 8"],[27,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths Hard problems are several easy ones joined together. Find the pieces first. Write down the limit formulas you need: ridge, occupancy, gcd for banks, scan work, tile AI, pipeline time, Amdahl. Always check with a second method: a small Python loop, a widget, or a limiting case such as n = 1. Research papers on GPU kernels are built from exactly these models, then confirmed with Nsight measurements."],[28,"","Exam revision","Formula sheets, slide corrections, traps and mixed problems. · revision · formula sheet · slide corrections · exam traps · mixed problems Brainy BytesGPU Programming learning path GPU Programming›Step 10: Exam practice›Exam revision Step 10 · Topic 3 of 3 · Pro The whole subject on a few screens: a one week plan, three formula sheets, every slide correction, the traps that cost marks, and mixed problems with fresh numbers. About 45 min8 lessons10 animations24 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How to revise in one week 2Formula sheet 1: speed and limits 3Formula sheet 2: threads and memory 4Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats 5Every slide correction in one table 6The traps that cost marks 7Mixed problems with fresh numbers 8From intuition to research Lesson 1 of 8"],[28,"plan","How to revise in one week","This chapter collects the whole subject on a few screens. Use it the week before the exam, after you have worked through the chapters. Day What to do Chapters 1 Speed and limits: speedup, Amdahl, peak, roofline. Redo the formula cards below from memory. 1.1 to 1.3, 4.2 2 Threads: indexing forwards and backwards, warps, occupancy. Do the launch problem below by hand. 2.1 to 2.4 3 Memory: hierarchy, coalescing, banks, tiling. Draw one warp and count sectors. 3.1 to 3.3, 5.2 4 Patterns: reduction, stencils, convolution, scan, histogram. 5.1 to 5.5 5 Concurrency: divergence, streams, races, locks, barriers, atomics. 6.1 to 7.2 6 Modern GPUs: number formats, tensor cores, fast matmul, tools. 4.1, 8.1 to 9.2 7 Quiz 1 again under time, then three challenge problems. 10.1, 10.2 In the exam Most lost marks come from counting the wrong thing: threads instead of warps, loads per thread instead of per block, bytes one way instead of both. Write the unit next to every number. The code below recomputes the mixed problems at the end of this chapter. Python import mathpeak = 40 * 64 * 2 * 1.59# T4 FP32 GFLOP/sprint(\"T4 peak:\", round(peak, 1), \" ridge:\", round(peak / 320, 2))print(\"blur naive and cached:\", 17 / 40 * 320, 17 / 8 * 320)n, B = 1_000_000, 256blocks = math.ceil(n / B)print(\"blocks:\", blocks, \" idle:\", blocks * B - n, \" warps:\", blocks * B // 32,\" waves:\", math.ceil(blocks / (40 * (1024 // B))))print(\"reduction adds:\", 2048 * 511, \" plus atomics:\", 2048)print(\"Amdahl 80 percent at 50x:\", round(1 / (0.2 + 0.8 / 50), 2)) Output T4 peak: 8140.8 ridge: 25.44 blur naive and cached: 136.0 680.0 blocks: 3907 idle: 192 warps: 31256 waves: 25 reduction adds: 1046528 plus atomics: 2048 Amdahl 80 percent at 50x: 4.63 Lesson 2 of 8"],[28,"limits","Formula sheet 1: speed and limits","Speedup and efficiencyExam favourite S = Told ÷ Tnew, E = S ÷ N Count copy in, kernel and copy out in Tnew. Amdahl (fixed problem)Exam favourite S(N) = 1 ÷ ((1 − P) + P ÷ N), ceiling 1 ÷ (1 − P) P is the parallel fraction of the old time. Gustafson (problem grows) S(N) = (1 − P) + P N Weak scaling: the parallel work grows with N. Peak FLOP/sExam favourite peak = SMs × cores per SM × 2 × clock The 2 is one FMA per core per cycle. Little's law in flight = latency × throughput Warps needed = in flight ÷ 32. Bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency. Arithmetic intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes moved Count bytes read and written. RooflineExam favourite attainable = min(peak, AI × bandwidth), ridge = peak ÷ bandwidth Left of the ridge: memory bound. Right: compute bound. Time bound t ≥ max(FLOPs ÷ peak, bytes ÷ bandwidth) The larger term tells you what to fix. Lesson 3 of 8"],[28,"threads","Formula sheet 2: threads and memory","Global index and blocksExam favourite i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x, blocks = ⌈N ÷ B⌉ Guard with i < N. Idle threads = blocks × B − N. Going backwards block = ⌊g ÷ (BxBy)⌋, x = r mod Bx, y = ⌊r ÷ Bx⌋ r is the leftover g − block × BxBy. WarpsExam favourite warps per block = ⌈B ÷ 32⌉, lane = t mod 32 t = x + y Bx + z BxBy, x moves fastest. OccupancyExam favourite blocks per SM = min(threads, blocks, registers, shared memory limits) Occupancy = active warps ÷ max warps per SM. Coalescing sectors per warp load = distinct 32 byte pieces touched Floats: stride 1 gives 4 sectors, stride 8 or more gives 32. Bank conflictsExam favourite degree = gcd(stride, 32) Same word for all lanes is a broadcast. Pad rows to 33. Matmul loadsExam favourite naive 2N³, tiled 2N³ ÷ T Per thread 2N against 2N ÷ T. Tile AI in FP32 is T ÷ 4. Halo tiles loads per output = (B + 2R)d ÷ Bd d is the number of dimensions, R the radius. Lesson 4 of 8"],[28,"patterns","Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats","Reduction treeExam favourite log₂ B levels, B − 1 additions, 1 + log₂ B barriers One atomic per block joins the blocks. ScansExam favourite Hillis and Steele n log₂ n − (n − 1) adds in log₂ n steps, Blelloch 2(n − 1) adds in 2 log₂ n steps Blelloch does less work, Hillis and Steele has fewer steps. Divergence efficiency = busy lane ticks ÷ (32 × total ticks) Paths inside one warp run one after the other. PipelineExam favourite T = (a + b + c) + (n − 1) × max(a, b, c) Equal stages give speedup 3n ÷ (n + 2). Waves waves = ⌈blocks ÷ (SMs × blocks per SM)⌉ A last wave that is almost empty wastes most of the GPU. Float valueExam favourite x = (−1)s × 1.m × 2e − bias, bias = 2E − 1 − 1 FP32 1/8/23, FP16 1/5/10, BF16 1/8/7, TF32 1/8/10. Symmetric INT8 s = max|x| ÷ 127, q = round(x ÷ s) Error at most s ÷ 2 unless a value is clipped. Interleavings (n s)! ÷ (s!)n n threads with s steps each. Two threads of 3 steps: 20 orders. Lesson 5 of 8"],[28,"corrections","Every slide correction in one table","While writing this subject every worked example in the notes was recomputed. These are the places where the slides or the class notes say something that does not hold. Each chapter explains the fix in full. Where The slide or class said What is right Chapter Lecture 3, slide 16 Global thread 343 has threadIdx (2, 3, 0) It is (3, 3, 0). (2, 3, 0) is thread 342. 2.2 Lecture 3, mapping Grids sit inside cores A thread runs on a core lane, a block on one SM, a grid across the GPU. 2.3 Lecture 4, slide 11 Asynchrony is risky because kernels overwrite each other Work in one stream runs in order. The risk is the CPU reading before the GPU is done. 6.2 Lecture 5 notes 1.00101 in binary = 1.5625 1.00101 in binary = 1.15625 = 37 ÷ 32. 4.1 Lecture 6, slide 14 Heat weights 0.25 centre and 0.1 for 6 neighbours They add to 0.85, so heat leaks away. The centre must be 0.4. 5.3 Lecture 7, slide 6 Threads 0 to 21 write out of bounds All 32 threads write out of bounds (indices 10 to 41). 9.1 Lecture 7, slide 8 The plain matmul reads Width² values Each of the Width² threads reads 2 × Width, so 2 × Width³ in total. 5.2 Lecture 8 Sharpening pixel 30 gives 35 5 × 30 − (25 + 35 + 20 + 40) = 30. 5.4 Lecture 9, slide 11 Bars show exact format speeds The bars are illustrative. TF32 is a compute mode, not a storage format. 8.1 Lecture 9, slide 16 The C tile lives in shared memory In real kernels the C tile stays in registers. Only the epilogue uses shared memory. 8.2 Lecture 9 blog numbers Ridge about 410 With the numbers used in class the ridge is about 378. 8.3 Lecture 10, slide 8 Attempt 2 deadlocks Attempt 2 breaks mutual exclusion. Attempt 3 is the one that deadlocks. 7.1 Lecture 10, class Peterson does not solve the problem Peterson is correct when memory is in order, so on a GPU it needs fences. 7.1 Lecture 11 code critical_kernel is safe as written It can hang before Volta and needs __threadfence. The priority counter skips levels. 7.2 In the exam If an exam question copies one of these slides, answer with the right value and add one line saying why. Markers reward the reasoning. Lesson 6 of 8"],[28,"traps","The traps that cost marks","Trap Do this instead Counting threads when the question asks for warps Divide by 32 and round up per block, then multiply by blocks. Loads per thread when the question says per block Multiply by threads per block. Quiz 1 question 2: 256 × 2048 = 524,288. Forgetting the write in bytes moved SAXPY reads x and y and writes y: 12 bytes per element. Taking FMA as 1 FLOP One FMA is 2 FLOPs, a multiply and an add. Putting __syncthreads inside a divergent if Every thread of the block must reach every barrier. Treating occupancy as the goal Enough warps to hide latency is enough. Measure, do not maximise. Reading GB as 230 bytes in bandwidth Bandwidth uses 109 bytes per GB. Forgetting the reserved 1 KB of shared memory per block On CC 8.x, blocks = ⌊pool ÷ (bytes + 1 KB)⌋. Thinking divergence happens across warps Different warps may take different paths for free. Only lanes inside one warp diverge. Amdahl with N where the question gives a speedup of a part Use 1 ÷ ((1 − p) + p ÷ s) with s the speedup of that part. Lesson 7 of 8"],[28,"mixed","Mixed problems with fresh numbers","Each problem joins two or more chapters. The numbers are new, so you cannot remember the answer, you have to work it out. Problem A. A T4 has 40 SMs with 64 FP32 cores each at 1.59 GHz and 320 GB/s. A 3 × 3 blur does 9 multiplies and 8 adds per pixel. Is it memory or compute bound, without and with perfect reuse of input pixels? Peak = 40 × 64 × 2 × 1.59 = 8140.8 GFLOP/s. Ridge = 8140.8 ÷ 320 = 25.44 FLOP/byte. Without reuse each pixel reads 9 floats and writes 1: 40 bytes for 17 FLOPs, AI = 0.425. Attainable 0.425 × 320 = 136 GFLOP/s. With perfect reuse each pixel reads 1 float and writes 1: 8 bytes, AI = 2.125, attainable 680 GFLOP/s. Memory bound either way. Tiling gives up to 5 times, but the ceiling is still 680 of 8141 GFLOP/s Problem B. Launch one thread per element for N = 1,000,000 with 256 threads per block on that T4 (1024 threads and 16 blocks per SM). Give the blocks, idle threads, total warps and waves. Blocks = ⌈1,000,000 ÷ 256⌉ = 3907. Threads launched = 1,000,192, so 192 are idle. Warps = 3907 × 8 = 31,256. Blocks per SM: threads allow 1024 ÷ 256 = 4, the block cap allows 16, so 4. One wave holds 40 × 4 = 160 blocks. Waves = ⌈3907 ÷ 160⌉ = 25, the last one 42 percent full Problem C. Sum 220 floats with 512 threads per block, a tree in shared memory and one atomic per block. Count blocks, levels, barriers per block and all additions. Blocks = 220 ÷ 512 = 2048. Levels = log₂ 512 = 9. Barriers = 1 after loading + 9 = 10. Additions inside blocks = 2048 × 511 = 1,046,528. Atomics = 2048. Together 1,048,576 = 220, one addition per input value counting the add onto the zero total. Same work as a serial sum, in 9 levels instead of 1,048,575 steps Problem D. You sum 4096 ones in FP16. What do you get, and why does a tensor core avoid it? FP16 has 10 mantissa bits, so the gap between numbers at 2048 is 211 − 10 = 2. 2048 + 1 = 2049 lies halfway, and round to nearest even gives 2048. Every later add is lost. Tensor cores take FP16 inputs but add in FP32, where the gap at 2048 is far below 1. FP16 sum = 2048, FP32 accumulator = 4096 Problem E. Copies take 20 percent of a job and the kernel takes 80 percent. You make the kernel 50 times faster. What is the overall speedup, and what should you do next? Speedup of a part: 1 ÷ ((1 − p) + p ÷ s) with p = 0.8 and s = 50. 1 ÷ (0.2 + 0.016) = 1 ÷ 0.216 = 4.63. The copies are now 0.2 ÷ 0.216 = 93 percent of the time. 4.63 times. Next: keep data on the GPU or overlap the copies with streams Lesson 8 of 8"],[28,"levels","From intuition to research","One idea, four depths A GPU is fast when many small jobs run at once and the data is close by. Almost every formula here counts one of those two things. For any kernel, write down threads, warps, bytes, FLOPs and the limiting resource. Then pick the formula. Combine models: roofline for the ceiling, occupancy and Little's law for latency hiding, waves for the tail, Amdahl for the copies. Research on GPU kernels checks these models against Nsight measurements and then attacks the gap, as in the fast matmul ladder of chapter 8.3."],[29,"","Cheatsheet","Every formula, key idea, table, memory aid and common mistake from the GPU Programming chapters on one page, from latency, Amdahl and thread indexing to occupancy, coalescing, bank conflicts, the roofline, tiling, reductions, streams, atomics and tensor cores. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · global thread index · warps · occupancy · Amdahl · coalescing · bank conflicts · roofline · arithmetic intensity · tiled matmul · reduction · tensor cores"],[29,"step-1","Step 1Why GPUs","1.1"],[29,"why-gpus","Why GPUs: latency, throughput and power","Open chapter CPU against GPU design, latency and throughput, FPS and batch time, clock and power, peak FLOPs, and the hardware spectrum. Key ideas Two designs CPU: big control and caches, few strong ALUs, low latency. GPU: thousands of small ALUs, small control, high throughput. Latency and throughput Latency = time for one job. Throughput = jobs ÷ time. FPS = 1000 ÷ ms per frame. Batch time = ⌈N ÷ C⌉ × time per round. Power P ∝ α C V² f. Higher clock needs higher voltage, so power explodes. GPUs use many cores at a moderate clock. Peak Peak = FLOPs per tick per core × clock × cores. FMA = 2 FLOPs. T4 ≈ 8.1, A100 FP32 ≈ 19.5 TFLOP/s. Hardware spectrum CPU → GPU → FPGA → ASIC: less flexible, more efficient. TPU and NPU are ASIC style AI accelerators. The CPU is always the host. Formulas Latency and throughputExam favourite Latency = time to finish ONE job Throughput = jobs finished ÷ time taken A CPU is built for low latency. A GPU is built for high throughput. Lesson: Latency, throughput and frames per second Frames per second FPS = 1000 ÷ (ms per frame) ms per frame = 1000 ÷ FPS There are 1000 milliseconds in one second. Lesson: Latency, throughput and frames per second Time for a batchExam favourite T = ⌈N ÷ C⌉ × (time per round) N jobs, C jobs done together in one round. ⌈ ⌉ means round up, because a part full round still costs a whole round. Lesson: Latency, throughput and frames per second Pipeline latency = sum of stage times throughput = 1 ÷ (slowest stage) Once the line is full, one result comes out every time the slowest stage finishes. Lesson: Latency, throughput and frames per second Cycle time tcycle = 1 ÷ f 1 GHz = 10⁹ ticks per second, so one tick lasts 1 ns. At 800 MHz a tick lasts 1.25 ns. Lesson: Clock speed, power and peak work Dynamic powerExam favourite Pdyn ∝ α · C · V² · f α is how often the circuit switches, C its capacitance, V the voltage (squared), f the clock. A faster clock needs a higher voltage, so power grows much faster than speed. Lesson: Clock speed, power and peak work Peak work per secondExam favourite peak = (FLOPs per tick per core) × f × cores One FMA, a × b + c, counts as 2 FLOPs. Lesson: Clock speed, power and peak work Host and device Host = CPU (starts the program, owns the operating system) Device = GPU (runs the parallel parts you send it) Every GPU system is driven by a CPU. CUDA is the library and language extension that lets the host hand work to the device. Lesson: CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA and ASIC Tables Table: part of the chip · cpu · gpu Part of the chip CPU GPU Control logic (decides what runs next) Large and clever: branch prediction, out of order execution Small and simple, shared by a group of threads Cache (fast memory on the chip) Large per core Small per core, plus a shared L2 ALUs (the units that add and multiply) A few, powerful Thousands, small and energy efficient Clock High, about 2 to 5 GHz Moderate, about 0.8 to 2 GHz Built for Low latency: one task, fast High throughput: many tasks per second Lesson: Two ways to build a processor Table: platform · strength · weakness · examples from the lecture Platform Strength Weakness Examples from the lecture CPU Most flexible, cheapest, runs any program Limited parallelism Arm Cortex-M, Raspberry Pi GPU Massive parallelism, good programmability Power hungry NVIDIA Jetson, T4, A100, Arm Mali FPGA Custom circuits, low latency, good work per watt, can be reprogrammed Small on chip memory, needs hardware design skill Xilinx Zynq, Intel Arria 10 ASIC (TPU, NPU) Lowest power, highest performance for one task Very costly to design, long time to market, fixed Google Edge TPU, Ascend 310 Lesson: CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA and ASIC Tips and traps Common mistake GPU does not mean \"always faster\". A GPU wins only when there is enough independent work to keep thousands of small units busy. For a single small task the CPU usually wins.Lesson: Two ways to build a processor Remember Peak FLOP/s is a ceiling, not a promise. Real kernels"],[29,"why-gpus","Why GPUs: latency, throughput and power","usually reach a fraction of it, because they wait for memory. Chapter 4.2 (the roofline) shows how to predict that fraction.Lesson: Clock speed, power and peak work In the exam A GPU never runs alone. The CPU boots the machine, runs the serial parts and launches GPU work. This is why the time to copy data between them matters so much (chapter 1.2).Lesson: CPU, GPU, TPU, NPU, FPGA and ASIC 1.2"],[29,"speedup-amdahl","Speedup, the PCIe trip and Amdahl's law","Open chapter Speedup with copies counted, Amdahl's law and its ceiling, working backwards, Gustafson's law, and break even sizes. Key ideas Speedup S = old ÷ new. Count copy in + kernel + copy out. Copy time = bytes ÷ bandwidth (PCIe about 12 to 16 GB/s). Amdahl S(N) = 1 ÷ ((1 − P) + P/N). Ceiling 1 ÷ (1 − P). Efficiency S ÷ N. Back: P = (1 − 1/S) ÷ (1 − 1/N). Part speedup Speeding a part p by s gives 1 ÷ ((1 − p) + p/s). Speed up the biggest part first. Gustafson Scaled speedup N − (1 − P)(N − 1) when the problem grows with N (weak scaling). Keep data on the GPU Copy once, run many kernels, copy back once. Small jobs have a break even size below which the CPU wins. Formulas SpeedupExam favourite S = old time ÷ new time S = 10 means the new version is ten times faster. With FPS, use new FPS ÷ old FPS. Lesson: Speedup and the trip over PCIe Total GPU timeExam favourite TGPU = Tcopy in + Tkernel + Tcopy out The GPU is worth it only if TGPU < TCPU. Lesson: Speedup and the trip over PCIe Copy time Tcopy = bytes ÷ bandwidth PCIe 3.0 x16 gives about 12 to 16 GB/s in practice. 12 GB/s = 12 × 10⁹ bytes per second. Lesson: Speedup and the trip over PCIe Amdahl's lawExam favourite S(N) = 1 ÷ [ (1 − P) + P ÷ N ] P is the parallel fraction measured on one processor, N the number of processors. Lesson: Amdahl's law The ceilingExam favourite Smax = 1 ÷ (1 − P) Let N grow without limit. 95 percent parallel can never beat 20 times. Lesson: Amdahl's law Working backwards P = (1 − 1/S) ÷ (1 − 1/N) From a measured speedup S on N processors. Lesson: Amdahl's law Efficiency E = S ÷ N How much of each processor is really used. Lesson: Amdahl's law Speeding up one part s times S = 1 ÷ [ (1 − p) + p ÷ s ] The same law for any part p that gets s times faster, for example a kernel. Lesson: Amdahl's law Gustafson's law (scaled speedup)Exam favourite Sscaled = N − (1 − P)(N − 1) Here P is the parallel share of the time measured on the N processor run. The speedup grows almost in a straight line with N. Lesson: Gustafson's law and scaling (beyond the lecture) Tables Table: grid · cpu (ms) · gpu (ms) · shared gpu (ms) Grid CPU (ms) GPU (ms) Shared GPU (ms) GPU speedup 256 × 256 51.55 0.48 0.53 107.76 512 × 512 236.54 1.48 1.87 160.28 1024 × 1024 906.35 5.34 6.13 169.79 2048 × 2048 3593.75 17.75 16.32 202.44 Lesson: Speedup and the trip over PCIe Table: · strong scaling (amdahl) · weak scaling (gustafson) Strong scaling (Amdahl) Weak scaling (Gustafson) What is fixed Total problem size Work per processor Question asked How much faster does the same job finish? How much bigger a job fits in the same time? Limit 1 ÷ (1 − P) Grows with N GPU example One 1024 × 1024 grid on more SMs Grid grows from 1024² to 4096² with a bigger GPU Lesson: Gustafson's law and scaling (beyond the lecture) Tips and traps Common mistake Keep data on the GPU across many kernel launches. Copy in once, run all time steps, copy out once. Copying every step can turn a 158 times speedup into 12 times (workbook P1.3.5).Lesson: Speedup and the trip over PCIe Common mistake P is a fraction of the time on one processor, not a fraction of the lines of code. A tiny loop that runs for 90 percent of the time gives P = 0.9.Lesson: Amdahl's law In the exam In an exam, Amdahl is the default unless the question says the problem grows with the number of processors.Lesson: Gustafson's law and scaling (beyond the lecture) 1.3"],[29,"first-workloads","First GPU workloads: heat simulation and convolution","Open chapter The heat simulation update rule and its stability, why it suits a GPU, and normal against depthwise separable convolution cost. Key ideas Heat rule T_new = T + α(up + down + left + right − 4T) + βQ. 1-D uses −2T. Updates = cells × steps. Why parallel Each cell needs only old neighbour values, so one thread per cell. Keep two buffers (old and new). Stability Explicit scheme: α ≤ 1/2 in 1-D, 1/4 in 2-D, 1/6 in 3-D, or values blow up. Convolution cost Normal H W Cin Cout K². Separable H W Cin K² + H W Cin Cout. Ratio (K² + Cout) ÷ (K² Cout) → 1/K². Same pattern Heat updates and convolutions are stencils: each output reads a small neighbourhood. Tiling with halos speeds them up later. Formulas The heat equation ∂T/∂t = α (∂²T/∂x² + ∂²T/∂y²) + Q T is temperature, α the thermal diffusivity (how fast heat spreads), Q the heat the battery makes itself. Lesson: Workload 1: a battery heat simulation 2-D update rule (5 points)Exam favourite Tnew = T + α (up + down + left + right − 4T) + βQ New temperature = old temperature + heat from the four neighbours + heat made inside. Lesson: Workload 1: a battery heat simulation 1-D version Tnew = T + α (left + right − 2T) + βQ The same idea along a rod. Lesson: Workload 1: a battery heat simulation How much work cells = height × width updates = cells × time steps A 1024 × 1024 grid has 1,048,576 cells per step. Lesson: Workload 1: a battery heat simulation Normal convolutionExam favourite mults = H × W × Cin × Cout × K × K Every output pixel of every output channel looks at a K × K patch of every input channel. Lesson: Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost Depthwise separable depthwise = H W CinK² pointwise = H W CinCout First one K × K filter per input channel, then a 1 × 1 convolution that mixes channels. MobileNet uses this. Lesson: Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost Saving ratioExam favourite separable ÷ normal = (K² + Cout) ÷ (K² × Cout) For many output channels this tends to 1 ÷ K², so at most 9 times fewer for 3 × 3 filters. Lesson: Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost Tips and traps Common mistake A GPU heat code must keep two buffers. If threads wrote into the same grid they read from, some would read a neighbour's new value and some its old value, and the answer would depend on timing.Lesson: Workload 1: a battery heat simulation Remember Fewer multiplications does not always mean proportionally faster on a GPU. Depthwise layers do little work per byte loaded, so they are often limited by memory, not maths (chapter 4.2).Lesson: Workload 2: image recognition and convolution cost"],[29,"first-workloads","Step 2The CUDA programming model","2.1"],[29,"first-cuda-program","Your first CUDA program","Open chapter Host and device, the CUDA recipe, __global__ void kernels, vector add line by line, nvcc, asynchronous launches, error checks and unified memory. Key ideas Host and device Host = CPU + RAM, device = GPU + its DRAM. Separate memories, so copy with cudaMemcpy (or use unified memory). Recipe cudaMalloc, cudaMemcpy H2D, kernel<<<blocks, threads>>>, cudaMemcpy D2H, cudaFree. blocks = (n + B − 1) / B. Qualifiers __global__ void kernel (host launches), __device__ helper (device only), __host__ default. Kernels cannot return values. Guard if (i < n) because the last block can overhang the array. Asynchronous Launch returns at once. cudaMemcpy and cudaDeviceSynchronize wait. Check errors with cudaGetLastError after a launch. Tables Table: qualifier · runs on · called from · notes Qualifier Runs on Called from Notes __global__ device host (and device with dynamic parallelism) A kernel. Must return void. Launched with <<< >>> __device__ device device A helper function used inside kernels. May return any type __host__ host host The default for normal functions __host__ __device__ both both Compiled twice, handy for small maths helpers Lesson: Host, device and the recipe every program follows Tips and traps In the exam Quiz 1 question 6: the correct kernel signature is __global__ void. A kernel cannot return a value, because thousands of threads run it and the launch returns to the host at once without waiting. Results go back through memory. __shared__ is a memory space, not a function qualifier.Lesson: Host, device and the recipe every program follows Common mistake The order of the six lines is not guaranteed. Blocks may run in any order, so block 1 can print first. Never write code that depends on the order blocks run in.Lesson: Hello world, asynchronous launches and unified memory 2.2"],[29,"thread-indexing","Threads, blocks and grids: indexing in 1-D, 2-D and 3-D","Open chapter The global index, blocks and idle threads, dim3 and 2-D image indexing, going backwards, grid stride loops and matrix vector per row. Key ideas Global index i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x. Blocks = (N + B − 1)/B. Guard i < N. Idle = blocks × B − N. 2-D col = bx·Bx + tx, row = by·By + ty, index = row × width + col. Grid = (⌈W/Bx⌉, ⌈H/By⌉), guard both. Linear order thread = tx + ty·Bx + tz·Bx·By (x fastest). Warps are cut from this order. Backwards block = ⌊g ÷ per block⌋, leftover = g mod per block, x = leftover mod Bx, y = ⌊leftover ÷ Bx⌋. Grid stride Start at i = idx, keep going while i < n, and add blockDim.x × gridDim.x each time. A fixed grid then covers any N. Formulas Global indexExam favourite i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x Skip all the full blocks before mine, then step to my seat in my block. Lesson: The global index in one dimension Blocks neededExam favourite blocks = ⌈N ÷ B⌉ = (N + B − 1) ÷ B Whole number division drops the remainder, so adding B − 1 first rounds up. Lesson: The global index in one dimension Going backwards block = ⌊i ÷ B⌋, thread = i − block × B Which thread handles element i. Lesson: The global index in one dimension Idle threads idle = blocks × B − N They fail the guard and do nothing. Lesson: The global index in one dimension Pixels in an imageExam favourite col = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x row = blockIdx.y × blockDim.y + threadIdx.y memory position = row × width + col Images are stored row by row (row major). x is the column, y is the row. Lesson: dim3, images and two dimensional grids Grid for a W × H image grid = (⌈W ÷ Bx⌉, ⌈H ÷ By⌉) Idle threads = all threads − W × H. Lesson: dim3, images and two dimensional grids Position to one number (x fastest)Exam favourite thread = tx + tyBx + tzBxBy block = bx + byGx + bzGxGy global = block × (threads per block) + thread This is how the hardware lines threads up before cutting them into warps. Lesson: dim3, images and two dimensional grids One number to position block = ⌊g ÷ threads per block⌋, leftover = g − block × threads per block x = leftover mod Bx, y = ⌊leftover ÷ Bx⌋ The same mod and divide works for the block inside the grid. Lesson: Three dimensions, going backwards, and the slide correction Tables Table: variable · meaning · range Variable Meaning Range threadIdx.x/y/z my position inside my block 0 to blockDim − 1 blockIdx.x/y/z my block's position inside the grid 0 to gridDim − 1 blockDim.x/y/z size of every block set by the launch, at most 1024 threads in total gridDim.x/y/z number of blocks x up to 2³¹ − 1, y and z up to 65,535 Lesson: The global index in one dimension Tips and traps Remember Using 32 × 8 blocks for 1920 × 1080 wastes nothing (1080 ÷ 8 = 135) and each warp covers one full 32 pixel row. Picking a block shape that divides the image is free speed.Lesson: dim3, images and two dimensional grids In the exam Exams love going backwards. Always divide by the number of threads per block first, then split the leftover with mod and divide, x first.Lesson: Three dimensions, going backwards, and the slide correction Common mistake In matVecMul neighbouring threads read A[i × N + j] for the same j, which are N floats apart. That is a strided, uncoalesced read (chapter 3.2). A transposed layout or one warp per row is faster for large N.Lesson: Grid stride loops and one thread per row 2.3"],[29,"warps-sms","SMs, warps and hiding latency","Open chapter SM anatomy, warps and lanes, wasted lanes, Little's law and latency hiding, and registers per thread. Key ideas Mapping Thread → core lane, warp → scheduler, block → one SM for life, grid → whole GPU. Warps warps = ⌈B/32⌉, warp = ⌊t/32⌋, lane = t mod 32, t = x + y·Bx. Efficiency B ÷ (32 × warps). Little's law In flight = latency × throughput. Warps = that ÷ 32. Bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency. Latency hiding Zero cost warp switching because registers stay on chip. Need many warps or independent instructions. Registers Registers per thread = file ÷ threads. 65,536 on modern SMs. Too many spill to slow local memory. Formulas Warps per blockExam favourite warps = ⌈B ÷ 32⌉ A part full warp still takes 32 lanes. Lesson: Warps, lanes and SIMT Warp and lane of thread tExam favourite warp = ⌊t ÷ 32⌋, lane = t − 32 × warp For 2-D blocks first compute t = x + y × Bx. Lesson: Warps, lanes and SIMT Wasted lanes wasted = 32 × warps − B efficiency = B ÷ (32 × warps) Use block sizes that are multiples of 32. Lesson: Warps, lanes and SIMT Little's lawExam favourite jobs in flight = latency (ticks) × throughput (jobs per tick) To keep a pipe full you need as many jobs inside it as its length times its width. Lesson: Hiding latency: Little's law In warps warps in flight = (latency × operations per tick) ÷ 32 One warp instruction is 32 operations. Lesson: Hiding latency: Little's law Memory version bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency To use all the bandwidth, this many bytes must be on the way at every moment. Lesson: Hiding latency: Little's law Registers per thread registers per thread = registers on the SM ÷ threads on the SM Lecture example: 32K registers for 1024 threads gives 32 each. Lesson: Registers: the hidden limit Binary K 32K = 32 × 1024 = 32,768 64K = 65,536 For registers K means 1024. Lesson: Registers: the hidden limit Bits to name a register smallest b with 2b ≥ number of registers An instruction names its operands, so 32 registers need 5 bits, 255 need 8. Lesson: Registers: the hidden limit Tables Table: software (what you write) · hardware (what runs it) · rule Software (what you write) Hardware (what runs it) Rule Thread A CUDA core (one lane) Has its own registers and program counter Warp of 32 threads A warp scheduler Issues one instruction for all 32 lanes Block (up to 1024 threads) One SM Stays on that SM until it finishes, shares its shared memory Grid (all blocks of a launch) The whole GPU Blocks spread over all SMs in any order Lesson: Streaming multiprocessors: what really runs your threads Table: fermi sm part (lecture 1) · count · job Fermi SM part (lecture 1) Count Job CUDA cores 32 Integer and floating point units, one lane each Warp schedulers and dispatch units 2 Two warps can issue at the same time Register file 32,768 × 32 bit Private variables of every resident thread Load/store units 16 Addresses for 16 threads per clock Special function units 4 sin, cos, reciprocal, square root Shared memory / L1 64 KB Split 48/16 or 16/48 between shared memory and L1 Lesson: Streaming multiprocessors: what really runs your threads Tips and traps Common mistake In class it was said that grids sit inside cores. It is the other way round: a thread runs on a core, a block on one SM, and a grid across all SMs.Lesson: Streaming multiprocessors: what really runs your threads Remember Latency hiding needs either many warps (thread level parallelism) or several independent instructions inside each warp (instruction level parallelism). Two independent loads per warp halve the warps needed.Lesson: Hiding latency: Little's law 2.4"],[29,"occupancy","Occupancy: how many warps fit on an SM","Open chapter Occupancy from four limits, register rounding, shared memory reserve, launch bounds and the occupancy API. Key ideas Occupancy Active warps ÷ max warps per SM. Blocks = min(warp limit, block limit, register limit, shared memory limit). Block limit Small blocks hit the block cap. Smallest block for 100 percent = max threads ÷ max blocks (A100: 64). Registers Per warp = ⌈32r ÷ 256⌉ × 256. Blocks = ⌊⌊65,536 ÷ per warp⌋ ÷ warps per block⌋. Shared memory Blocks = ⌊pool ÷ (bytes + 1 KB reserved)⌋ on CC 8.x. Not the goal Enough warps to hide latency is enough. Measure achieved occupancy in Nsight Compute. Formulas OccupancyExam favourite occupancy = active warps per SM ÷ maximum warps per SM A100: maximum 64 warps (2048 threads). 48 active warps is 75 percent. Lesson: What occupancy means Blocks that fitExam favourite blocks = min(warp limit, block limit, register limit, shared memory limit) Every resource must fit at the same time, so the tightest one decides. Lesson: What occupancy means Smallest block for full occupancy Bmin = max threads per SM ÷ max blocks per SM A100: 2048 ÷ 32 = 64 threads. Anything smaller cannot reach 100 percent. Lesson: Limit 1 and 2: threads and blocks Registers per warpExam favourite per warp = ⌈(r × 32) ÷ 256⌉ × 256 r = registers per thread. 37 registers give 1184, rounded up to 1280, the same as 40. Lesson: Limit 3: registers, with rounding Register limit in blocksExam favourite blocks = ⌊⌊65,536 ÷ per warp⌋ ÷ warps per block⌋ First whole warps, then whole blocks. Lesson: Limit 3: registers, with rounding Shared memory limitExam favourite blocks = ⌊shared memory per SM ÷ (bytes per block + reserved)⌋ CC 8.6: 102,400 bytes per SM, 1024 reserved per block. Lesson: Limit 4: shared memory Tables Table: gpu (compute capability) · max threads · max warps · max blocks GPU (compute capability) Max threads Max warps Max blocks Registers Shared memory per SM Fermi (2.0) 1536 48 8 32,768 48 KB T4 (7.5) 1024 32 16 65,536 64 KB A100 (8.0) 2048 64 32 65,536 up to 164 KB RTX 30xx (8.6) 1536 48 16 65,536 up to 100 KB H100 (9.0) 2048 64 32 65,536 up to 228 KB Lesson: What occupancy means Tips and traps Remember The flag -maxrregcount=N caps registers for every kernel in a file. __launch_bounds__ is better because it is per kernel and says why.Lesson: Limit 3: registers, with rounding Remember The printed numbers above are what this code gives on an A100 by the four limit rule. They were worked out, not measured on a real GPU.Lesson: Limit 4: shared memory"],[29,"step-3","Step 3GPU memory","3.1"],[29,"memory-hierarchy","The GPU memory hierarchy","Open chapter Memory spaces, scope and lifetime, constant memory, caches, pinned and unified memory, copy time. Key ideas Spaces Register (thread), local (thread, in DRAM), shared (block), global (all, host, until cudaFree), constant (all, read only). Q8 Visible to all SMs for the whole application: global memory. L2 is a cache, not a space. Constant 64 KB, host writes with cudaMemcpyToSymbol, broadcast when a warp reads one address. Host memory Pageable malloc, pinned cudaMallocHost (needed for async overlap), managed cudaMallocManaged with prefetch. Copies Copy time = bytes ÷ bandwidth. PCIe is far slower than device DRAM. Copy once, reuse many times. Formulas Copy timeExam favourite t = bytes ÷ bandwidth The PCIe link is far slower than the GPU's own DRAM, so copy once and reuse. Lesson: Host memory: pageable, pinned and unified Worth sending to the GPU tcopy in + tkernel + tcopy out < tCPU From chapter 1.2: count the copies too. Lesson: Host memory: pageable, pinned and unified Tables Table: memory · who sees it · lives for · speed Memory Who sees it Lives for Speed How you get it Registers one thread the thread about 1 tick any normal local variable Local one thread the thread DRAM speed (cached) big private arrays, spilled registers Shared all threads of one block the block about 20 to 30 ticks __shared__ Global all threads, all kernels, the host until cudaFree about 400 to 800 ticks cudaMalloc Constant all threads, read only the application fast when the warp reads one address __constant__ and cudaMemcpyToSymbol Lesson: Five places a value can live Table: cache · where · what it holds Cache Where What it holds L1 / texture per SM, shares space with shared memory recent global and local loads Constant cache per SM recent constant memory reads L2 one for the whole GPU all traffic to device DRAM (40 MB on an A100) Lesson: Constant memory and caches Tips and traps Common mistake Local memory is not near the thread. \"Local\" means private. It lives in device DRAM and is only cached on chip.Lesson: Five places a value can live In the exam A memory space is something you name and address. A cache is a hardware copy of global data that fills and empties by itself. This difference decides questions like Q8.Lesson: Quiz 1 question 8: visible to all SMs, for the whole application 3.2"],[29,"coalescing","Coalescing: reading global memory well","Open chapter Sectors per warp read, stride and alignment, Quiz 1 Q5, sector model, AoS and SoA, transposes. Key ideas Sectors Warp loads are coalesced into 32 byte sectors. Efficiency = 32 × element ÷ (sectors × 32). Strides Floats: stride 1 → 4 sectors, 2 → 8, 4 → 16, ≥ 8 → 32. Misaligned start adds a sector. Row major Let threadIdx.x move along the row. Q5 store B[x·h + y] touches 32 segments. Sector model Useful bandwidth ≈ stride 1 bandwidth ÷ min(s, 8). T4 206 GB/s at stride 1. Fixes SoA instead of AoS, float4 loads, and shared memory tiles for transposes. Formulas Bytes the warp wantsExam favourite useful = 32 × element size Floats: 128 bytes. Doubles: 256 bytes. Lesson: Memory hands out 32 byte boxes EfficiencyExam favourite efficiency = useful bytes ÷ (sectors × 32) Stride 1 floats: 128 ÷ (4 × 32) = 100 percent. Lesson: Memory hands out 32 byte boxes Sectors for stride s (floats, aligned) sectors = min(32, 4 × s) Each thread lands in its own sector once s ≥ 8. Lesson: Memory hands out 32 byte boxes Sector modelExam favourite useful bandwidth ≈ (stride 1 bandwidth) ÷ min(s, 8) Once threads are 8 floats apart each lane needs its own sector, and the model stops falling. Real GPUs fall further at big strides because of DRAM page and TLB effects. Lesson: The stride benchmark (lecture 11) Vector loads float4: 32 lanes × 16 bytes = 512 bytes = 16 sectors, 100 percent One instruction moves four floats per thread, so fewer instructions for the same bytes. The address must be 16 byte aligned. Lesson: Data layout: AoS against SoA, and wider loads Tables Table: thread t reads · neighbouring lanes are · sectors per warp read (floats) Thread t reads Neighbouring lanes are Sectors per warp read (floats) A[row][t] (along a row) 4 bytes apart 4 (100 percent) A[t][col] (down a column of width 1024) 4096 bytes apart 32 (12.5 percent) Lesson: Rows, columns and Quiz 1 question 5 Table: stride · model (gb/s) · sectors per warp read Stride Model (GB/s) Sectors per warp read 1 206 4 2 103 8 4 51.5 16 8 25.75 32 16 25.75 32 32 25.75 32 Lesson: The stride benchmark (lecture 11) Tips and traps Remember AoS is natural for the CPU and often slow on the GPU. When kernels touch only some fields, store each field in its own array.Lesson: Data layout: AoS against SoA, and wider loads 3.3"],[29,"shared-memory","Shared memory and bank conflicts","Open chapter Shared memory use, barriers, 32 banks, gcd conflict rule, broadcast, padding and layout. Key ideas Shared memory __shared__, one copy per block, fill then __syncthreads() then read. extern __shared__ size is the third launch value. Banks 32 banks × 4 bytes. bank = ⌊byte ÷ 4⌋ mod 32. Different words in one bank take turns. Conflicts Stride s gives gcd(s, 32) ways. Same word is a broadcast, no conflict. Padding Tile [32][33] makes column reads conflict free for 128 extra bytes. Layout Let lanes write consecutive words: powers example 1024 passes against 32. Formulas Bank of an addressExam favourite bank = ⌊byte address ÷ 4⌋ mod 32 Word w lives in bank w mod 32. Byte 260 is word 65, bank 1. Lesson: Banks and conflicts Conflict for stride sExam favourite ways = gcd(s, 32) Odd strides are always conflict free. Lesson: Banks and conflicts Broadcast same word for many lanes → no conflict One read is sent to every lane that asked for it. Lesson: Banks and conflicts Tips and traps Common mistake __syncthreads() must be reached by every thread of the block. Putting it inside an if that only some threads enter can hang the block or give wrong results.Lesson: Shared memory: a cache you control"],[29,"step-4","Step 4Numbers and performance limits","4.1"],[29,"floating-point","Floating point numbers on the GPU","Open chapter IEEE-754 encoding, binary fractions, float addition, non associativity, FMA and reduced precision formats. Key ideas Format value = (−1)^S × 1.f × 2^(E − bias). Float 1/8/23 bias 127, double 1/11/52 bias 1023. Exact Exact in binary only when the denominator is a power of 2. 5.6 is stored as 5.599999904632568. Add Align exponents, add mantissas, normalise, round. Not associative: compare with a tolerance. FMA a × b + c with one rounding, one instruction, counted as 2 FLOPs. Formats FP16 1/5/10 (max 65,504), BF16 1/8/7, TF32 1/8/10. Epsilon = 2^−mantissa bits. Formulas IEEE-754 valueExam favourite value = (−1)S × 1.f × 2E − bias bias = 127 for float (8 exponent bits), 1023 for double (11 bits). Lesson: Sign, exponent and mantissa Binary fraction 0.b₁b₂b₃… = b₁ × ½ + b₂ × ¼ + b₃ × ⅛ + … 1.00101₂ = 1 + ⅛ + 1/32 = 1.15625. Lesson: Sign, exponent and mantissa Exact or not a decimal fraction is exact in binary only if its denominator is a power of 2 0.625 = 5/8 is exact. 0.6 = 3/5 repeats for ever. Lesson: Sign, exponent and mantissa Machine epsilon ε = 2−mantissa bits FP32: 2⁻²³ ≈ 1.19 × 10⁻⁷. The gap between 1 and the next float. Relative rounding error is at most ε ÷ 2. Lesson: Encoding by hand Tables Table: format · sign · exponent · mantissa Format Sign Exponent Mantissa About this many decimal digits FP64 double 1 11 52 16 FP32 float 1 8 23 7 TF32 (tensor cores) 1 8 10 3 BF16 1 8 7 2 to 3 FP16 half 1 5 10 3 Lesson: Sign, exponent and mantissa Table: exponent field · mantissa · meaning Exponent field Mantissa Meaning 0 0 ±0 0 not 0 subnormal (tiny numbers, no hidden 1) 1 to 254 anything normal number 255 0 ±infinity (overflow, 1 ÷ 0) 255 not 0 NaN (0 ÷ 0, √−1) Lesson: Special values and precision on GPUs Tips and traps Common mistake The lecture 5 notes give 1.00101₂ = 1.5625. Adding the bits gives 1 + 0.125 + 0.03125 = 1.15625 = 37/32, which is the value the workbook uses.Lesson: Sign, exponent and mantissa In the exam A GPU reduction adds numbers in a different order from a CPU loop, so float sums can differ in the last digits. That is expected. Compare float results with a tolerance, never with ==.Lesson: Adding floats, and why order matters 4.2"],[29,"roofline","FLOPs, bandwidth and the roofline","Open chapter FLOPs and peak, bandwidth, arithmetic intensity, roofline and ridge point, reading benchmarks. Key ideas FLOPs Matmul 2n³, k × k filter 2k² per output. Peak = SMs × cores × 2 × clock. FMA = 2 FLOPs. Bandwidth GB/s = (read + written bytes) ÷ 10⁹ ÷ s. Count both directions. AI FLOPs ÷ bytes. SAXPY 1/6, plain matmul 0.25, perfect reuse n/8 float, n/16 double. Roofline Attainable = min(peak, AI × B). Ridge = peak ÷ B. Left memory bound, right compute bound. Time bound t ≥ max(FLOPs ÷ peak, bytes ÷ bandwidth). Memory bound kernels gain nothing from more cores. Formulas Matrix multiply n × nExam favourite FLOPs = 2n³ Each of n² outputs needs n multiplies and n adds. Lesson: Counting the work: FLOPs Filter k × k FLOPs = 2k² × outputs A 5 × 5 filter on 1020 × 1020 outputs: 52,020,000. Lesson: Counting the work: FLOPs Peak of a GPUExam favourite peak = SMs × cores per SM × 2 (FMA) × clock A100: 108 × 64 × 2 × 1.41 GHz ≈ 19.5 TFLOP/s. Lesson: Counting the work: FLOPs Efficiency achieved ÷ peak A simple kernel at 3171 GFLOP/s on an H100 with about 51,000 is about 6.2 percent. Lesson: Counting the work: FLOPs BandwidthExam favourite GB/s = (bytes read + bytes written) ÷ 10⁹ ÷ seconds Count both directions. Lesson: Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity Arithmetic intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes moved to or from DRAM Unit FLOP per byte. Lesson: Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity SAXPY AI = 2 ÷ 12 ≈ 0.167 Read x, read y, write y (3 × 4 bytes) for one multiply and one add. DAXPY is half that. Lesson: Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity Matrix multiply no reuse: 2 FLOPs per 2 loads → 0.25 (float), perfect reuse: AI = n ÷ 8 (float) or n ÷ 16 (double) Reuse is what raises AI. Lesson: Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity Attainable speedExam favourite attainable = min(peak FLOP/s, AI × bandwidth) The lower roof wins. Lesson: The roofline model Ridge pointExam favourite AIridge = peak ÷ bandwidth Left of it a kernel is memory bound, right of it compute bound. Lesson: The roofline model Time t ≥ max(FLOPs ÷ peak, bytes ÷ bandwidth) The shortest possible time is set by the slower of the two. Lesson: The roofline model Tables Table: kernel · flops per element · bytes per element (float) · ai Kernel FLOPs per element Bytes per element (float) AI y = a·x 1 8 0.125 SAXPY y = a·x + y 2 12 0.167 dot product (double) 2 16 0.125 y = x³ + x 3 8 0.375 Lesson: Bandwidth and arithmetic intensity Table: picture · cpu (ms) · gpu kernel (ms) · speedup Picture CPU (ms) GPU kernel (ms) Speedup Lena 512 × 512 13.59 0.188 about 72 Kid 47.27 0.336 about 141 Lesson: Reading benchmark numbers (lecture 4 Sobel) Tips and traps In the exam For a memory bound kernel, doubling the maths units changes nothing. Only more bandwidth or more reuse helps. Doubling Fermi's cores gives plain matrix multiply no gain.Lesson: The roofline model"],[29,"roofline","Step 5Parallel patterns","5.1"],[29,"reduction","Parallel reduction","Open chapter Tree reduction, pairing and divergence, barrier rule, grid level reductions, atomics and warp shuffles. Key ideas Tree log₂ n levels, n − 1 additions, 1 + levels barriers. 1024 → 10 levels, 11 barriers. Pairing Sequential (t < s, s halves) packs active threads: only levels with s < 32 split a warp. Barrier rule Every thread must reach every __syncthreads(). Never put it inside a divergent if. Grid One atomicAdd per block (⌈n/B⌉ atomics) or repeated launches until one value is left. Shuffles __shfl_down_sync with offsets 16 to 1 sums a warp in 5 steps without shared memory. Formulas LevelsExam favourite levels = ⌈log₂ n⌉ 1024 numbers need 10 levels instead of 1023 serial additions. Lesson: From a serial loop to a tree AdditionsExam favourite total additions = n − 1 The tree does the same work, it only runs it in parallel. Lesson: From a serial loop to a tree Barriers barriers = 1 (after loading) + levels A 1024 thread tree uses 11 __syncthreads(). Lesson: From a serial loop to a tree Launches without atomics repeat: n ← ⌈n ÷ B⌉ until n = 1 1,000,000 numbers with B = 256: 3907, then 16, then 1, so 3 launches. Lesson: Beyond one block: launches and atomics Atomics neededExam favourite one per block = ⌈n ÷ B⌉ 2²⁰ numbers with B = 1024: 1024 atomics instead of 1,048,576. Lesson: Beyond one block: launches and atomics Tips and traps Remember Floating point addition is not associative (chapter 4.1), so the tree order gives a slightly different float sum from a CPU loop. Compare with a tolerance.Lesson: Warp shuffles: the modern way 5.2"],[29,"tiled-matmul","Tiled matrix multiplication","Open chapter Plain against tiled loads, Quiz 1 Q2 to Q4, two barriers, ragged edges and tile limits. Key ideas Plain 2N loads per thread, 2N³ total (not N² as slide 8 says). Q2: 524,288 per block. Tiled ⌈N/T⌉ phases, 2⌈N/T⌉ loads per thread, 2N³/T total. Q3: 32,768 per block. Barriers Sync after loading the tile and again before the next phase. Q4: without them both a and b happen (answer 3). Ragged Pad out of range loads with 0 and guard the store. n = 5, T = 4: 4 blocks, 39 idle. Limits T² ≤ 1024 threads so T ≤ 32. Shared memory 8T² bytes. AI ≈ T/4. Formulas Loads per thread (plain)Exam favourite 2N N from A and N from B. Lesson: The plain kernel and its traffic Loads per block (plain)Exam favourite threads per block × 2N Quiz 1 Q2: 256 × 2048 = 524,288. Lesson: The plain kernel and its traffic Total loads (plain) 2N³ Not N² as lecture 7 slide 8 says. For N = 1024 that is 2048 times more. Lesson: The plain kernel and its traffic PhasesExam favourite phases = ⌈N ÷ T⌉ N = 1024, T = 16: 64 phases. Lesson: Tiles in shared memory Loads per thread (tiled)Exam favourite 2 × ⌈N ÷ T⌉ One A value and one B value per phase. Lesson: Tiles in shared memory Total loads (tiled)Exam favourite 2N²⌈N ÷ T⌉ = 2N³ ÷ T when T divides N Global traffic drops by a factor of T. Lesson: Tiles in shared memory Arithmetic intensity AI ≈ T ÷ 4 (float) Plain matmul has 0.25. T = 16 gives 4. Lesson: Tiles in shared memory Shared memory for two tiles bytes = 2 × T² × 4 T = 32: 8 KB. 48 KB allows T up to 78. Lesson: Ragged edges and tile size limits Thread limit T² ≤ 1024 So T ≤ 32 when each thread computes one output. Lesson: Ragged edges and tile size limits Tips and traps Common mistake Lecture 7 slide 8 states the plain kernel reads Width² values. Each of the Width² threads reads 2 × Width values, so the total is 2 × Width³.Lesson: The plain kernel and its traffic 5.3"],[29,"stencils","Stencils, halos and heat simulation","Open chapter Stencil patterns, halos, reads per output, 3-D tile intensity, heat step stability. Key ideas Stencil Each output from a fixed neighbour pattern. 2-D: ±1, ±W. 3-D: ±1, ±N, ±N². Halo Tile B + 2R. Reads per output (B + 2R)/B against 2R + 1. Barrier after loading. 3-D tiles AI = 13(T − 2)³/(4T³) ≤ 3.25. Halo share 57.8 percent at T = 8. Memory bound. Heat Tnew = (1 − 6λ)T + λΣ6. Weights must add to 1 (slide 14 used 0.25 + 0.6). Stable for λ ≤ 1/6. Ping-pong Read the old grid, write the new grid, swap pointers. Formulas Neighbours in 2-DExam favourite N = i − W, S = i + W, W = i − 1, E = i + 1 Row major with width W. Pixel (row 10, col 20) of a 640 wide image is 6420. Lesson: What a stencil is Neighbours in 3-DExam favourite ± 1, ± N, ± N² Index i = (z × N + y) × N + x. Lesson: What a stencil is Tile sizeExam favourite B + 2R values R = 3, B = 256: 262. Lesson: Halos in shared memory Global reads per outputExam favourite (B + 2R) ÷ B with the tile, 2R + 1 without R = 3, B = 256: 1.023 against 7, about 6.8 times fewer. Lesson: Halos in shared memory Without tiling AI = 13 ÷ 28 ≈ 0.46 7 multiplies and 6 adds, 7 floats loaded. Lesson: 3-D tiles: why big tiles still stay memory bound With T × T × T tilesExam favourite AI(T) = 13 × (T − 2)³ ÷ (4T³) Only the inner (T − 2)³ points make outputs. Lesson: 3-D tiles: why big tiles still stay memory bound Halo shareExam favourite (T³ − (T − 2)³) ÷ T³ T = 8: 57.8 percent of loaded points are border. Lesson: 3-D tiles: why big tiles still stay memory bound 3-D explicit heat stepExam favourite Tnew = (1 − 6λ) T + λ × (sum of 6 neighbours) λ = α Δt ÷ h². Weights add to 1, so a constant field stays constant. Lesson: The heat equation and stability StabilityExam favourite λ ≤ 1/6 in 3-D, 1/4 in 2-D, 1/2 in 1-D Above it the centre weight is negative and values oscillate and grow. Lesson: The heat equation and stability Largest time step Δt ≤ h² ÷ (6α) α = 0.0001, h = 0.01 gives Δt ≤ 0.167 s. Lesson: The heat equation and stability Tables Table: stencil · points · example Stencil Points Example 1-D radius R 2R + 1 moving sum, R = 3 gives 7 2-D plus (radius 1) 5 heat in a plate 2-D box radius r (2r + 1)² blur, r = 2 gives 25 3-D plus (radius 1) 7 heat in a block 3-D box radius 1 27 smoothing a volume Lesson: What a stencil is Tips and traps Common mistake Lecture 6 slide 14 uses 0.25 for the centre and 0.1 for each of the 6 neighbours. They add to 0.85, so a field of 100 drops to 85 every step. With neighbour weight 0.1 the centre must be 1 − 6 × 0.1 = 0.4.Lesson: The heat equation and stability 5.4"],[29,"convolution","Convolution and Sobel edge detection","Open chapter Convolution sizes and work, Sobel, constant memory filters, halo tiles and savings. Key ideas Size out = ⌊(W + 2P − K)/S⌋ + 1. Same padding P = (K − 1)/2. Work MACs = out² K² Cin Cout. FLOPs = 2 × MACs. Sobel Gx, Gy 3 × 3. M1 = |Sx| + |Sy| clamped to 255. Weights in constant memory. Tiles Tile (B + 2r)², loads per output (B + 2r)²/B², best saving 1 − 1/K². Correction Sharpening 30 with 25, 35, 20, 40 gives 30, not 35. Smooth slopes do not change. Formulas Output sizeExam favourite out = ⌊(W + 2P − K) ÷ S⌋ + 1 W input width, K filter size, P padding, S stride. Lesson: Convolution: slide, multiply, add \"Same\" padding P = (K − 1) ÷ 2 3 × 3 needs 1, 5 × 5 needs 2. Lesson: Convolution: slide, multiply, add WorkExam favourite multiply-adds = out² × K² × Cin × Cout AlexNet layer 1: 55² × 11² × 3 × 96 = 105,415,200. Lesson: Convolution: slide, multiply, add Edge strengthExam favourite M1 = |Sx| + |Sy|, M2 = √(Sx² + Sy²) M1 is cheaper and is what the lecture code uses. Both are clamped to 255. Lesson: Sobel edge detection (lecture 4) Tile sideExam favourite B + 2r, with r = (K − 1) ÷ 2 B = 32, 3 × 3: 34 × 34. Lesson: Halo tiles for convolution Loads per outputExam favourite (B + 2r)² ÷ B² Against K² without a tile. B = 16, 5 × 5: 1.5625 against 25. Lesson: Halo tiles for convolution Saving 1 − (B + 2r)² ÷ (K² B²) Best possible for large B: 1 − 1/K². 3 × 3 gives 88.9 percent. Lesson: Halo tiles for convolution Tables Table: picture · cpu · gpu kernel · speedup Picture CPU GPU kernel Speedup Lena 512 × 512 13.59 ms 0.188 ms about 72 Kid 47.27 ms 0.336 ms about 141 8K frame not given 1.65 ms (8.66 ms with copies) copies are 81 percent Lesson: Sobel edge detection (lecture 4) Tips and traps Common mistake Lecture 8 sharpens the pixel 30 at (2, 1) to 35. With neighbours 25, 35, 20 and 40 the result is 5 × 30 − 120 = 30. On a smooth slope sharpening changes nothing.Lesson: Convolution: slide, multiply, add 5.5"],[29,"scan-histogram","Scan, compaction and histograms","Open chapter Scans, work efficiency, compaction, histograms with privatised atomics. Key ideas Scan Inclusive y_i = x_0 + … + x_i. Exclusive starts at 0 and stops before x_i. Algorithms Hillis and Steele: log₂ n steps, n log₂ n − (n − 1) adds. Blelloch: 2 log₂ n steps, 2(n − 1) adds. Race Never scan in place in one step. Double buffer or barrier between read and write. Compaction Exclusive scan of keep flags gives each kept value its output slot. Histogram Atomics per bin. Privatise in shared memory, merge with at most blocks × bins global atomics. Formulas Inclusive scanExam favourite yi = x0 + x1 + … + xi [3, 1, 7, 0] → [3, 4, 11, 11]. Lesson: Prefix sums: inclusive and exclusive Exclusive scanExam favourite yi = x0 + … + xi−1, y0 = 0 [3, 1, 7, 0] → [0, 3, 4, 11]. The inclusive scan shifted right by one. Lesson: Prefix sums: inclusive and exclusive Global atomics without privatisation one atomic per element n = 10⁶ bytes → 10⁶ global atomics, all on 256 addresses. Lesson: Histograms and atomic contention Privatised (shared memory) histogramExam favourite global atomics = blocks × bins at most Each block counts in fast shared memory, then merges once. Lesson: Histograms and atomic contention Tables Table: algorithm · steps · additions · note Algorithm Steps Additions Note Serial loop n − 1 n − 1 one thread Hillis and Steele log₂ n n log₂ n − (n − 1) simple, extra work Blelloch 2 log₂ n about 2(n − 1) work efficient, exclusive Lesson: Prefix sums: inclusive and exclusive Table: use · how the scan helps Use How the scan helps Stream compaction positions of kept items Radix sort positions of keys with bit 0 and bit 1 Sparse matrices (CSR) row start offsets from row lengths Allocating variable output each thread's write offset Lesson: What scans are for: compaction Tips and traps Common mistake Updating one array in place with buf[t] += buf[t − off] is a race: thread t may read buf[t − off] after thread t − off has already added to it. Double buffering or a barrier between the read and the write fixes it.Lesson: A block scan in CUDA"],[29,"step-6","Step 6Execution and overlap","6.1"],[29,"divergence-scheduling","Warp divergence and warp scheduling","Open chapter Divergence cost, Quiz 1 Q9, branch free tricks, warp schedulers, hiding waits. Key ideas Divergence Inside a warp only. Time = sum of used path lengths. Efficiency = lane ticks ÷ (32 × time). Q9 tid % 4 == 1 → lanes 1, 5, …, 29, sum 120. Avoid it Branch on warp number, use selects, keep data dependent branches short. Schedulers RR waits together, GTO spreads loads (best = (instructions − 1) + L), two level in between. Hiding Warps = ⌈(W + c) ÷ c⌉. More ILP per warp means fewer warps needed. Formulas Warp timeExam favourite time = sum of the lengths of every path at least one lane takes Paths no lane takes are skipped. Lesson: Warp divergence EfficiencyExam favourite efficiency = (sum over lanes of their path length) ÷ (32 × warp time) 8 lanes on a 10 tick path and 24 on a 30 tick path: 800 ÷ 1280 = 62.5 percent. Lesson: Warp divergence Worst case 32 different paths: 32 × L, efficiency 1/32 = 3.1 percent A 32 times slowdown. Lesson: Warp divergence Rules (workbook 7.4) one instruction per tick, a load at tick t lets the warp go again at t + L finish = tick of the last load + L. Lesson: Warp schedulers Best possible finishExam favourite finish ≥ (total instructions − 1) + L 4 warps × 12 instructions, L = 12: 47 + 12 = 59. GTO reaches it. Lesson: Warp schedulers Warps to hide a waitExam favourite warps = ⌈(W + c) ÷ c⌉ Work 1 tick, wait 3: 4 warps. One warp leaves 75 percent idle, two leave 50 percent. Lesson: How many warps hide the wait? Tables Table: policy · rule · strength · weakness Policy Rule Strength Weakness Round robin (RR) take turns in order fair all warps reach their loads together and wait together Greedy then oldest (GTO) keep issuing the same warp until it stalls, then the oldest ready one spreads loads out, good cache locality less fair, a warp can starve Two level RR inside a small active group, switch group when all stall between the two needs tuning of the group size Lesson: Warp schedulers Tips and traps Remember Divergence only happens inside a warp. If whole warps take the same side, as with if ((tid / 32) % 2 == 0), there is no divergence cost at all.Lesson: Warp divergence Remember More independent instructions per warp (unrolling, ILP) cut the warps needed: c from 10 to 20 at W = 400 saves 20 warps.Lesson: How many warps hide the wait? 6.2"],[29,"streams","Streams, asynchrony and overlap","Open chapter Async calls, streams, overlap speedup, pinned memory, events and graphs. Key ideas Async Launches and cudaMemcpyAsync return at once. cudaMemcpy and the synchronise calls block the CPU. Streams In order inside a stream, overlap across streams. Async copies need pinned memory. Pipeline Equal stages: (N + 2)t, speedup 3N/(N + 2) < 3. Unequal: fill + N × slowest stage. Danger Not kernels overwriting each other in one stream, but the CPU reading before the GPU is done. Events cudaEventRecord marks a point. ElapsedTime for timing, StreamWaitEvent for cross stream order. Formulas SerialExam favourite t = N × (tin + tkernel + tout) One stream, nothing overlaps. Lesson: Overlapping copies and compute Overlapped, equal stages tExam favourite t = (N + 2) × t, speedup 3N ÷ (N + 2) N = 4: 2 times. N = 16: 2.67. Never more than 3 (the number of stages). Lesson: Overlapping copies and compute Unequal stages t ≈ fill + N × slowest stage The slowest stage sets the pace. 2, 5, 2 ms with 10 chunks: 2 + 50 + 2 = 54 ms. Lesson: Overlapping copies and compute Tables Table: call · blocks the cpu? · notes Call Blocks the CPU? Notes kernel<<>>() no queued in the default stream cudaMemcpy yes starts after earlier work in the stream, returns when the copy is done cudaMemcpyAsync no needs pinned host memory to really overlap cudaStreamSynchronize(s) yes until stream s is empty cudaDeviceSynchronize() yes until all GPU work is done Lesson: Who waits for whom Tips and traps Common mistake In class it was said that asynchrony is dangerous because a second kernel could overwrite the first. Work in one stream always runs in order. The real danger is the CPU reading or reusing a host buffer before the GPU has finished with it. Synchronise before reading results.Lesson: Who waits for whom"],[29,"step-7","Step 7Synchronisation","7.1"],[29,"races-locks","Race conditions and locks","Open chapter Races, the three lock rules, Dekker, Peterson, Bakery and Filter. Key ideas Three steps counter++ is load, add, store. Other threads can come between them. Counting orders (n × s)! ÷ (s!)ⁿ. Two threads: 20, three threads: 1680. Only the serial orders are correct. Three rules Mutual exclusion, progress, bounded waiting. Breaking them gives two inside, deadlock, starvation. Dekker attempts 1 fails progress (strict turns), 2 fails mutual exclusion (not deadlock), 3 deadlocks. Peterson flag[me] = 1, turn = other, wait while flag[other] and turn == other. Correct, with fences on real hardware. N threads Bakery: tickets plus id, first come first served. Filter: N − 1 gates, starvation free but not bounded. Formulas Orders of n threads with s steps eachExam favourite (n × s)! ÷ (s!)n Pick which thread takes each time slot. Steps inside one thread keep their order. Lesson: Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong Two threads, three steps 6! ÷ (3! × 3!) = 20 Only the 2 fully serial orders give 2. That is 10 percent. Lesson: Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong Three threads, three stepsExam favourite 9! ÷ (3!)3 = 1680 Only 3! = 6 serial orders give 3. That is about 0.4 percent. Lesson: Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong Tables Table: time · thread 1 · thread 2 · counter in memory Time Thread 1 Thread 2 counter in memory 1 load, r1 = 0 0 2 load, r2 = 0 0 3 r1 = 1 0 4 r2 = 1 0 5 store 1 1 6 store 1 1, but it should be 2 Lesson: Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong Table: rule · meaning · if it breaks Rule Meaning If it breaks Mutual exclusion At most one thread is inside at any moment. Two threads change the data together. Progress If the section is free and someone wants in, someone gets in. A thread that does not want in cannot block others. Deadlock, or waiting for a thread that has gone home. Bounded waiting After I ask, others can go first only a limited number of times. Starvation: I wait for ever. Lesson: The critical section and Dekker's three attempts Table: attempt · idea · mutual exclusion · progress Attempt Idea Mutual exclusion Progress What goes wrong 1. Take turns a shared turn variable yes no Strict alternation. If thread 2 stops wanting in, thread 1 enters once and then waits for ever. 2. Check, then set look at the other flag, then raise mine no yes Both check before either sets. Both enter. 3. Set, then check raise my flag, then look yes no Both raise flags, then each waits for the other. Deadlock. Lesson: The critical section and Dekker's three attempts Table: lock · threads · fairness · cost per entry Lock Threads Fairness Cost per entry Peterson 2 waits at most one turn constant Bakery N first come, first served O(N), tickets grow without limit Filter N starvation free, but no fixed bound O(N²) checks in the worst case Lesson: Locks for N threads: Bakery and Filter Tips and traps Common mistake The unsafe result is not guaranteed to be 1. It depends on how the hardware schedules the threads. A race means the answer is unpredictable, which is worse than always wrong.Lesson: Why counter = counter + 1 goes wrong In the exam Slide 8 says attempt 2 can deadlock. It cannot: a thread sets its flag only after passing its loop, so nobody waits after the flags are set. The real flaw is that both can pass the check, which breaks mutual exclusion. The class also concluded that the three attempts suffer from the same drawback. They fail in three different ways: attempt 2 checks too early, attempt 3 claims too early, attempt 1 is safe but too rigid.Lesson: The critical section and Dekker's three attempts In the exam In class the conclusion was that the deadlock danger is still looming. That is not correct. With reads and writes that happen in program order, Peterson meets all three rules. Swapping steps 1 and 2 (turn first, then flag) does break mutual exclusion, so the order matters.Lesson: Peterson's algorithm and why it is correct In the exam Slide 3 says the Filter lock has bounded waiting. It is starvation free"],[29,"races-locks","Race conditions and locks","(every waiting thread gets in in the end), but a thread can be overtaken any number of times at a gate, so there is no fixed bound r. The Bakery lock is the one with first come, first served order.Lesson: Locks for N threads: Bakery and Filter 7.2"],[29,"barriers-atomics","Barriers, atomics and GPU locks","Open chapter Barriers, atomics, CAS and ticket locks, privatisation, priority locks, grid sync. Key ideas Barrier against lock __syncthreads(): all threads of one block meet. Lock: one thread at a time, only those who want it wait. Who shares Registers: nothing. Shared memory: __syncthreads(). Global across blocks: atomics, locks or a new kernel. atomicCAS lock Spin on atomicCAS(&l, 0, 1) until it returns 0, do the work, call __threadfence(), then atomicExch(&l, 0). One thread per block takes it. Privatisation Count in shared memory, then one global atomicAdd per block. 10 × 256: 2560 global atomics become 10. Priority counter Same level runs together, levels get skipped, and non resident blocks can deadlock. grid.sync() needs a cooperative launch. Formulas Compare and swapExam favourite old = *p, if old = cmp then *p = v, return old If old = cmp, you won. Any other value means somebody else changed it first. Lesson: Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock Largest cooperative gridExam favourite blocks ≤ (blocks per SM) × (number of SMs) The occupancy chapter gives blocks per SM. T4: 40 SMs, 4 blocks of 256 per SM, so at most 160 blocks. Lesson: Priority locks and grid wide barriers Tables Table: · __syncthreads() · lock from atomiccas __syncthreads() Lock from atomicCAS Purpose everyone reaches a point one at a time Who waits all threads of the block only threads that want the lock Scope one block the whole device (global memory), or shared memory Typical use tiled matmul, reductions, stencils global counters, shared queues, rare updates Lesson: Barrier or lock Table: who shares the data · where it lives · how to synchronise Who shares the data Where it lives How to synchronise one thread registers, local memory nothing threads of one block shared memory __syncthreads(), or shared atomics threads of different blocks global memory atomics, locks, or end the kernel and start another Lesson: Barrier or lock Table: function · what it does as one step · returns Function What it does as one step Returns atomicAdd(p, v) *p = *p + v the old value atomicSub, atomicMin, atomicMax subtract, keep the smaller, keep the larger the old value atomicExch(p, v) *p = v the old value atomicAnd, atomicOr, atomicXor bit operations the old value atomicCAS(p, cmp, v) if *p == cmp then *p = v the old value Lesson: Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock Table: problem · why · fix Problem Why Fix Not mutual exclusion All threads with the same priority pass together. Add a real lock inside each level. Levels get skipped Three threads at level 2 each add 1, so the counter goes from 2 to 5. Move the counter on once per level, when the last thread of that level is done. Deadlock by residency If the priority 0 blocks are not running yet because spinning blocks fill the GPU, nobody moves the counter. Do not spin on other blocks. Use a grid barrier or separate kernels. Starvation A steady stream of high priority work keeps low priority work waiting. Ageing: raise the priority of work that has waited long. Lesson: Priority locks and grid wide barriers Tips and traps Common mistake Every thread of the block must reach every __syncthreads(). A barrier inside an if that only some threads enter is undefined behaviour: the kernel may hang, or seem to work and give wrong results. The reduction chapter shows the lecture 12 example.Lesson: Barrier or lock In the exam The slide's critical_kernel lets every thread call acquire_lock. Before Volta (2017) the 32 lanes of a warp move in lock step. One lane wins the lock, but the warp keeps running the spin loop for the losers and never lets the winner reach release_lock, so the kernel hangs. Newer GPUs avoid the hang, but the lock then makes every thread of the grid wait in a single line, which is very slow. Take the lock with one thread per block, and add a fence before the release.Lesson: Atomic instructions and the atomicCAS lock Remember compute-sanitizer --tool racecheck finds races on shared memory, such as the"],[29,"barriers-atomics","Barriers, atomics and GPU locks","unsafe_inc example. Run it whenever a result changes from run to run.Lesson: Fewer atomics: privatisation In the exam In the block priority code of slide 7, the counter must be read as volatile or atomically, or the compiler may move the read out of the loop and spin for ever. CUDA does not promise which blocks are running or in what order they start. That this code usually works is luck.Lesson: Priority locks and grid wide barriers"],[29,"step-8","Step 8Tensor cores and fast matmul","8.1"],[29,"number-formats","Low precision numbers and quantisation","Open chapter Float formats, range and detail, mixed precision, INT8 quantisation. Key ideas The formula x = (−1)^s × 1.m × 2^(e − bias), bias = 2^(E−1) − 1, gap after 1.0 = 2^−M. Range against detail Exponent bits give range, mantissa bits give detail. FP16 overflows at 65,504, BF16 keeps FP32 range with about 2 to 3 digits. Which format FP32 master weights, BF16 training, FP16 with loss scaling, TF32 for FP32 code on tensor cores, INT8 or FP8 for inference. Wide accumulator Narrow inputs, FP32 sums. In FP16, 2048 + 1 = 2048. Quantisation q = round(x ÷ s) + z, x̂ = s(q − z). Symmetric INT8: s = R ÷ 127. Error at most s ÷ 2 unless clipped. Formulas Fewer bytes, higher intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes Halve the bytes and the arithmetic intensity doubles. Batch 1 FC layer: AI ≈ 1 in FP16, ≈ 2 with INT8 weights. Lesson: Why use fewer bits? Value of a normal numberExam favourite x = (−1)s × 1.m1m2…mM (binary) × 2e − bias The leading 1 is not stored, so M bits give M + 1 bits of precision. Lesson: How a float is stored, and the formats side by side BiasExam favourite bias = 2E − 1 − 1 5 bits: 15. 8 bits: 127. 11 bits: 1023. Lesson: How a float is stored, and the formats side by side Gap after 1.0 ε = 2−M FP32 1.2 × 10⁻⁷, FP16 9.8 × 10⁻⁴, BF16 7.8 × 10⁻³. Lesson: How a float is stored, and the formats side by side Decimal digits ≈ (M + 1) × log10 2 ≈ 0.3 (M + 1) FP32 about 7, FP16 about 3, BF16 about 2.4. Lesson: How a float is stored, and the formats side by side QuantiseExam favourite q = clip(round(x ÷ s) + z, qmin, qmax) s is the scale, z the zero point. Lesson: Quantisation step by step Bring backExam favourite x̂ = s × (q − z) The rounding error is at most s ÷ 2 unless the value was clipped. Lesson: Quantisation step by step Symmetric INT8 s = R ÷ 127, z = 0 For values between −R and R. Lesson: Quantisation step by step Asymmetric (UINT8) s = (max − min) ÷ 255, z = round(−min ÷ s) For values that are not centred on 0, such as ReLU outputs. Lesson: Quantisation step by step Tables Table: family · examples · time goes into · tensor cores help? Family Examples Time goes into Tensor cores help? Math limited convolution, fully connected, recurrent calculating a lot Memory limited bias add, normalisation, ReLU, dropout moving numbers not at all Lesson: Why use fewer bits? Table: format · e · m · largest Format E M Largest Gap after 1.0 Digits FP64 11 52 1.8 × 10³⁰⁸ 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁶ 16 FP32 8 23 3.4 × 10³⁸ 1.2 × 10⁻⁷ 7.2 TF32 8 10 3.4 × 10³⁸ 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ 3.3 FP16 5 10 65,504 9.8 × 10⁻⁴ 3.3 BF16 8 7 3.4 × 10³⁸ 7.8 × 10⁻³ 2.4 FP8 E4M3 4 3 448 0.125 about 1 FP8 E5M2 5 2 57,344 0.25 under 1 Lesson: How a float is stored, and the formats side by side Table: format · used for · watch out for Format Used for Watch out for FP32 master weights in training, science, anything delicate slow on tensor cores TF32 FP32 code on Ampere and later, about 8 times faster on an A100 not a storage format: memory still holds FP32 FP16 inference, training with loss scaling tiny range: gradients underflow, 70,000 overflows BF16 the default for training large models only about 2 to 3 digits FP8, FP4 Hopper and Blackwell training and inference needs a scale factor per tensor or per block INT8, INT4 inference after quantisation needs a scale factor, accuracy must be checked Lesson: Which format when, and why the sum stays wide Tips and traps Remember Loss scaling for FP16 training: multiply the loss by a large number such as 1024 before back propagation so that small gradients do not round to 0, then divide the gradients by the same number. BF16 has the range of FP32 and does not need it.Lesson: Which format when, and why the sum stays wide In the exam Slide 11's bars (TF32, FP16 and BF16 at 4 times, FP8 at 8, FP4 at 16) are labelled illustrative. On an A100, TF32 is 8 times and FP16 16 times the FP32 CUDA core rate. Always use the data sheet of the GPU in front of you.Lesson: Which format when, and why the sum stays wide 8.2"],[29,"tensor-cores","Tensor cores, GEMM and WMMA","Open chapter FC layer intensity, GEMM, im2col, tensor core peaks, WMMA, waves. Key ideas Math limited test AI = ops ÷ bytes > peak ÷ bandwidth. A100 FP16 ridge ≈ 201. FC layer AI = N_in N_out B ÷ (N_in B + N_in N_out + N_out B). 4096 layer: 4096B ÷ (4096 + 2B), math limited from batch 223. GEMM 2MNK FLOPs. Convolution: M = out channels, N = batch × output pixels, K = in channels × filter size (implicit GEMM). Tensor core peak SMs × TCs × FMAs per clock × 2 × clock. V100 125, A100 312, H100 989 TFLOP/s. WMMA One warp per 16 × 16 tile, fragments, load_matrix_sync, mma_sync (8192 FLOPs), store_matrix_sync. FP16 in, FP32 sum. Waves waves = ⌈blocks ÷ (SMs × blocks per SM)⌉, efficiency = n ÷ (W × waves). 217 blocks on 108 SMs: 67 percent. Formulas Time for maths and for data Tmath = ops ÷ BWmath, Tmem = bytes ÷ BWmem With overlap, the larger one decides the speed. Lesson: When is a layer limited by maths? Math limited whenExam favourite ops ÷ bytes > BWmath ÷ BWmem Left: the arithmetic intensity of the operation. Right: the ops per byte ratio of the GPU, the ridge point of the roofline. Lesson: When is a layer limited by maths? Work ops = 2 Nin Nout B Each output is a dot product of length Nin: one multiply and one add per term. Lesson: When is a layer limited by maths? Bytes in FP16 bytes = 2 (Nin B + Nin Nout + Nout B) Read X, read W, write Y. Lesson: When is a layer limited by maths? 4096 × 4096 layerExam favourite AI = 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B) Batch 1 gives about 1. The limit for a huge batch is 2048. Lesson: When is a layer limited by maths? Peak of the tensor coresExam favourite peak = SMs × tensor cores per SM × FMAs per clock × 2 × clock An FMA counts as 2 FLOPs. Lesson: Tensor cores and where their speed comes from Warp granularity lane efficiency = T ÷ (32 ⌈T ÷ 32⌉) 33 threads need 2 warps: 51.6 percent of lanes used. 65 threads: 67.7 percent. Lesson: Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation WavesExam favourite waves = ⌈blocks ÷ (blocks per SM × SMs)⌉ One wave fills every SM once. Lesson: Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation Wave efficiencyExam favourite n ÷ (W × ⌈n ÷ W⌉), with W = blocks per wave 217 blocks on an A100: 217 ÷ 324 = 67 percent. Lesson: Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation Tables Table: layer · m · n · k Layer M N K Fully connected output features batch input features Convolution (implicit GEMM) output channels batch × output height × output width input channels × filter height × filter width Attention sequence sequence or head size head size or sequence Lesson: Everything is a matrix multiply Table: gpu · sms × tcs · fmas per clock · clock GPU SMs × TCs FMAs per clock Clock Peak V100 FP16 80 × 8 64 1.53 GHz 125 TFLOP/s T4 FP16 40 × 8 64 1.59 GHz 65 TFLOP/s (FP32: 8.1) A100 FP16 / BF16 108 × 4 256 1.41 GHz 312 TFLOP/s A100 TF32 108 × 4 128 1.41 GHz 156 TFLOP/s (FP32 CUDA cores: 19.5) H100 SXM BF16 132 × 4 512 1.83 GHz 989 TFLOP/s Lesson: Tensor cores and where their speed comes from Table: level · what you write Level What you write Libraries cuBLAS (cublasGemmEx), cuDNN, PyTorch with FP16, BF16 or allow_tf32 Templates CUTLASS and CuTe WMMA the API above: portable and simple PTX mma.sync warp level with exact register layouts (Ampere) PTX wgmma Hopper: 4 warps issue asynchronous tensor core work fed by TMA Lesson: Programming tensor cores with WMMA Tips and traps Remember im2col copies each pixel into up to 9 rows, so the matrix is 9 times bigger than the picture. Libraries such as cuDNN never build it in memory. They compute the addresses on the fly, which is why it is called implicit GEMM.Lesson: Everything is a matrix multiply Remember Real kernels rarely see the data sheet peak. Under a heavy matmul an H100 hits its 700 W power limit and lowers its clock, so 750 TFLOP/s may be closer to what is reachable than 750 ÷ 989 = 76 percent suggests.Lesson: Tensor cores and where their speed comes from In the exam Slide 16 says shared memory is used for the matrix C. In real"],[29,"tensor-cores","Tensor cores, GEMM and WMMA","kernels such as CUTLASS, C stays in registers during the main loop and only passes through shared memory when it is written out at the end.Lesson: Programming tensor cores with WMMA Remember Fixes: sizes that are multiples of 32 threads and of the wave size, a tile size that divides the problem evenly, persistent kernels, or stream-K, which splits the leftover work along K across the idle SMs (as CUTLASS does). The same effect exists on AMD GPUs.Lesson: Staircases: warp granularity and wave quantisation 8.3"],[29,"fast-matmul","How fast matmul kernels are built","Open chapter H100 peak, naive kernel, tile intensity, swizzling, wgmma, pipelines, the ladder. Key ideas Speed of light H100 SXM BF16: 132 × 4 tensor cores × 1024 FLOPs × 1.83 GHz ≈ 989 TFLOP/s. Power throttling lowers the real ceiling. Naive kernel Coalesced order 3171 GFLOP/s (43 ms), swapped order 243 GFLOP/s (565 ms). 13 times from one index. Tile intensity AI = (2 ÷ b) B_M B_N ÷ (B_M + B_N). Square is best. FP32 T × T: T ÷ 4. BF16: T ÷ 2. Hopper TMA copies tiles, swizzle slot = chunk XOR row, wgmma on 4 warps, producer and consumer warp groups. Ladder 32 (no tensor cores) to 317 (tensor cores + TMA) to 764 TFLOP/s, about 77 percent of peak. Formulas Intensity of a BM × BN tileExam favourite AI = (2 ÷ b) × BMBN ÷ (BM + BN) b = bytes per number. BK cancels, so only the tile shape matters. Lesson: Tiles: more work per byte Square is best BM + BN ≥ 2√(BMBN) For a fixed area the sum is smallest when BM = BN (AM-GM). Lesson: Tiles: more work per byte Square FP32 tile T × TExam favourite AI = T ÷ 4 32 × 32: 8. 128 × 128: 32. BF16 doubles these: AI = T ÷ 2. Lesson: Tiles: more work per byte Tables Table: part · h100 sxm5 Part H100 SXM5 SMs 132 (the full chip has 144, some are switched off) Each SM 4 quadrants, each with a warp scheduler, FP32 units, a tensor core and load and store units Warps issuing per clock 4 per SM (one per quadrant), out of up to 64 resident Registers 64K 32 bit registers per SM Shared memory and L1 up to 228 KB per SM, the same SRAM split by configuration L2 50 MB, shared by all SMs HBM3 80 GB at about 3.35 TB/s Lesson: The H100 and its speed of light Table: kernel · measured on h100 · time for 2 × 4096³ flops Kernel Measured on H100 Time for 2 × 4096³ FLOPs x feeds the column (coalesced) 3171 GFLOP/s 43 ms x feeds the row (uncoalesced) 243 GFLOP/s 565 ms, 13 times slower Lesson: The naive kernel and a 13 times lesson Table: trick · what it fixes Trick What it fixes Persistent kernel one long lived block per SM loops over tiles, so stores overlap the next loads and no wave is half empty Hilbert curve order of tiles neighbouring SMs use the same strips of A and B, so L2 hits go up Thread block clusters and multicast 2 SMs share loaded tiles through distributed shared memory Two consumer warp groups a 128 × 256 tile without running out of registers Lesson: Hopper tricks: TMA, swizzling, wgmma, pipelines Table: course idea · where it shows up in the ladder Course idea Where it shows up in the ladder Coalescing (3.2) the 13 times gap of the naive kernel Shared memory and banks (3.3) tiles and swizzling Tiled matmul (5.2) block tiles, warp tiles, thread tiles Occupancy and waves (2.4, 8.2) persistent kernels Streams and overlap (6.2) producer and consumer pipelines Barriers (7.2) mbarrier and faster barriers Tensor cores (8.2) the 10 times jump Lesson: The performance ladder Tips and traps Remember Under a heavy matmul the H100 reaches its 700 W power limit and lowers its clock. At 1.5 GHz the peak is 989 × 1.5 ÷ 1.83 ≈ 811 TFLOP/s, so a kernel at 750 TFLOP/s is at about 92.5 percent of what is reachable.Lesson: The H100 and its speed of light In the exam With the numbers used here (756 TFLOP/s BF16 on the PCIe card, 2.0 TB/s) the ridge is about 378 FLOP/byte. The blog quotes about 410 with slightly different specs. Either way a kernel needs hundreds of FLOPs per DRAM byte, so tiles must also be reused through L2 and shared memory.Lesson: Tiles: more work per byte"],[29,"step-9","Step 9Tools and Python on the GPU","9.1"],[29,"debug-profile","Debugging and profiling CUDA code","Open chapter Error checks, compute-sanitizer, cuda-gdb, Nsight, PTX and SASS. Key ideas Order of tools Check every call. Wrong: compute-sanitizer, printf, cuda-gdb. Slow: nsys, then ncu on the hot kernel. Sanitizer tools memcheck (bounds), racecheck (shared memory races), initcheck (uninitialised reads), synccheck (bad barriers). cuda-gdb Build with -g -G. set cuda break_on_launch application, cuda block (1,0,0) thread (31,0,0), p x. Reading a report Time = cycles ÷ clock. SM active ÷ elapsed shows idle SMs. Both units under 60 percent: latency bound, read the stall reasons. PTX and SASS PTX is virtual and portable (%ctaid.x, ld.global), SASS is the real code (S2R, LDG, FADD, STG). See them with nvcc -ptx, cuobjdump -sass. Tables Table: tool · finds · example in this course Tool Finds Example in this course memcheck (default) out of bounds or misaligned accesses, bad frees, leaks badAccess above racecheck shared memory races (a missing __syncthreads) unsafe_inc in chapter 7.2 initcheck reads of global memory that was never written reading cudaMalloc memory before setting it synccheck illegal barriers (a __syncthreads inside a divergent if) the reduction in chapter 5.1 Lesson: compute-sanitizer Table: tool · shows · use it Tool Shows Use it Nsight Systems (nsys) the whole program timeline: CPU, API calls, copies, kernels per stream, NVTX ranges first: which kernel or copy takes the time? Nsight Compute (ncu) one kernel in depth: throughput, occupancy, traffic per memory level, stall reasons, roofline second: why is that kernel slow? compute-sanitizer correctness for wrong results, not speed nvvp, nvprof the old profilers not supported on compute capability 8.0 and newer Lesson: Profiling with Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute Table: metric · value · meaning Metric Value Meaning SM frequency 880 MHz the profiler locks the clock to the base value Elapsed cycles 10,633 10,633 ÷ 880 MHz = 12.08 µs, matching the duration Duration 12.06 µs a tiny kernel: a launch costs about 5 µs Memory throughput 44.35 % the busiest memory unit at 44 % of its peak DRAM throughput 42.92 % of the T4's 320 GB/s SM active cycles 8,132 8132 ÷ 10,633 = 76 %: SMs idle a quarter of the time Compute (SM) throughput 36.98 % the busiest compute pipe at 37 % Lesson: Profiling with Nsight Systems and Nsight Compute Table: sass · meaning SASS Meaning S2R R0, SR_CTAID.X read a special register (blockIdx.x) IMAD integer multiply add, for example the global index or an address LDG.E load from global memory with a 64 bit address FADD, FFMA float add, fused multiply add STG.E store to global memory ISETP, @P0 BRA compare into a predicate, then a branch taken only where the predicate holds Lesson: Reading PTX and SASS Tips and traps Common mistake This program usually runs without any message. The stray writes land in memory the program happens to own, so nothing crashes. That is exactly why out of bounds bugs are dangerous and why the sanitizer comes first.Lesson: A workflow for wrong or slow code In the exam Slide 6 says threads 0 to 21 write out of bounds. Thread i writes data[i + 10], so even thread 0 writes data[10], one past the end. All 32 threads write out of bounds, to indices 10 to 41.Lesson: compute-sanitizer Remember Lecture 7 showed the PTX of the naive matmul: two ld.global loads for every mad. Global loads cost hundreds of cycles, the multiply add only a few. That is the case for tiling and shared memory, read straight from the compiler output.Lesson: Reading PTX and SASS 9.2"],[29,"gpu-python","GPU programming from Python","Open chapter Numba, CuPy, Triton and PyTorch, mapped to CUDA C. Key ideas Routes CuPy for NumPy code, Numba for kernels in Python, Triton for block level kernels, PyTorch for deep learning. All compile just in time. Numba twins @cuda.jit, cuda.grid(1), to_device, device_array_like, add[blocks, threads], copy_to_host, shared.array, syncthreads, atomic.add. Simulator NUMBA_ENABLE_CUDASIM=1 runs kernels on the CPU for testing and debugging. CuPy Each operation is a kernel and a full memory pass. Fuse with ElementwiseKernel or cp.fuse. RawKernel for CUDA C. Triton and PyTorch Triton: program_id, offs, mask, tl.load and tl.store. PyTorch: pin_memory, non_blocking, Stream, synchronize. Tables Table: library · you write · good for Library You write Good for CuPy NumPy style arrays on the GPU, plus element wise and raw CUDA kernels speeding up NumPy code with almost no change Numba kernels in Python with @cuda.jit, cuda.grid, shared arrays, atomics learning CUDA from Python, custom kernels PyCUDA CUDA C source strings, driver level control full control from Python Triton block level kernels, the compiler handles threads and tiling fast custom deep learning kernels (used by PyTorch) PyTorch tensors, torch.cuda streams, pinned memory deep learning, and most real GPU work in Python Lesson: Five ways to reach the GPU from Python Table: cuda c · numba CUDA C Numba __global__ void add(...) @cuda.jit above a Python function blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x cuda.grid(1) cudaMalloc + cudaMemcpy host to device cuda.to_device(a) cudaMalloc only cuda.device_array_like(a) add<<>>(...) add[blocks, threads](...) cudaMemcpy device to host d_c.copy_to_host() __shared__ float s[256] cuda.shared.array(256, dtype=float32) __syncthreads() cuda.syncthreads() atomicAdd(&out[0], v) cuda.atomic.add(out, 0, v) Lesson: Numba: CUDA kernels written in Python Table: cuda idea · pytorch CUDA idea PyTorch cudaMallocHost tensor.pin_memory() cudaMemcpyAsync tensor.to(\"cuda\", non_blocking=True) cudaStream_t torch.cuda.Stream() cudaDeviceSynchronize torch.cuda.synchronize() TF32 tensor cores torch.backends.cuda.matmul.allow_tf32 = True custom kernel torch.compile (generates Triton) or a C++ extension Lesson: PyTorch: streams, pinned memory and tensor cores Tips and traps Remember The simulator runs each GPU thread as a Python thread on the CPU. It is very slow but lets you test and debug kernels, including with print and pdb, on any laptop. The listings above were checked this way.Lesson: Numba: CUDA kernels written in Python Remember Every separate operation reads and writes the whole array. 2 * x + 1 is two passes over memory. cp.fuse or an ElementwiseKernel does it in one pass, which matters because element wise work is memory bound.Lesson: CuPy: NumPy on the GPU"],[29,"step-10","Step 10Exam practice","10.1"],[29,"quiz1-solved","Quiz 1 solved step by step","Open chapter Quiz 1: all ten answers with steps, traps and variants. Key ideas Q1 Use the statement about the SM: 4 FMA × 2 × 1.5 GHz = 12 GFLOPS, option 2. Q2 and Q3 Naive: B² × 2N loads per block (524,288). Tiled: B² × 2 × N/B (32,768). Q4 No __syncthreads: both races possible, answer 3. Fix with one barrier after loading and one after computing. Q5 and Q10 Column store with stride 2048 floats: 32 segments. Warps = blocks × threads per block ÷ 32 = 32,768. Q6 to Q9 __global__ void, y[2] = 13, global memory, tids 1 to 29 step 4 sum to 120. Tables Table: q · topic · answer · chapter Q Topic Answer Chapter 1 peak FP32 throughput of one SM 2 (12 GFLOPS) 1.1, 4.2 2 global loads per block, naive matmul 524,288 5.2 3 global loads per block, tiled matmul 32,768 5.2 4 races in a tiled kernel without barriers 3 (both true) 5.2, 7.2 5 128 byte segments for a transposed store 32 3.2 6 return type of a kernel 5 (__global__ void) 1.4 7 trace a matrix vector kernel 13 2.1 8 memory visible to all SMs for the whole run 1 (global memory) 3.1 9 sum of thread ids in a divergent branch 120 6.1 10 warps launched 32,768 2.3 Lesson: The paper and the answers Table: qualifier · runs on · called from Qualifier Runs on Called from __global__ GPU CPU (a launch), must return void __device__ GPU GPU code only, any return type __host__ CPU CPU (the default) __shared__ not a function: a variable in shared memory Lesson: Q1, Q6 and Q8: peak, kernels and memory Tips and traps In the exam The other reading, 4 FMA per core, gives 64 × 4 × 2 × 1.5 = 768 GFLOPS per SM, which matches no option. When a question gives two numbers for the same thing, use the one that describes exactly what is asked (here the SM) and check the options.Lesson: Q1, Q6 and Q8: peak, kernels and memory In the exam Answer 3: both statements are true for the quiz code, because it has no __syncthreads. The fixed version above needs one barrier after loading and one after computing.Lesson: Q2 to Q4: loads and races in matrix multiply 10.2"],[29,"challenge-problems","Challenge problems","Open chapter Hard multi step problems with full solutions. Key ideas Batch threshold n × n layer: AI = nB ÷ (n + 2B). A100 4096: 223. H100 4096: 345. A100 1024: 330. Occupancy Blocks = min(registers, threads, block limit, shared memory). Registers bind at 40 × 256 on an A100: 75 percent. Banks Stride k gives a gcd(k, 32) way conflict. Pad rows to 33 for column reads. Scan work Hillis and Steele n log n − (n − 1) in log n steps. Blelloch 2(n − 1) in 2 log n steps. Tile budget Double buffered FP32 with BK = 32 in 48 KB: BM + BN ≤ 192, best 96 × 96, AI 24. Formulas Conflict degree for stride kExam favourite degree = gcd(k, 32) Thread t goes to bank (t k) mod 32. Lesson: Problem 3: banks, strides and padding Hillis and Steele adds = n log₂ n − (n − 1), steps = log₂ n Step d updates n − 2d elements. Lesson: Problem 4: work against steps in scan BlellochExam favourite adds = 2 (n − 1), steps = 2 log₂ n Up sweep then down sweep. Lesson: Problem 4: work against steps in scan 10.3"],[29,"exam-revision","Exam revision","Open chapter Formula sheets, slide corrections, traps and mixed problems. Key ideas Three formula groups Speed and limits (Amdahl, peak, roofline), threads and memory (index, warps, occupancy, banks, tiling), patterns (reduction, scan, pipeline, formats). Write the unit Threads or warps, per thread or per block, bytes read and written, FMA = 2 FLOPs, GB = 10⁹ bytes. Slide corrections Thread 343 is (3, 3, 0). Heat centre 0.4. Width³ loads. Sharpen gives 30. Attempt 2 breaks mutual exclusion. C tile in registers. T4 numbers 40 SMs, 8.14 TFLOP/s FP32, 320 GB/s, ridge 25.4, 1024 threads and 16 blocks per SM. Next step rule Memory bound: reuse data (tiles). Copy bound: keep data on the GPU or overlap. Latency bound: more warps or more independent work. Formulas Speedup and efficiencyExam favourite S = Told ÷ Tnew, E = S ÷ N Count copy in, kernel and copy out in Tnew. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Amdahl (fixed problem)Exam favourite S(N) = 1 ÷ ((1 − P) + P ÷ N), ceiling 1 ÷ (1 − P) P is the parallel fraction of the old time. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Gustafson (problem grows) S(N) = (1 − P) + P N Weak scaling: the parallel work grows with N. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Peak FLOP/sExam favourite peak = SMs × cores per SM × 2 × clock The 2 is one FMA per core per cycle. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Little's law in flight = latency × throughput Warps needed = in flight ÷ 32. Bytes in flight = bandwidth × latency. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Arithmetic intensityExam favourite AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes moved Count bytes read and written. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits RooflineExam favourite attainable = min(peak, AI × bandwidth), ridge = peak ÷ bandwidth Left of the ridge: memory bound. Right: compute bound. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Time bound t ≥ max(FLOPs ÷ peak, bytes ÷ bandwidth) The larger term tells you what to fix. Lesson: Formula sheet 1: speed and limits Global index and blocksExam favourite i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x, blocks = ⌈N ÷ B⌉ Guard with i < N. Idle threads = blocks × B − N. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Going backwards block = ⌊g ÷ (BxBy)⌋, x = r mod Bx, y = ⌊r ÷ Bx⌋ r is the leftover g − block × BxBy. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory WarpsExam favourite warps per block = ⌈B ÷ 32⌉, lane = t mod 32 t = x + y Bx + z BxBy, x moves fastest. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory OccupancyExam favourite blocks per SM = min(threads, blocks, registers, shared memory limits) Occupancy = active warps ÷ max warps per SM. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Coalescing sectors per warp load = distinct 32 byte pieces touched Floats: stride 1 gives 4 sectors, stride 8 or more gives 32. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Bank conflictsExam favourite degree = gcd(stride, 32) Same word for all lanes is a broadcast. Pad rows to 33. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Matmul loadsExam favourite naive 2N³, tiled 2N³ ÷ T Per thread 2N against 2N ÷ T. Tile AI in FP32 is T ÷ 4. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Halo tiles loads per output = (B + 2R)d ÷ Bd d is the number of dimensions, R the radius. Lesson: Formula sheet 2: threads and memory Reduction treeExam favourite log₂ B levels, B − 1 additions, 1 + log₂ B barriers One atomic per block joins the blocks. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats ScansExam favourite Hillis and Steele n log₂ n − (n − 1) adds in log₂ n steps, Blelloch 2(n − 1) adds in 2 log₂ n steps Blelloch does less work, Hillis and Steele has fewer steps. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats Divergence efficiency = busy lane ticks ÷ (32 × total ticks) Paths inside one warp run one after the other. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats PipelineExam favourite T = (a + b + c) + (n − 1) × max(a, b, c) Equal stages give speedup 3n ÷ (n + 2). Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns,"],[29,"exam-revision","Exam revision","concurrency and number formats Waves waves = ⌈blocks ÷ (SMs × blocks per SM)⌉ A last wave that is almost empty wastes most of the GPU. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats Float valueExam favourite x = (−1)s × 1.m × 2e − bias, bias = 2E − 1 − 1 FP32 1/8/23, FP16 1/5/10, BF16 1/8/7, TF32 1/8/10. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats Symmetric INT8 s = max|x| ÷ 127, q = round(x ÷ s) Error at most s ÷ 2 unless a value is clipped. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats Interleavings (n s)! ÷ (s!)n n threads with s steps each. Two threads of 3 steps: 20 orders. Lesson: Formula sheet 3: patterns, concurrency and number formats Tables Table: day · what to do · chapters Day What to do Chapters 1 Speed and limits: speedup, Amdahl, peak, roofline. Redo the formula cards below from memory. 1.1 to 1.3, 4.2 2 Threads: indexing forwards and backwards, warps, occupancy. Do the launch problem below by hand. 2.1 to 2.4 3 Memory: hierarchy, coalescing, banks, tiling. Draw one warp and count sectors. 3.1 to 3.3, 5.2 4 Patterns: reduction, stencils, convolution, scan, histogram. 5.1 to 5.5 5 Concurrency: divergence, streams, races, locks, barriers, atomics. 6.1 to 7.2 6 Modern GPUs: number formats, tensor cores, fast matmul, tools. 4.1, 8.1 to 9.2 7 Quiz 1 again under time, then three challenge problems. 10.1, 10.2 Lesson: How to revise in one week Table: where · the slide or class said · what is right · chapter Where The slide or class said What is right Chapter Lecture 3, slide 16 Global thread 343 has threadIdx (2, 3, 0) It is (3, 3, 0). (2, 3, 0) is thread 342. 2.2 Lecture 3, mapping Grids sit inside cores A thread runs on a core lane, a block on one SM, a grid across the GPU. 2.3 Lecture 4, slide 11 Asynchrony is risky because kernels overwrite each other Work in one stream runs in order. The risk is the CPU reading before the GPU is done. 6.2 Lecture 5 notes 1.00101 in binary = 1.5625 1.00101 in binary = 1.15625 = 37 ÷ 32. 4.1 Lecture 6, slide 14 Heat weights 0.25 centre and 0.1 for 6 neighbours They add to 0.85, so heat leaks away. The centre must be 0.4. 5.3 Lecture 7, slide 6 Threads 0 to 21 write out of bounds All 32 threads write out of bounds (indices 10 to 41). 9.1 Lecture 7, slide 8 The plain matmul reads Width² values Each of the Width² threads reads 2 × Width, so 2 × Width³ in total. 5.2 Lecture 8 Sharpening pixel 30 gives 35 5 × 30 − (25 + 35 + 20 + 40) = 30. 5.4 Lecture 9, slide 11 Bars show exact format speeds The bars are illustrative. TF32 is a compute mode, not a storage format. 8.1 Lecture 9, slide 16 The C tile lives in shared memory In real kernels the C tile stays in registers. Only the epilogue uses shared memory. 8.2 Lecture 9 blog numbers Ridge about 410 With the numbers used in class the ridge is about 378. 8.3 Lecture 10, slide 8 Attempt 2 deadlocks Attempt 2 breaks mutual exclusion. Attempt 3 is the one that deadlocks. 7.1 Lecture 10, class Peterson does not solve the problem Peterson is correct when memory is in order, so on a GPU it needs fences. 7.1 Lecture 11 code critical_kernel is safe as written It can hang before Volta and needs __threadfence. The priority counter skips levels. 7.2 Lesson: Every slide correction in one table Table: trap · do this instead Trap Do this instead Counting threads when the question asks for warps Divide by 32 and round up per block, then multiply by blocks. Loads per thread when the question says per block Multiply by threads per block. Quiz 1 question 2: 256 × 2048 = 524,288. Forgetting the write in bytes moved SAXPY reads x and y and writes y: 12 bytes per element. Taking FMA as 1 FLOP One FMA is 2 FLOPs, a multiply and an add. Putting __syncthreads inside a divergent if Every thread of the block must reach every barrier. Treating occupancy as the goal Enough warps to hide latency is enough. Measure, do not maximise. Reading GB as 230 bytes in bandwidth Bandwidth uses 109 bytes per GB."],[29,"exam-revision","Exam revision","Forgetting the reserved 1 KB of shared memory per block On CC 8.x, blocks = ⌊pool ÷ (bytes + 1 KB)⌋. Thinking divergence happens across warps Different warps may take different paths for free. Only lanes inside one warp diverge. Amdahl with N where the question gives a speedup of a part Use 1 ÷ ((1 − p) + p ÷ s) with s the speedup of that part. Lesson: The traps that cost marks Tips and traps In the exam Most lost marks come from counting the wrong thing: threads instead of warps, loads per thread instead of per block, bytes one way instead of both. Write the unit next to every number.Lesson: How to revise in one week In the exam If an exam question copies one of these slides, answer with the right value and add one line saying why. Markers reward the reasoning.Lesson: Every slide correction in one table"],[30,"","Interview Questions","GPU and CUDA interview questions for ML engineers, HPC developers and systems roles: the execution model, thread indexing, warps and divergence, occupancy, the memory hierarchy, coalescing, bank conflicts, the roofline, tiling, reductions, atomics, streams, tensor cores, profiling and debugging scenarios. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · CUDA thread hierarchy · warp · SIMT · occupancy · coalescing · shared memory · bank conflict · __syncthreads · atomicAdd · roofline · streams · pinned memory · tensor cores · Nsight GPU Programming · Interview Questions 🎤"],[30,"","GPU Programming Interview Questions","The questions asked in interviews for CUDA, HPC, ML systems and ML performance roles: the execution model, thread indexing, warps and divergence, occupancy, the memory hierarchy, coalescing and bank conflicts, the roofline, the classic parallel patterns, synchronisation and atomics, streams, tensor cores and mixed precision, and the debugging and profiling scenarios interviewers use to see how you work on a real kernel. Every numerical answer is worked out step by step. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","Answer out loud first, the way you would in the interview. Open the hint only if you are stuck, then compare your answer with the model answer. 0/43known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 1 Execution model Host and device, kernels, grids, blocks, threads, warps, SMs and SIMT. 2 Indexing and launch Global index, rounding up, guards, 2D grids, grid stride loops, waves. 3 Occupancy and latency Registers, shared memory and block limits, Little's law, when occupancy matters. 4 Memory Hierarchy, coalescing, sectors, shared memory banks, pinned and unified memory. 5 Performance models Peak FLOP/s, bandwidth, arithmetic intensity, roofline, Amdahl. 6 Parallel patterns Reduction, tiled matmul, stencils, scan, histogram, privatisation. 7 Synchronisation Races, __syncthreads, atomics, fences, locks, cooperative groups, streams. 8 Modern GPUs and tools Tensor cores, mixed precision, quantisation, Nsight, compute-sanitizer, scenarios. 0 of 43 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review Execution model Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptExecution model Why is a GPU faster than a CPU for some jobs and slower for others? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Latency against throughput. Your answer Model answer A CPU spends its chip on a few fast cores with big caches and branch prediction, so it finishes one task quickly (low latency). A GPU spends its chip on thousands of simple lanes and hides memory waits by switching between many threads, so it finishes many tasks per second (high throughput). Work with lots of independent, similar pieces (pixels, matrix entries) suits the GPU. Serial, branchy or small work suits the CPU, especially once copy time is counted. CPU: latency, big caches GPU: throughput, many lanes Copies can cancel the gain Detailed explanation A good answer names the trade off and gives one example of each side, for example matrix multiply on the GPU and parsing a JSON file on the CPU. How did you do? Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptExecution model Explain thread, warp, block and grid, and where each runs on the hardware. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Software names on one side, hardware on the other. Your answer Model answer A thread runs on one lane of an SM. 32 consecutive threads of a block form a warp, which the SM schedules as one unit. A block is a group of up to 1024 threads that runs on one SM for its whole life and shares that SM's shared memory. A grid is all the blocks of one kernel launch, spread across all SMs. Thread on a lane Warp of 32 scheduled together Block on one SM Grid across the GPU Detailed explanation Blocks never migrate between SMs, which is why __syncthreads and shared memory only work inside a block. A common wrong statement is that grids sit inside cores: it is the other way round. How did you do? Q3 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptExecution model Why must a __global__ function return void, and how do you get results back? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who waits for the kernel? Your answer Model answer A launch is asynchronous: the CPU queues the kernel and continues, so there is no caller waiting for a return value. Results are written into device memory through pointer arguments and copied back with cudaMemcpy, which waits for the kernel to finish. Asynchronous launch Write through pointers cudaMemcpy synchronises Detailed explanation Errors from the launch are read with cudaGetLastError, and errors during the run appear at the next synchronising call. How did you do? Q4 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptExecution model What is SIMT, and how does it differ from SIMD? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who writes the vector code? Your answer Model answer SIMT (single instruction, multiple threads) means you write code for one scalar thread and the hardware runs 32 of them in lock step as a warp. In SIMD the programmer or compiler writes explicit vector instructions over fixed width registers. With SIMT each thread has its own registers and can branch, and the hardware masks off lanes on paths they do not take. Scalar code, warp execution Per"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","thread registers Branch masking Detailed explanation Since Volta each thread also has its own program counter (independent thread scheduling), so threads of a warp can make progress separately, which is why code must use __syncwarp or the _sync shuffle variants. How did you do? Indexing and launch Q5 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptIndexing and launch You add two arrays of 1,000,000 floats with 256 threads per block. How many blocks, how many idle threads, and what guard do you need? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Round up. Your answer Model answer Blocks = ⌈1,000,000 ÷ 256⌉ = 3907. Threads launched = 3907 × 256 = 1,000,192, so 192 are idle. Each thread computes i = blockIdx.x × blockDim.x + threadIdx.x and does the work only if i < n. 3907 blocks 192 idle threads if (i < n) Detailed explanation (n + 255) / 256 is the integer way to round up. Without the guard the last 192 threads write past the array, which compute-sanitizer reports as an invalid global write. How did you do? Q6 🌱 Easy🖨️ OutputIndexing and launch What does this program print? block, threads, thread = 3, 64, 5 n, B = 1_000_000, 256print(block * threads + thread, (n + B - 1) // B) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Global index = block × threads per block + thread. Your answer Model answer It prints 197 3907. Thread 5 of block 3 with 64 threads per block has index 3 × 64 + 5 = 197. The number of blocks for one million elements in blocks of 256 is (1,000,000 + 255) // 256 = 3907. Detailed explanation The same rounding trick is used in every launch configuration. 1973907 How did you do? Q7 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptIndexing and launch A grid of 5 × 4 blocks has blocks of 5 × 5 threads. Which blockIdx and threadIdx does global thread 343 have? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Divide by threads per block first. Your answer Model answer Threads per block = 25. Block number = ⌊343 ÷ 25⌋ = 13, leftover 18. Block 13 in a grid 5 wide is x = 13 mod 5 = 3, y = ⌊13 ÷ 5⌋ = 2. Thread 18 in a block 5 wide is x = 3, y = 3. So blockIdx = (3, 2, 0) and threadIdx = (3, 3, 0). Divide then mod x varies fastest (3, 2, 0) and (3, 3, 0) Detailed explanation A well known lecture slide gives threadIdx (2, 3, 0), which is thread 342. Interviewers like this because it tests that you know x varies fastest. How did you do? Q8 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingIndexing and launch Write a grid stride loop for SAXPY and explain why it is useful. __global__ void saxpy(int n, float a, constfloat* x, float* y) { for (int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x; i < n; i += blockDim.x * gridDim.x) y[i] = a * x[i] + y[i]; } ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Start at your index, jump by the whole grid. Your answer Model answer Each thread starts at its global index and jumps by blockDim.x × gridDim.x until it passes n. A fixed grid, for example a few blocks per SM, then covers any n, the launch cost does not grow with n, and the same kernel works for small and huge inputs. Start at global index Stride = blockDim.x × gridDim.x Fixed grid covers any n Detailed explanation It also makes it easy to tune the grid size separately from the problem size, and it keeps per thread setup (like loading constants) out of the inner loop. How did you do? Q9 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptIndexing and launch On a T4 (40 SMs, 1024 threads and 16 blocks per SM) you launch 3907 blocks of 256 threads. How many waves run, and how full is the last one? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Blocks per SM first. Your answer Model answer Blocks per SM = min(1024 ÷ 256, 16) = 4. One wave = 40 × 4 = 160 blocks. Waves = ⌈3907 ÷ 160⌉ = 25. The first 24 waves hold 3840 blocks, so the last wave has 67 blocks, 42 percent full. 4 blocks per SM 160 blocks per wave 25 waves, last 42 percent full Detailed explanation A tail wave that is almost empty wastes most of the GPU for its duration. For large kernels the tail matters little, for short ones you can change the block size or use a persistent kernel. How did you do? Occupancy and latency Q10 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptOccupancy and"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","latency A kernel on an A100 uses 256 threads per block, 40 registers per thread and 20 KB of shared memory per block. What is the occupancy and what limits it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Work out each limit and take the smallest. Your answer Model answer Registers: 40 × 256 = 10,240 per block, 65,536 ÷ 10,240 = 6 blocks. Threads: 2048 ÷ 256 = 8. Blocks: 32. Shared memory: 20 KB + 1 KB reserved = 21 KB, 164 ÷ 21 = 7. The smallest is 6, so 48 of 64 warps are active: 75 percent, limited by registers. Registers bind at 6 blocks 75 percent __launch_bounds__ to cap registers Detailed explanation To reach 100 percent you need at most 32 registers per thread, for example with __launch_bounds__(256, 8). Check for spills in the -Xptxas -v output before and after. How did you do? Q11 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptOccupancy and latency Is higher occupancy always faster? Give a case where lower occupancy wins. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does occupancy buy you? Your answer Model answer No. Occupancy only helps hide latency. Once there are enough warps (or enough independent instructions per warp) to keep the pipelines busy, more warps add nothing. A kernel that keeps a large register tile per thread, as fast matrix multiply does, runs at low occupancy but reuses data from registers so well that it is much faster than a high occupancy version. Occupancy hides latency ILP is an alternative Register tiles win at low occupancy Detailed explanation Volkov's talk \"Better performance at lower occupancy\" is the classic reference. Measure achieved occupancy and stall reasons in Nsight Compute instead of chasing the number. How did you do? Q12 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptOccupancy and latency Memory latency is 400 cycles and each warp issues one independent load every 4 cycles. How many warps per scheduler hide the latency? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Little's law. Your answer Model answer In flight needed = latency × throughput. A warp covers 4 cycles of issue before it has to wait, so warps ≈ 400 ÷ 4 = 100 on paper, more than any SM allows. The fix is more independent loads per warp: with 4 independent loads in flight per warp you need about 25 warps. Latency × throughput More ILP, fewer warps Detailed explanation This is why real kernels unroll loads and use vector loads (float4): instruction level parallelism multiplies the bytes in flight without needing more warps. How did you do? Memory Q13 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptMemory List the CUDA memory spaces with their scope and speed. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first From registers out to global. Your answer Model answer Registers: one thread, fastest. Local memory: one thread, but it lives in device DRAM, used for spills. Shared memory: one block, on chip, about as fast as L1. Global memory: all threads and the host, large and slow, lives until cudaFree. Constant memory: read only, 64 KB, cached and broadcast when a warp reads one address. L1 and L2 are caches, not separate spaces. Registers and local per thread Shared per block Global for all Constant read only Detailed explanation \"Local\" is a famous trap: it is private to the thread but as slow as global memory. How did you do? Q14 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMemory What is coalescing, and how many 32 byte sectors does a warp touch when each thread reads a float with stride 1, 2 and 32? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first 32 threads × 4 bytes. Your answer Model answer The hardware merges the loads of a warp into as few 32 byte sectors as possible. Stride 1: 128 bytes in 4 sectors, 100 percent useful. Stride 2: spread over 256 bytes, 8 sectors, 50 percent useful. Stride 32: every thread in its own sector, 32 sectors, 12.5 percent useful. 32 byte sectors 4, 8 and 32 sectors threadIdx.x along contiguous memory Detailed explanation Make threadIdx.x walk along the fastest changing index of the array. For transposes, read coalesced into shared memory and write coalesced from it. How did you do? Q15 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMemory Shared memory has 32 banks of 4 bytes."],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","Thread t reads s[t × k]. What is the conflict degree for k = 1, 2, 8 and 32, and how does padding fix a column read? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first gcd. Your answer Model answer Degree = gcd(k, 32): 1 (none), 2, 8 and 32. For a 32 × 32 float tile a column read has stride 32, a 32 way conflict. Declaring the tile as [32][33] makes the stride 33, gcd(33, 32) = 1, so every lane hits a different bank. gcd(k, 32) Broadcast is free Pad to 33 Detailed explanation All lanes reading the same word is a broadcast and costs nothing extra. Padding costs 32 × 4 = 128 bytes for the whole tile. How did you do? Q16 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptMemory What is pinned memory, and when do you need it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does the copy engine need? Your answer Model answer Pinned (page locked) host memory cannot be moved by the operating system, so the GPU can copy from it directly with DMA. Normal pageable memory is first copied by the driver into a pinned staging buffer. You need pinned memory for cudaMemcpyAsync to overlap with kernels, and it usually gives higher copy bandwidth. Page locked, DMA ready Needed for async overlap Faster copies Detailed explanation Pinning too much memory starves the operating system, so pin the buffers you stream through, not everything. How did you do? Q17 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputMemory What does this program print? from collections importCounterdef degree(k): return max(Counter((t * k) % 32for t inrange(32)).values()) print(*[degree(k) for k in (1, 2, 8, 32, 33)]) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Bank = index mod 32, count the busiest bank. Your answer Model answer It prints 1 2 8 32 1. These are the conflict degrees for strides 1, 2, 8, 32 and 33: the largest number of different addresses that fall in one bank. Detailed explanation The program checks the gcd rule by counting, which is a good habit in an interview whiteboard too. 128321 How did you do? Q18 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptMemory Unified memory makes code simpler. What performance traps does it bring, and how do you avoid them? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Page faults. Your answer Model answer With cudaMallocManaged pages move on demand. The first GPU touch of a page causes a fault and a migration, and data that ping pongs between CPU and GPU moves again and again. Use cudaMemPrefetchAsync to move data before the kernel, cudaMemAdvise to set the preferred location, and avoid touching the same pages from both sides in a loop. On demand page migration Prefetch and advise Avoid ping pong Detailed explanation Nsight Systems shows the page fault and migration events on the timeline, which is how you find the problem. How did you do? Performance models Q19 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptPerformance models How do you compute the peak FP32 throughput of a GPU? Do it for an A100 (108 SMs, 64 FP32 cores per SM, 1.41 GHz). ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One FMA is 2 FLOPs. Your answer Model answer Peak = SMs × cores per SM × 2 × clock = 108 × 64 × 2 × 1.41 GHz = 19.49 TFLOP/s, the quoted 19.5 TFLOP/s. SMs × cores × 2 × clock A100 about 19.5 TFLOP/s Detailed explanation Tensor core peaks are computed the same way with FLOPs per tensor core per clock, which gives 312 TFLOP/s in FP16 on the A100. How did you do? Q20 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPerformance models Explain the roofline model and place SAXPY and a 4096 × 4096 FP32 matrix multiply on an A100 (19.5 TFLOP/s, 1555 GB/s). ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first AI = FLOPs ÷ bytes. Your answer Model answer Attainable = min(peak, AI × bandwidth). Ridge = 19,500 ÷ 1555 = 12.5 FLOP/byte. SAXPY does 2 FLOPs per 12 bytes, AI = 0.17, so it is memory bound at about 0.17 × 1555 = 259 GFLOP/s. Matrix multiply does 2 × 4096³ FLOPs on 3 × 4 × 4096² bytes if each matrix moves once, AI = 683, far right of the ridge, so it can be compute bound if the kernel reuses data well. min(peak, AI × BW) Ridge 12.5 SAXPY memory bound GEMM compute bound Detailed explanation The model tells you what to fix: memory bound kernels need less traffic (fusion,"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","tiling, smaller types), compute bound ones need better instructions (tensor cores). How did you do? Q21 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputPerformance models What does this program print? peak, bw = 19500, 1555 ai = 2 / 12 ridge = peak / bw print(round(ridge, 2), \"memory\"if ai < ridge else\"compute\", round(min(peak, ai * bw), 1)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Compare AI with the ridge. Your answer Model answer It prints 12.54 memory 259.2. The ridge is 19,500 ÷ 1555 = 12.54. SAXPY has AI = 2 ÷ 12 = 0.167, below the ridge, so it is memory bound at 0.167 × 1555 = 259.2 GFLOP/s. Detailed explanation Rounding to one decimal hides the repeating 6. 12.54 memory 259.2 How did you do? Q22 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptPerformance models Copies take 20 percent of a job and the kernel 80 percent. You make the kernel 50 times faster. What is the overall speedup, and what next? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Amdahl for one part. Your answer Model answer Speedup = 1 ÷ ((1 − 0.8) + 0.8 ÷ 50) = 1 ÷ 0.216 = 4.63. The copies are now 93 percent of the time, so the next step is to keep data on the GPU across kernels, or to overlap copies with compute using streams and pinned memory. 4.63 times Copies now dominate Keep data resident or overlap Detailed explanation Gustafson's view is the other side: if the problem grows with the machine, the parallel part grows and the serial share shrinks. How did you do? Q23 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptPerformance models For a 4096 × 4096 fully connected layer in FP16 on an A100 (312 TFLOP/s, 1.555 TB/s), from which batch size is it math limited? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first AI = nB ÷ (n + 2B) in FLOP per byte. Your answer Model answer FLOPs = 2 × 4096² × B, bytes = 2 × (4096² + 2 × 4096 × B), so AI = 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B). The ridge is 312 ÷ 1.555 = 200.6. Solving 4096 B ÷ (4096 + 2B) > 200.6 gives B > 222.4, so from batch 223. AI = nB ÷ (n + 2B) Ridge 200.6 Batch 223 Detailed explanation NVIDIA's performance guide uses the same model. The smaller the layer, the larger the batch needs to be, because there are fewer weights to reuse. How did you do? Parallel patterns Q24 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingParallel patterns Write a shared memory block reduction and explain why sequential addressing beats interleaved addressing. __global__ void blockSum(constfloat* x, float* out, int n) { extern __shared__ float s[]; int t = threadIdx.x, i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + t; s[t] = i < n ? x[i] : 0.0f; __syncthreads(); for (int stride = blockDim.x / 2; stride > 0; stride /= 2) { if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride]; __syncthreads(); } if (t == 0) atomicAdd(out, s[0]); } ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Which threads are active in each step? Your answer Model answer Each block loads its values into shared memory, then for stride = blockDim.x / 2 down to 1 the first stride threads add s[t + stride] into s[t], with a barrier after each step. Thread 0 then holds the block sum. With sequential addressing the active threads are packed into the first warps, so whole warps idle instead of diverging, and the accesses hit different banks. Interleaved addressing (t % (2 × stride) == 0) spreads active threads over every warp. Barrier after every level Active threads packed One atomic per block Detailed explanation For 512 threads: 9 levels, 511 additions, 10 barriers including the one after loading. The last 5 levels can use warp shuffles instead of shared memory. How did you do? Q25 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptParallel patterns How many global loads does a naive N × N matrix multiply do, and how much does tiling with T × T tiles save? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Loads per thread × threads. Your answer Model answer Naive: each of the N² threads reads a row of A and a column of B, 2N loads, so 2N³ in total. Tiled: each value loaded into shared memory is used by T threads, so 2N³ ÷ T. With T = 16 that is 16 times less traffic, and the arithmetic intensity in FP32 rises to about T ÷ 4 FLOP per byte. 2N³ naive 2N³ ÷ T tiled Two barriers per phase Detailed explanation Each"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","phase needs two barriers: one after loading the tiles, so nobody reads a slot before it is written, and one after the inner loop, so nobody overwrites a tile still in use. How did you do? Q26 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptParallel patterns A 2D 5 point stencil reads its neighbours from global memory. How many reads per output does a 16 × 16 tile with a 1 cell halo need? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first (B + 2R)² ÷ B². Your answer Model answer Naive: 5 reads per output. Tiled: the block loads (16 + 2)² = 324 values for 256 outputs, 1.27 reads per output, about 4 times less traffic. The corners of the halo are not needed for a 5 point stencil, so a careful load uses 16² + 4 × 16 = 320 values. 5 against 1.27 reads Halo of radius R Still memory bound Detailed explanation Stencils stay memory bound even with tiles because they do few FLOPs per value. 3D tiles help less because the halo share is larger. How did you do? Q27 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptParallel patterns How do warp shuffles speed up a reduction? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Registers of other lanes. Your answer Model answer __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, v, offset) reads v from the lane offset places higher, straight from its register. With offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 lane 0 holds the warp sum after 5 steps, with no shared memory traffic and no __syncthreads. A block reduction then only needs shared memory for the one value per warp. Register to register 5 steps for 32 lanes No barrier inside a warp Detailed explanation The mask names the lanes that take part. Since Volta it must be correct, because lanes are not guaranteed to run in lock step. How did you do? Q28 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptParallel patterns Compare the Hillis and Steele and the Blelloch scans for n = 1024. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Work against steps. Your answer Model answer Hillis and Steele does n log₂ n − (n − 1) = 10,240 − 1023 = 9217 additions in 10 steps. Blelloch does an up sweep and a down sweep, 2(n − 1) = 2046 additions in 20 steps. When n is far larger than the number of lanes, total work decides the time, so Blelloch wins. For a single warp, Hillis and Steele with shuffles is often faster because it has fewer steps. 9217 against 2046 additions 10 against 20 steps Work efficient wins at scale Detailed explanation Large scans run as three kernels: scan each block, scan the block sums, add the scanned sums back. How did you do? Q29 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptParallel patterns A histogram of 256 bins over 50 million bytes is slow. What is happening and how do you fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Contention. Your answer Model answer Every thread does atomicAdd on one of only 256 global counters, so many atomics target the same addresses and are serialised. Privatise: each block counts into its own 256 bins in shared memory, where atomics are cheap and only one block competes, then adds its bins to the global histogram, at most 256 global atomics per block. Atomic contention Shared memory private copy Merge once per block Detailed explanation Skewed data, for example a dark image where most bytes are 0, makes contention much worse. Per warp sub histograms reduce it further. How did you do? Synchronisation Q30 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptSynchronisation Why does counter++ from many threads give a wrong answer, and what are two fixes? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first How many steps is ++? Your answer Model answer counter++ is a load, an add and a store. Two threads can load the same old value and both store old + 1, so an update is lost. Fixes: atomicAdd(&counter, 1), which does the three steps as one, or let each thread or block count privately and combine the results once with a reduction. Load, add, store atomicAdd Private counts and reduce Detailed explanation For two threads that each do three steps there are 6! ÷ (3! × 3!) = 20 possible interleavings, and only 2 of them give the right answer. How did you do? Q31 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputSynchronisation What does this program print? from math import factorial as f print(f(6)"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","// f(3) ** 2, f(9) // f(3) ** 3) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Choose the positions of one thread's steps. Your answer Model answer It prints 20 1680. Two threads with 3 steps each have 6! ÷ (3!)² = 20 interleavings. Three threads with 3 steps have 9! ÷ (3!)³ = 1680. Detailed explanation The numbers grow so fast that testing cannot cover all orders, which is why races must be ruled out by design. 201680 How did you do? Q32 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptSynchronisation What goes wrong when __syncthreads() is inside an if that only some threads of the block take? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Who reaches the barrier? Your answer Model answer Every thread of the block must reach the same __syncthreads(). If some take the branch and others do not, the behaviour is undefined: the block can hang, or the barrier can match a different __syncthreads() and let threads read data that is not ready. Keep barriers outside divergent branches. Put only the work under the condition, as in if (t < stride) s[t] += s[t + stride], and call __syncthreads() after the if, where every thread reaches it. All threads must arrive Undefined behaviour, hangs synccheck Detailed explanation compute-sanitizer --tool synccheck finds this bug. How did you do? Q33 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptSynchronisation Copy in, kernel and copy out each take 4 ms per chunk. How long do 8 chunks take serially and with 3 streams overlapping? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Fill, steady state, drain. Your answer Model answer Serial: 8 × 12 = 96 ms. Overlapped: (4 + 4 + 4) + 7 × 4 = 40 ms, a speedup of 2.4. With equal stages the speedup is 3n ÷ (n + 2), which tends to 3. The copies need pinned host memory and cudaMemcpyAsync on the non default streams. 96 against 40 ms 3n ÷ (n + 2) Pinned and async Detailed explanation With unequal stages the slowest one sets the pace: total = sum of stages + (n − 1) × slowest stage. How did you do? Q34 🔥 Hard⌨️ CodingSynchronisation Implement a simple spin lock for one thread per block, and say why the fence matters. __device__ void lock(int* m) { while (atomicCAS(m, 0, 1) != 0) { } } __device__ void unlock(int* m) { __threadfence(); atomicExch(m, 0); } ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first atomicCAS and atomicExch. Your answer Model answer Spin while atomicCAS(&lock, 0, 1) returns 1, do the critical work, call __threadfence() so the writes are visible to other blocks before the lock is released, then atomicExch(&lock, 0). Only one thread per block may try, because threads of one warp spinning on a lock held by a lane of the same warp could deadlock on older GPUs. atomicCAS to take __threadfence before release One thread per block Detailed explanation Locks across blocks are slow and fragile. Prefer atomics on the data itself, privatisation, or a second kernel launch. How did you do? Q35 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptSynchronisation How do you synchronise all blocks of a grid? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Normally you do not. Your answer Model answer The safe default is to end the kernel and launch the next one, since kernel boundaries in one stream are a grid wide barrier. Inside one kernel, cooperative groups give grid.sync(), but only with a cooperative launch (cudaLaunchCooperativeKernel) and only if all blocks fit on the GPU at the same time. A home made barrier with atomics can deadlock when some blocks are not resident. Kernel boundary grid.sync with cooperative launch All blocks must be resident Detailed explanation Use cudaOccupancyMaxActiveBlocksPerMultiprocessor to size a cooperative grid. How did you do? Modern GPUs and tools Q36 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools Your kernel gives wrong results only sometimes. Which tools do you use and in what order? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Correctness before speed. Your answer Model answer First check every CUDA call and cudaGetLastError after the launch. Then run compute-sanitizer: memcheck for out of bounds accesses, racecheck for shared memory races, synccheck for bad barriers and initcheck for"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","reads of uninitialised memory. If needed, step through with cuda-gdb (build with -g -G). Only then profile. Check every call compute-sanitizer tools cuda-gdb Detailed explanation \"Only sometimes\" points to a race or an uninitialised read. CUDA_LAUNCH_BLOCKING=1 makes errors show up at the launch that caused them. How did you do? Q37 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools What does a tensor core do, and why does mixed precision use an FP32 accumulator? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Small matrix multiply add. Your answer Model answer A tensor core computes a small matrix product D = A × B + C per instruction, for example on 16 × 16 tiles per warp through WMMA, with FP16, BF16, TF32, INT8 or FP8 inputs. Sums are kept in FP32 because narrow formats lose small additions: in FP16 the gap at 2048 is 2, so 2048 + 1 rounds back to 2048. Matrix multiply add per instruction Narrow inputs FP32 accumulator Detailed explanation Summing 4096 ones in FP16 stops at 2048. With an FP32 accumulator you get 4096. How did you do? Q38 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools FP16 or BF16 for training, and why? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Exponent bits against mantissa bits. Your answer Model answer BF16 has 8 exponent bits, the same range as FP32, and 7 mantissa bits. FP16 has 5 exponent bits (max 65,504) and 10 mantissa bits. Gradients often go out of FP16 range, so FP16 training needs loss scaling. BF16 almost never overflows, so it trains without loss scaling at the cost of less detail. BF16: FP32 range FP16: needs loss scaling TF32 is a compute mode Detailed explanation TF32 is a tensor core compute mode with 8 exponent and 10 mantissa bits for FP32 code, not a storage format. How did you do? Q39 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputModern GPUs and tools What does this program print? import numpy as np s16, s32 = np.float16(0), np.float32(0) for _ inrange(4096): s16 = np.float16(s16 + np.float16(1)) s32 = np.float32(s32 + np.float32(1)) print(float(s16), float(s32)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first FP16 has 10 mantissa bits. Your answer Model answer It prints 2048.0 4096.0. Adding ones in FP16 stalls at 2048, where the gap between numbers is 2 and 2048 + 1 rounds to even, back to 2048. In FP32 the sum is exact. Detailed explanation This is the reason for FP32 master weights and FP32 accumulators in mixed precision training. 2048.04096.0 How did you do? Q40 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools How does symmetric INT8 quantisation work? Quantise 1.0 when the largest value is 6.35. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Scale = max ÷ 127. Your answer Model answer Scale s = 6.35 ÷ 127 = 0.05. q = round(1.0 ÷ 0.05) = 20, and it reads back as 20 × 0.05 = 1.0. The error is at most s ÷ 2 for values in range. Outliers stretch the scale and waste levels, which is why per channel scales or clipping are used. s = max ÷ 127 Error ≤ s ÷ 2 Per channel scales Detailed explanation Asymmetric quantisation adds a zero point z: q = round(x ÷ s) + z. How did you do? Q41 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools Nsight Compute shows a kernel at 20 percent of peak FLOPs and 25 percent of peak memory bandwidth. What does it mean and what do you do next? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Neither unit is busy. Your answer Model answer The kernel is latency bound: it is neither compute nor memory bound, it waits. Look at the warp stall reasons and achieved occupancy. Typical fixes are more warps (fewer registers or less shared memory per block), more independent work per thread (unrolling, vector loads), removing long dependency chains, or fixing uncoalesced accesses and bank conflicts that waste bandwidth. Latency bound Stall reasons More parallelism or ILP Detailed explanation Use Nsight Systems first to find which kernel matters on the timeline, then Nsight Compute on that kernel. How did you do? Q42 🧠 Expert📘 ConceptModern GPUs and tools A teammate's matrix transpose runs at 15 percent of the copy bandwidth. Walk through how you would fix it. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer"],[30,"interview","GPU Programming Interview Questions","first Reads and writes cannot both be coalesced naively. Your answer Model answer A naive transpose reads rows (coalesced) but writes columns (each lane in its own sector), so writes waste most of the bandwidth. Load a 32 × 32 tile with coalesced reads into shared memory, __syncthreads(), then write it out transposed with coalesced writes. Pad the tile to [32][33] so the column reads from shared memory have no bank conflicts. Have each thread move several elements to give more bytes in flight. A good tiled transpose reaches close to copy bandwidth. Uncoalesced writes Shared memory tile Pad to 33 Measure each step Detailed explanation Measure each step: naive, tiled, tiled plus padding. Showing the numbers is what makes this answer strong. How did you do? Q43 🧠 Expert🏗️ DesignModern GPUs and tools Design a GPU pipeline that processes a 4K video stream at 60 frames per second with a 3 × 3 filter and a small CNN. What do you check first? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Budget per frame. Your answer Model answer The budget is 16.7 ms per frame. Keep frames on the GPU: decode on the GPU if possible, otherwise copy each frame once with pinned buffers on two or three streams so the copy of frame k + 1 overlaps compute on frame k. Fuse the filter with the first CNN layer or the preprocessing to avoid an extra memory pass. Run the CNN in FP16 or INT8 on tensor cores with batching only if latency allows. Check first the copy time (a 3840 × 2160 RGB frame is 24.9 MB, about 2 ms at 12 GB/s), then each kernel against its roofline, using Nsight Systems for the timeline. 16.7 ms budget Pinned buffers and streams Fusion Measure with Nsight Detailed explanation The interviewer wants the budget, the copy, the overlap, fusion and the measurement plan. How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
